var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Metadata-overview","page":"Overview","text":"NMRData objects contain metadata on processing and acquisition parameters, populated automatically when loading a spectrum.","title":"Metadata overview"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Types-of-metadata","page":"Overview","text":"Metadata is organised into two categories:\n\nSpectrum metadata: information about the experiment as a whole: number of scans, receiver gain, pulse programme, title, temperature, the noise level (calculated when the spectrum is loaded), acquisition parameters from the acqus file, and the contents of auxiliary files such as vclists and vdlists.\nAxis metadata: information specific to each dimension: number of points, the original time-domain size (before zero filling, linear prediction or extraction of a subregion), carrier frequency, spectral width, window function and phase correction.\n\nMetadata are stored in dictionaries keyed by symbols such as :ns or :pulseprogram. The standard keys are listed in the Metadata reference page.","title":"Types of metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Quick-start","page":"Overview","text":"","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Accessing-spectrum-metadata","page":"Overview","text":"using NMRTools\n\n# Load example spectrum\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\n\n# The whole dictionary\nmetadata(spec)\n\n# A single entry, using the metadata function\nmetadata(spec, :ns)\n\n# Or use dictionary-style lookup\nspec[:pulseprogram]","title":"Accessing spectrum metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Accessing-axis-metadata","page":"Overview","text":"# By dimension type\nspec[F2Dim, :label]\n\n# Or by dimension number\nspec[2, :swhz]\n\n# All metadata for one axis\nmetadata(spec, F1Dim)","title":"Accessing axis metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Accessing-acquisition-parameters","page":"Overview","text":"# Get acquisition parameter\nacqus(spec, :te)\n\n# Access arrayed parameters\nacqus(spec, :p, 1)  # First pulse length\n\nParameter names can be given as strings (case insensitive, e.g. \"TE\") or as lowercase symbols (e.g. :te).\n\nnote: Note\nArrayed parameters such as pulse lengths and delays are returned as dictionaries rather than lists, to avoid confusion between Bruker arrays, which are zero-based, and Julia arrays, which are one-based.","title":"Accessing acquisition parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Spectrometer-channels","page":"Overview","text":"Frequency dimensions carry their own :bf, :sf and :nucleus metadata, but these only describe nuclei that were detected, i.e. that have an axis. To reach a nucleus that was manipulated but never detected, for example the carbon channel in a ¹³C-edited experiment read out on ¹H, use the spectrometer channel model.\n\nChannels describe the physical RF channels (:f1…:f8) independently of the detected dimensions. channels(spec) returns a dictionary of all channels, and channels(spec, ...) returns a single channel by label, by Nucleus, or by a string giving either:\n\nchannels(spec, :f1)        # by spectrometer channel label\n\nchannels(spec, \"13C\")[:bf] # carbon base frequency, even with no carbon axis\n\nEach channel dictionary has keys :label, :nucleus, :bf (base frequency, Hz), :sfo (carrier frequency, Hz), :offsethz and :offsetppm.","title":"Spectrometer channels"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Special-metadata-types","page":"Overview","text":"NMRTools provides specialized types for handling complex metadata:","title":"Special metadata types"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Window-functions","page":"Overview","text":"Window functions (apodization) applied during processing are stored as structured objects that preserve all parameters:\n\n# Get window function for a dimension\nwin = spec[F2Dim, :window]\n\nSee the Window functions page for detailed documentation.","title":"Window functions"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Power-levels","page":"Overview","text":"Power levels are represented as Power objects that handle conversion between Watts and dB:\n\n# Power levels from acqus file\np = acqus(spec, :pl, 1)\ndb(p)      # Get as dB\nwatts(p)   # Get as Watts\n\nSee the Power levels page for detailed documentation.","title":"Power levels"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Frequency-lists","page":"Overview","text":"Frequency lists (from fq1list, etc.) are stored as FQList objects that preserve unit and reference information:\n\n# Get frequency list\nfqlist = acqus(spec, :fq1list)\n\n# Extract as ppm or Hz\nppm(fqlist, dims(spec, F2Dim))\nhz(fqlist, dims(spec, F2Dim))\n\nSee the Frequency lists page for detailed documentation.","title":"Frequency lists"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Sample-metadata","page":"Overview","text":"Sample metadata describing the physical sample, buffer composition, and experimental details can be automatically loaded when present:\n\n# Check if sample metadata is available\nhassample(spec)\n\n# Access sample metadata\nsample(spec)\n\n# Navigate nested metadata\nsample(spec, :sample, :label)\n\nSee the Sample metadata page for detailed documentation.","title":"Sample metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Auxiliary-files","page":"Overview","text":"Files such as vclist, vdlist, and frequency lists are automatically imported when present:\n\n# Access variable delay list\nrelaxation_spec = exampledata(\"pseudo3D_HN_R2\")\nacqus(relaxation_spec, :vclist)\n\nNMRTools performs automatic unit conversion:\n\n:vclist: Variable loop counter (dimensionless)\n:vdlist: Variable delays, in seconds\n:valist: Variable amplitude, in dB (converted from Watts if necessary)\n:vplist: Variable pulse lengths, in seconds\n:fq1list through :fq8list: Frequency lists (see Frequency lists)","title":"Auxiliary files"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/#Next-steps","page":"Overview","text":"Times and frequencies: Units used for pulse lengths, delays and frequencies\nPower levels: Representation of powers and RF field strength calculations\nFrequency lists: Frequency list handling and conversions\nWindow functions: Apodization functions and lineshape effects\nSample metadata: Working with sample metadata and the NMRSamples schema\nReference: Metadata: metadata functions and the standard metadata keys","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Processing-raw-data","page":"Processing raw data","text":"warning: Under development\nLoading and processing raw time-domain data is new and its interface may change. The examples on this page use raw Bruker data, which is not shipped with the package, so they are not run when the documentation is built.\n\nNMRTools can process raw Bruker data (fid/ser files) into spectra. Processing steps are ordinary functions that take and return NMRData, so they can be chained with |>. Throughout processing the element type is Multicomplex: each dimension's quadrature is carried by its own imaginary unit (im1 for the direct dimension, im2 for the first indirect dimension, …), and the :mcindex metadata of each dimension records which unit belongs to it.","title":"Processing raw data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Quick-start:-autoprocess","page":"Processing raw data","text":"autoprocess turns raw data into a phased spectrum in one step: water suppression for ¹H spectra in H₂O, a cos² window, zero-filling to twice the size, Fourier transformation and automatic phasing of every dimension.\n\nusing NMRTools\nspec = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\") |> autoprocess\nspec = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\") |> autoprocess(window=ExponentialWindow(1.0), solvent=false)\n\nEach step can be changed with a keyword, for all dimensions or per dimension with a dictionary:\n\nspec = autoprocess(loadfid(\"hsqc/1\");\n                   window=Dict(T1Dim => Cos2Window(), T2Dim => Cos2Window()),\n                   zerofill=Dict(T2Dim => 4),\n                   phase=Dict(F2Dim => (0.0, 0.0)))     # fixed phases for the indirect dimension\n\nThe rest of this page describes the individual steps.","title":"Quick start: autoprocess"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Loading-raw-data","page":"Processing raw data","text":"loadfid reads a fid (1D) or ser (nD) file and returns time-domain data:\n\nusing NMRTools\nfid = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\")       # or the path of the fid/ser file\n\nOn loading:\n\nindirect dimensions acquired with States, States-TPPI or echo/anti-echo are combined into an extra multicomplex order, so a 2D experiment has Multicomplex{Float64,2,4} elements;\nnon-phase-sensitive dimensions (relaxation delays, CEST offsets, …) become X2Dim/X3Dim;\nthe digital filter's build-up points stay in the FID, on a time axis that starts before t = 0; ft removes the group delay (see below);\nthe t = 0 point of each Fourier dimension is scaled by 0.5 (change this with firstpoint), except in the direct dimension of baseopt data;\nthe data are scaled by 2^NC, as TopSpin scales them, so that intensities after ft, which applies no normalisation, match TopSpin's.","title":"Loading raw data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Digital-filtering-and-baseopt","page":"Processing raw data","text":"Bruker spectrometers filter and decimate the oversampled signal digitally. The filter's response builds up over the first GRPDLY points of the stored FID, so time zero falls GRPDLY points into the data. The filter's response is symmetric, so those build-up points carry part of the signal at negative time.\n\nloadfid keeps the build-up points: the direct dimension's time axis starts at -GRPDLY dwell times, and the delay is recorded in its :grpdly metadata. The processing functions respect this:\n\napodize starts the window at t = 0 and leaves the build-up points unwindowed;\nzerofill appends zeros after the end of the FID;\nlinearpredict predicts from the FID from t = 0;\nft removes the delay with a linear phase ramp after the Fourier transform, which moves the build-up points to negative time (the end of the zero-filled array).\n\nThis is how TopSpin and NMRPipe process the data, and it gives the same spectrum as TopSpin. Discarding the build-up points instead truncates the filter's response and produces a \"smile\": a baseline that falls away at both edges of the spectrum.\n\nHow the delay is found depends on the acquisition parameters:\n\nParameters Treatment\nDIGMOD = 3 (baseopt, DSPFIRM rectangle) delay GRPDLY (an integer); t = 0 point not scaled\nDIGMOD = 1 (digital), DSPFVS 20–23 delay GRPDLY (may be fractional); t = 0 point scaled by 0.5\nDIGMOD = 0 (analogue) no delay\nDSPFVS outside 10–23 (FID modified, for example by cryoproc1d) time axis starts after t = 0 by the points removed beyond GRPDLY, with a warning\n\nIn baseopt mode the spectrometer aligns acquisition with time zero (the pulse programme's acqt0) and corrects the initial points of the FID itself, so no first-order phase correction should be needed and the t = 0 point already has its half weight.\n\nA DSPFVS outside 10–23 is not a firmware version Bruker defines. TopSpin's cryoproc1d, used to process X-nucleus spectra from cryoprobes, sets DSPFVS = 0 after rewriting the FID with its start removed: the filter build-up and some points after time zero (the original is saved in another experiment number, recorded in audita.txt). loadfid compares TD in acqu, which keeps the original value, with TD in acqus to find how many points after time zero are missing, and starts the time axis at that time, so that ft restores the correct time origin and the lines can be phased together. A warning reports the number of missing points. Their absence leaves a ripple around strong lines. TopSpin rebuilds them by backward linear prediction with thousands of coefficients; linearpredict(fid, T1Dim; direction=:backward, npoints=n), or autoprocess(fid; rebuild=true), rebuilds them with a low-order prediction, which removes the ripple but can distort the baseline when the FID contains fast-decaying signals such as probe background.","title":"Digital filtering and baseopt"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#The-processing-pipeline","page":"Processing raw data","text":"spec = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\") |>\n       apodize(ExponentialWindow(1.0)) |>\n       zerofill |>\n       ft |>\n       phase(F1Dim, 128.5) |>\n       realest\n\nFunction Acts on Effect\napodize time domain multiply by a window function\nzerofill time domain append zeros (default: double the size)\nlinearpredict time domain extend a dimension, or repair its first points\nsolventfilter time domain remove a solvent signal\nft either Fourier transform (inverse=true for the inverse)\nphase either zero- and first-order phase correction\nautophase frequency domain estimate and apply a phase correction\nextract either select a region, e.g. extract(F1Dim, 6.0 .. 10.0)\nrealest either the purely real part, giving Float64 data\n\nWithout a dimension argument, apodize, zerofill, ft and autophase act on each Fourier dimension in turn (for autophase, each frequency dimension), applying the same settings to every one; linearpredict extends the indirect dimensions, and solventfilter acts on the direct dimension, where the solvent signal is. Pass a dimension to act on one only, for example to use different windows in each dimension of a 2D experiment:\n\nspec = loadfid(\"hsqc/1\") |>\n       apodize(T1Dim, Cos2Window()) |> apodize(T2Dim, Cos2Window()) |>\n       zerofill |> ft |>\n       phase(F1Dim, -87.5) |> phase(F2Dim, 0.0, 180.0) |>\n       realest\n\nEach function also accepts a Dict of spectra and processes every entry. Without a spectrum argument, each returns a function for use with |>, as in the examples above.\n\nrealest(spec) gives the purely real part of every point, real in every dimension. real and imag act elementwise as in MulticomplexNumbers, taking the coefficients of the highest-order imaginary unit: for 2D data real(spec) and imag(spec) are the parts real and imaginary in the indirect dimension, each still complex in the direct dimension. Individual components are available with component.","title":"The processing pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Phase-conventions","page":"Processing raw data","text":"phase(spec, dim, p0, p1) applies the phase θ = p0 + p1·n/N to point n (counting from zero at the left-hand, highest-ppm edge), in degrees. The sign and pivot match TopSpin's PHC0 and PHC1, so values can be copied between the two. Pass pivot (in ppm) to move the point at which only p0 acts.\n\nEach call adds to the dimension's :phase metadata, which holds the total correction applied so far as (p0=…, p1=…) with p0 referred to the first point:\n\nmetadata(spec, F1Dim, :phase)    # (p0 = 128.5, p1 = 0.0)\n\nnote: Echo/anti-echo data\nFor 1D data, NMRTools and TopSpin need the same PHC0/PHC1. For 2D echo/anti-echo data the phases currently differ from TopSpin's, by about 90° in the direct dimension and 180° in the indirect dimension. This is a known issue in the echo/anti-echo recombination in loadfid.","title":"Phase conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Automatic-phasing","page":"Processing raw data","text":"autophase estimates the phase correction of a frequency-domain spectrum and applies it, to every frequency dimension in turn unless a dimension is given:\n\nspec = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\") |> apodize(ExponentialWindow(1.0)) |> zerofill |> ft |>\n       autophase |> realest\nmetadata(spec, F1Dim, :phase)    # (p0 = 141.3, p1 = -4.2)\n\nhsqc = loadfid(\"hsqc/1\") |> apodize(Cos2Window()) |> zerofill |> ft |>\n       autophase(F1Dim) |> phase(F2Dim, 0.0, 180.0)    # autophase the direct dimension only\n\nThe estimate is logged at debug level; set ENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"NMRTools\" to see it.\n\nTo see the estimate without applying it, use estimatephase, which works on one dimension (the direct dimension unless another is given). It returns the phases with a residual (the weighted rms phase error of the lines used, in degrees), the number of line groups used, and the groups left out of the fit because their phase disagreed. A large residual, few groups, or a strong rejected group indicates an unreliable estimate:\n\nest = estimatephase(spec)\nest.p0, est.p1, est.residual, est.nlines\nest.rejected      # [(shift = -130.36, residual = 70.0, relsnr = 0.029)]\nest.edges         # [(edge = :left, snr = 95.2, residual = 0.4, used = true), …]\nphase(spec, F1Dim, est.p0, est.p1)\n\nautophase warns when a rejected line is at least 2% as strong as the strongest line used. Such a line may have a genuinely different phase, or the first-order phase may be far from its expected value. The second case is common for heteronuclei with wide spectral windows: there, set the expected first-order phase with p1 (increasing maxdelay if needed) or fix it with order=0.\n\nUseful options:\n\norder=0 fits the zero-order phase only, with the first-order phase fixed at p1 (default 0). Once the digital-filter group delay has been removed, the first-order phase of the direct dimension should be close to zero, so this is often the more robust choice.\nmaxdelay sets how far the first-order phase may range. A first-order phase is a shift of the time origin, so the range is given as a timing uncertainty (default 20 µs) and converted with the spectral width: ±100° for a 14 kHz ¹H spectrum, ±950° for a 130 kHz ¹⁹F spectrum.\nexclude=[4.6 .. 4.9] ignores lines in the given chemical-shift ranges, for example water or buffer.\nλ sets how strongly the first-order phase is pulled towards p1. The pull is scaled by the scatter of the line phases and the number of lines, so it has little effect when many lines agree. Increase it when the lines cover only part of the spectrum.","title":"Automatic phasing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#How-it-works","page":"Processing raw data","text":"The spectrum is modelled as a sum of Lorentzian lines with estimatepoles (see below), so each line has a frequency and a phase. Lines within a coupling constant of each other are grouped and the phase of their summed amplitude is used: echoes in the pulse sequence (excitation sculpting, for example) give the lines of a multiplet antiphase dispersive components that cancel only in the sum. Lines much broader than the typical linewidth are ignored when they overlap a narrower line, since they then describe baseline distortions or the tails of a non-Lorentzian line such as a radiation-damped water signal. Isolated broad lines are kept.\n\nThe two edges of the spectrum give two further observations. With the correct phase the real part of the spectrum is flat far from the lines, while the imaginary part carries the dispersion tails of the strong lines. A small phase error leaks those tails into the real part, tilting the baseline at the edges (zero order) or curling it (first order). The phase at which the real part of each edge region vanishes is therefore a precise phase measurement wherever the edges carry signal, as they do in protein spectra and spectra with residual water.\n\nThe correction is a weighted circular regression of the observed phases against position. Each observation is weighted by its inverse variance: a statistical part from its signal-to-noise ratio, plus a systematic part, which for line groups is their scatter about the fit and for edges is 2°. The line phases are taken modulo 180°, so negative peaks are treated as correctly phased rather than as phase errors. For each first-order phase the best zero-order phase has a closed form, so the fit is a one-dimensional search over the first-order phase. Observations that disagree with the fit by more than maxresidual degrees are discarded and the fit repeated. The remaining 180° ambiguity is resolved by a majority vote of the line groups, so a single dominant line (residual water, say) cannot decide it.\n\nCompared with hand phasing in TopSpin on ¹H spectra of proteins and small molecules, the estimates agreed within a few degrees across the spectrum where the edges carry signal. Without edge signal, as in a broadband ¹⁹F spectrum with weak lines, the estimate rests on the lines alone and can be off by 10–30° at the edges. With only one or two lines, the first-order phase cannot be determined from the lines and stays close to p1. Check the result on new types of data, and use order=0, p1 and exclude where the fit is poor.\n\nFor multidimensional data, autophase(spec, dim) estimates the correction from a single trace along dim and applies it to the whole dataset. The trace takes the first point of every other time dimension, and sums every other frequency dimension over the points that carry signal. Apodize indirect dimensions before phasing: the truncation ripples of an unapodized dimension reach the edges of the spectrum and bias the edge observations by several degrees. A dimension with no lines in its trace is left unphased by autophase without a dimension, with a warning.\n\nwarning: Multidimensional phasing is less reliable\nCollapsing a 2D spectrum to one trace per dimension crowds many cross peaks into a few overlapping line groups, and the estimate rests on those. On a ¹⁵N TROSY with well over a hundred resolved cross peaks, the estimates differed from the correct phases by tens of degrees. Check the result, and set phases known from the pulse sequence (indirect dimensions are often 0° or 90°, with a first-order phase of 0° or 180°) with phase.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Pole-models","page":"Processing raw data","text":"estimatepoles models a spectrum as a sum of damped complex exponentials, one Pole per line, using band-wise harmonic inversion (HSVD). Each pole has a chemical shift, a linewidth (Hz), a complex amplitude (whose magnitude is the line integral and whose argument is the line's phase) and a signal-to-noise ratio:\n\npoles = estimatepoles(spec)\n# Pole(3.6848 ppm, 2.6 Hz, phase 128.2°, SNR 2.31e4)","title":"Pole models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Linear-prediction-and-solvent-suppression","page":"Processing raw data","text":"linearpredict extends a time-domain dimension by linear prediction, typically a truncated indirect dimension. Process the direct dimension first, so that each indirect vector belongs to one direct-dimension frequency:\n\nspec = loadfid(\"hsqc/1\") |>\n       apodize(T1Dim) |> zerofill(T1Dim) |> ft(T1Dim) |>\n       linearpredict(T2Dim) |>\n       apodize(T2Dim) |> zerofill(T2Dim) |> ft(T2Dim) |>\n       realest\n\nlinearpredict(spec, dim; replace=n) replaces the first n points by backward prediction instead.\n\nsolventfilter removes a solvent signal from the FID before apodization. By default it acts on the carrier frequency; pass shift (ppm) to target another position. method=:lowpass subtracts a low-pass filtered copy of the FID (the convolution difference method of nmrPipe's SOL), and method=:hsvd fits and subtracts only the lines within a band around the target, which is slower but more selective.","title":"Linear prediction and solvent suppression"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/processing/#Saving-in-Bruker-format","page":"Processing raw data","text":"savenmr writes a frequency-domain spectrum as Bruker processed data, and savefid writes time-domain data as a raw Bruker fid/ser:\n\nfid = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\")\nspec = fid |> apodize(ExponentialWindow(1.0)) |> zerofill |> ft |> autophase\n\nsavenmr(\"myexperiment/1\", spec; procno=2)          # writes myexperiment/1/pdata/2\nsavefid(\"processed/1\", linearpredict(fid))          # new experiment folder\n\nsavenmr writes the real part (1r, 2rr, 3rrr) and, for multicomplex 1D and 2D data, the imaginary parts, as 32-bit integers scaled by NC_proc. The processing parameter files, both current (proc, proc2, …) and status (procs, proc2s, …), record the axes, the window function and the phase correction applied.\n\nsavefid undoes the steps taken by loadfid: it restores the first points and the digital-filter group delay, re-encodes each indirect dimension with its original quadrature scheme and puts the dimensions back in acquisition order. loadfid on the written experiment returns the same data. Data are written as Float64 (DTYPA=2, as recorded by Avance NEO spectrometers) by default; pass dtype=Int32 for older TopSpin versions.\n\nBoth functions copy the acquisition parameter files (acqus, acqu2s, pulse programme, lists, …) from the experiment the data were loaded from, updating only the parameters that describe the written data, such as TD. If there is no source experiment, minimal parameter files are generated from the dimension metadata. Existing data are not overwritten unless overwrite=true is passed.\n\nnote: Sign convention\nNMRTools spectra are the complex conjugate of TopSpin's: the real parts agree and the imaginary parts have the opposite sign. savenmr and loadnmr(...; allcomponents=true) convert between the two, so imaginary data read from or written to Bruker files follow each program's own convention.","title":"Saving in Bruker format"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Power-levels","page":"Power levels","text":"Power levels from acquisition parameters are represented using the Power type, which handles both Watts (W) and dB attenuation units with automatic conversion.","title":"Power levels"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Creating-Power-objects","page":"Power levels","text":"Power values must be created with an explicit unit specification:\n\n# From dB attenuation\np1 = Power(30.0, :dB)\n\n# From Watts\np2 = Power(0.001, :W)\n\n# Works with any numeric type\np3 = Power(20, :dB)\n\nnote: Note\nCalling Power(value) without a unit will throw an error.","title":"Creating Power objects"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Accessing-from-acqus-data","page":"Power levels","text":"Power levels are automatically parsed when loading data:\n\nspec = loadnmr(\"experiment\")\n\n# Get power level (returns Power object)\np = acqus(spec, :pl, 1)  # First power level","title":"Accessing from acqus data"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Accessing-values","page":"Power levels","text":"Use db() and watts() to retrieve values in either unit:\n\np = Power(20.0, :dB)\n\ndb(p)     # 20.0 (dB)\nwatts(p)  # 0.01 (W)\n\nConversion formulas:\n\nmathrmdB = -10 log_10(mathrmwatts)\n\nmathrmwatts = 10^-mathrmdB10","title":"Accessing values"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Zero-power-handling","page":"Power levels","text":"Zero watts is handled specially to avoid logarithm issues:\n\np_zero = Power(0.0, :W)\ndb(p_zero)     # 120.0 (very high attenuation)\nwatts(p_zero)  # ≈ 1e-12\n\nThis is relevant when parsing acqus files where unused power levels may be zero.","title":"Zero power handling"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Converting-to-RF-field-strength","page":"Power levels","text":"The hz() function converts power to radiofrequency field strength in Hz using calibration data.","title":"Converting to RF field strength"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Using-a-reference-RF-strength","page":"Power levels","text":"# Known calibration: 25 kHz at 30 dB\nref_power = Power(30.0, :dB)\nref_hz = 25000.0\n\n# Calculate RF at different power\ntest_power = Power(36.0, :dB)\nrf = hz(test_power, ref_power, ref_hz)\n\nConversion formula:\n\nmathrmHz = mathrmHz_ref times 10^-DeltamathrmdB20\n\nwhere DeltamathrmdB = mathrmdB - mathrmdB_ref.","title":"Using a reference RF strength"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/power/#Using-pulse-calibration","page":"Power levels","text":"# Known: 10 μs pulse gives 90° at 30 dB\nref_power = Power(30.0, :dB)\npulse_length = 10.0    # μs\nflip_angle = 90.0      # degrees\n\n# Calculate RF at different power\ntest_power = Power(33.0, :dB)\nrf = hz(test_power, ref_power, pulse_length, flip_angle)\n\nThis first calculates the reference RF strength:\n\nmathrmHz_ref = fractheta360 times t\n\nwhere theta is the flip angle (degrees) and t is the pulse length (seconds), then applies the power scaling formula above.","title":"Using pulse calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Plotting-with-Makie","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"NMRTools provides a Makie extension with nmrplot and nmrplot! for interactive or publication-quality figures. Load NMRTools and a Makie backend:\n\nusing NMRTools\nusing CairoMakie   # static figures; use GLMakie for interactive\n\nnote: NMR conventions applied automatically\nSeveral behaviours differ from plain Makie to match NMR conventions:Titles come from the spectrum's label (set from the title file on load). Pass title=\"\" to suppress or title=\"My title\" to override.\nAxes are reversed (high ppm on the left/bottom) and labelled automatically from dimension metadata.\nxlims/ylims are passed directly to nmrplot rather than calling xlims!(ax, ...) afterwards. For 1D plots the y-axis rescales to fit the displayed region.\nContour levels for 2D spectra are set automatically from the noise level (starting at 5σ, geometric spacing of 1.7). The keyboard can be used to raise or lower them interactively (see below).\nLegends are disabled by default. When enabled, entries are named automatically from spectrum labels.","title":"Plotting with Makie"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#1D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"fig = nmrplot(exampledata(\"1D_1H\"))\nsave(\"makie-1D.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nSet the plot range with xlims — the y-axis rescales to fit the visible region:\n\nfig = nmrplot(exampledata(\"1D_1H\"), xlims=(-1, 4.5))\nsave(\"makie-1D-xlim.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nOverlay additional spectra with nmrplot!:\n\nnmrplot!(fig, exampledata(\"1D_1H\") / 2)\nsave(\"makie-1Db.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\n1D spectra can be stacked by passing the argument vstack=true, or vstack=X where X controls the spacing:\n\nfig = Figure()\nnmrplot(fig[1,1], exampledata(\"1D_19F_titration\"), xlims=(-121.8,-125.2), vstack=true, title=\"vstack=true\")\nnmrplot(fig[1,2], exampledata(\"1D_19F_titration\"), xlims=(-121.8,-125.2), vstack=2, title=\"vstack=2\")\nnmrplot(fig[1,3], exampledata(\"1D_19F_titration\"), xlims=(-121.8,-125.2), vstack=10, title=\"vstack=10\")\nsave(\"makie-1D-vstack.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"1D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#2D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"spec2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\nfig = nmrplot(spec2d)\nsave(\"makie-2D.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nAdd 1D projections along either axis:\n\nfig = nmrplot(exampledata(\"2D_HN\"), xprojection=true, yprojection=true)\nsave(\"makie-2D-projections.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nPlot a series of spectra in one call; colours cycle automatically and a legend can be added:\n\nfig = nmrplot(exampledata(\"2D_HN_titration\"), legend=:lt)\nsave(\"makie-2D-series.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nOr add spectra one-by-one with nmrplot!:\n\ndats = exampledata(\"1D_19F_titration\")\nfig, ax = nmrplot(dats[1], title=\"\", xlims=(-120, -128))\nnmrplot!(fig, dats[5])\nnmrplot!(fig, dats[10])\naxislegend(ax)\nsave(\"makie-2D-series-add.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"2D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Contour-colours-and-levels","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"Negative contours are shown by default as a faded version of the positive colour. Disable them or set a different colour:\n\nfig = nmrplot(spec2d; negcontours=false)\nsave(\"makie-2Db.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nfig = nmrplot(spec2d; negcolor=:red)\nsave(\"makie-2Dc.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nPass levels as an integer to change the number of contour levels, or as a vector of absolute values to use explicit levels:\n\nnmrplot(spec2d; levels=8)                    # 8 geometric levels from 5σ\nnmrplot(spec2d; levels=[2e6, 4e6, 8e6])      # explicit positive levels\n\nspacing sets the geometric ratio between levels (default 1.7):\n\nnmrplot(spec2d; spacing=2.0, levels=10)","title":"Contour colours and levels"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Keyboard-navigation-(GLMakie)","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"In GLMakie, press ↑ / ↓ to raise or lower all contour levels in the figure by one step (multiplied/divided by spacing). The key repeats while held. This works for any spectrum added with nmrplot or nmrplot!.","title":"Keyboard navigation (GLMakie)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Pseudo-2D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"Pseudo-2D spectra (one frequency + one non-frequency dimension) support three display styles via the style keyword:\n\nstyle Description\n:heatmap colour map (default)\n:flat overlaid 1D slices\n:waterfall 3D slice view\n\ndiffusiondata = exampledata(\"pseudo2D_XSTE\")\ndiffusiondata = setgradientlist(diffusiondata, LinRange(0.02, 0.98, 10))\nfig = nmrplot(diffusiondata, xlims=(7, 9.5))\nsave(\"makie-pseudo2D-heatmap.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nfig = nmrplot(diffusiondata, xlims=(7, 9.5), style=:flat)\nsave(\"makie-pseudo2D-flat.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nfig = nmrplot(diffusiondata, xlims=(7, 9.5), style=:waterfall)\nsave(\"makie-pseudo2D-waterfall.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Pseudo-2D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Saving-figures","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"Makie figures can be saved using GLMakie or CairoMakie as png files:\n\nsave(\"spectrum.png\", fig)\nsave(\"spectrum.png\", fig; px_per_unit=2)  # 2× resolution\n\nTo save figures as vector graphics (pdf or svg), switch to CairoMakie:\n\nusing CairoMakie\nsave(\"spectrum.svg\", fig)          # vector graphics\nsave(\"spectrum.pdf\", fig)","title":"Saving figures"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Layout-and-composition","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"nmrplot accepts a GridPosition to place spectra into a shared figure alongside other Makie plots:\n\nfig = Figure()\nnmrplot(fig[1, 1], exampledata(\"1D_1H\"); title=\"\", xlims=(-1, 4.5))\nnmrplot(fig[1, 2], exampledata(\"2D_HN\"); title=\"\", xlims=(6, 10))\nscatter(fig[2, :], randn(100))\nsave(\"makie-plots.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nAxis properties can be customised after the fact via the returned ax handle, or passed up-front via the axis keyword:\n\nfig, ax, plt = nmrplot(spec)\nax.title[] = \"My spectrum\"\n\n# equivalently:\nfig = nmrplot(spec; axis=(; title=\"My spectrum\"))","title":"Layout and composition"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Animations","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"The standard Makie record function works directly with nmrplot!. This example cycles through a titration series and saves a GIF:\n\nspectra = exampledata(\"2D_HN_titration\")\nref = spectra[1]\n\nfig, ax, _ = nmrplot(spectra[1]; normalize=ref, xlims=(6, 10.5))\nrecord(fig, \"makie-titration.gif\", spectra; framerate=4) do s\n    empty!(ax)\n    ax.title[] = label(s)\n    nmrplot!(ax, s; normalize=ref)\nend\nnothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nFor interactive use with GLMakie, the same record approach works — or you can update the plot live by re-calling nmrplot! in response to user events.","title":"Animations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#Options-reference","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"","title":"Options reference"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#1D-spectra-2","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"Keyword Default Description\ntitle spectrum label Pass \"\" to suppress.\ncolor auto (Wong palette) Line colour.\nnormalize true Scale by scans and receiver gain. Pass false to use raw intensities.\nvstack false Stack spectra vertically. Pass a number to scale the offset.\nxlims auto Chemical shift range (ppm). y-axis rescales to fit.\nlegend false Legend position, e.g. :rt. Entries named from spectrum labels.\naxis (;) Extra Axis keyword arguments (escape hatch).","title":"1D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/makie/#2D-spectra-2","page":"Plotting with Makie","text":"Keyword Default Description\ntitle spectrum label Pass \"\" to suppress.\ncolor / poscolor auto (Wong palette) Positive contour colour.\nnegcontours true Show negative contours.\nnegcolor faded positive Negative contour colour.\nnormalize true Scale contour threshold by scans/gain. Pass a reference spectrum for cross-spectrum comparison.\nlevels 12 Number of contour levels (Int), or a vector of explicit positive level values.\nspacing 1.7 Geometric ratio between successive contour levels; also the step size per ↑/↓ keypress.\nxprojection nothing 1D projection along the direct dimension. Pass true for max-projection or a 1D NMRData.\nyprojection nothing 1D projection along the indirect dimension.\nxlims auto Direct-dimension range (ppm).\nylims auto Indirect-dimension range (ppm).\nlegend false Legend position, e.g. :lt. Entries named from spectrum labels.\naxis (;) Extra Axis keyword arguments (escape hatch).","title":"2D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Utilities","page":"Utilities","text":"This page covers tools that work alongside the main data types: combining raw Bruker experiments, and importing assigned chemical shifts from the BMRB.","title":"Utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Combining-NMR-experiments","page":"Utilities","text":"sumexpts(INPUTFILES...; out=OUTPUT) is a utility function provided by NMRTools that allows you to combine multiple Bruker NMR experiments with optional weighting factors. The function accepts the following parameters:\n\nINPUTFILES...: One or more experiment numbers or paths to be combined\nout: The experiment number or path where the combined data will be stored (keyword argument)\n\nThe function works with both 1D experiments (using fid files) and multidimensional experiments (using ser files).","title":"Combining NMR experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Basic-usage","page":"Utilities","text":"The most common way to use sumexpts is with experiment numbers, which is the standard Bruker convention.\n\njulia> using NMRTools\n\njulia> cd(\"myexperiment\")\n\njulia> sumexpts(11, 12, out=999)\n=== NMR Experiment Summation ===\nOutput: 999\nInputs: 11, 12\nWeights: 1.0, 1.0\nCreated output experiment from 11\nData format: Float64 (little-endian)\nDetected nD experiment, using ser files\nCleaning processed data files in pdata/1...\n  Removed: 3rrr\nRemoved pdata/101 directory\nRemoved pdata/999 directory\nLoading 11/ser\nLoading 12/ser\nApplied weight 1.0 to experiment 11\nApplied weight 1.0 to experiment 12\nWriting output to 999/ser\nScans from 11: 8 × 1.0 = 8.0\nScans from 12: 16 × 1.0 = 16.0\nUpdated number of scans to 24\nUpdated title file with experiment summary\n=== Operation completed successfully ===\n\nThis creates a new experiment directory by copying experiment 11 as a template, then adding the raw data from all input experiments together. The result is stored in experiment 999.\n\nYou can also use full paths if your experiments are in different directories:\n\n# Combine experiments from a specific path\njulia> sumexpts(\"myexperiment/11\", \"myexperiment/12\", out=\"myexperiment/999\")\n# Output similar to above example","title":"Basic usage"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Adding-multiple-experiments","page":"Utilities","text":"To combine more than two experiments, provide all experiment numbers:\n\n# Add three experiments together\njulia> cd(\"myexperiment\")\n\njulia> sumexpts(11, 12, 13, out=999)\n# Output shows all three experiments being combined\n\nBy default, all experiments are weighted equally. The result will be the sum of all input data.","title":"Adding multiple experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Creating-difference-spectra","page":"Utilities","text":"To create a difference spectrum, use the weights parameter:\n\n# Create a difference spectrum (experiment 12 subtracted from experiment 11)\njulia> cd(\"myexperiment\")\n\njulia> sumexpts(11, 12, out=999, weights=[1.0, -1.0])\n=== NMR Experiment Summation ===\nOutput: 999\nInputs: 11, 12\nWeights: 1.0, -1.0\nCreated output experiment from 11\nData format: Float64 (little-endian)\nDetected nD experiment, using ser files\nCleaning processed data files in pdata/1...\n  Removed: 2rr\nLoading 11/ser\nLoading 12/ser\nApplied weight 1.0 to experiment 11\nApplied weight -1.0 to experiment 12\nWriting output to 999/ser\nScans from 11: 16 × 1.0 = 16.0\nScans from 12: 16 × 1.0 = 16.0\nUpdated number of scans to 32\nUpdated title file with experiment summary\n=== Operation completed successfully ===\n\nThis is particularly useful for applications like:\n\nReaction monitoring\nComparing before/after spectra\nHighlighting changes between samples\nRemoving background signals","title":"Creating difference spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Weighted-combinations","page":"Utilities","text":"You can apply different weights to each experiment to create weighted averages or to emphasize certain spectra:\n\n# Weighted combination of three experiments\njulia> cd(\"myexperiment\")\n\njulia> sumexpts(11, 12, 13, out=999, weights=[0.5, 1.0, 2.0])\n=== NMR Experiment Summation ===\nOutput: 999\nInputs: 11, 12, 13\nWeights: 0.5, 1.0, 2.0\nCreated output experiment from 11\nData format: Float64 (little-endian)\nDetected nD experiment, using ser files\nCleaning processed data files in pdata/1...\nLoading 11/ser\nLoading 12/ser\nLoading 13/ser\nApplied weight 0.5 to experiment 11\nApplied weight 1.0 to experiment 12\nApplied weight 2.0 to experiment 13\nWriting output to 999/ser\nScans from 11: 8 × 0.5 = 4.0\nScans from 12: 16 × 1.0 = 16.0\nScans from 13: 32 × 2.0 = 64.0\nUpdated number of scans to 84\nUpdated title file with experiment summary\n=== Operation completed successfully ===\n\nThe weights vector must have the same length as the number of input experiments.","title":"Weighted combinations"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Experimental-details","page":"Utilities","text":"The function handles several important details automatically:\n\nThe number of scans (NS parameter) in the output is updated to the weighted sum of input scan counts\nThe title file is updated to indicate which experiments were combined and their weights\nProcessed data files in pdata/1 are removed to prevent inconsistencies\nIf experiments have different data sizes, they are truncated to match the smallest dataset","title":"Experimental details"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Command-line-usage","page":"Utilities","text":"The function can also be called from the command line:\n\ncd myexperiment\njulia -e 'using NMRTools; sumexpts(11, 12, out=999)'\n\nOr for creating a difference spectrum:\n\ncd myexperiment\njulia -e 'using NMRTools; sumexpts(11, 12, out=999, weights=[1.0, -1.0])'\n\nThis allows for easy integration with processing workflows and shell scripts.","title":"Command-line usage"},{"category":"section","location":"utilities/#Assigned-chemical-shifts-from-the-BMRB","page":"Utilities","text":"loadbmrb downloads the assigned chemical shifts of a BMRB entry and assembles them into the peaks expected in a given experiment, as a peak list of the kind described in Peaks and peak lists:\n\nhsqc = loadbmrb(15000, H1, N15)          # ¹H,¹⁵N HSQC, side chains included\nnmrplot(hsqc; xlabel=\"¹H (ppm)\", ylabel=\"¹⁵N (ppm)\")\n\nloadbmrb(15000, H1, C13)                 # ¹H,¹³C HSQC\n\nCalled with just an entry ID (or the name of a local NMR-STAR file), loadbmrb returns the shifts themselves. Download them once and build several lists from them with peaklist, which takes the same dimension arguments:\n\nshifts = loadbmrb(15000)\nhsqc = peaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\")        # backbone amide ¹H,¹⁵N HSQC\n\nDimensions are given in spectrum order, direct dimension first. Each argument names an atom, optionally with an offset to a neighbouring residue, so any triple-resonance correlation can be written down directly:\n\npeaklist(shifts, \"CO-1\", \"N\")            # CON: C′ of residue i-1 with N of residue i\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\", \"CO-1\")       # HNCO\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\", \"CA\")         # HNCA, i peaks\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\", \"CA-1\")       # HNCA, i-1 peaks\n\nPassing nuclei instead of atom names gives every directly bonded X–H pair, which is what an HSQC actually shows. This includes side-chain NH and NH₂ groups for ¹⁵N, and every CH, CH₂ and CH₃ group for ¹³C. Methyl and methylene protons with identical shifts give a single peak:\n\npeaklist(shifts, H1, N15)                # ¹H,¹⁵N HSQC including side chains\npeaklist(shifts, H1, C13)                # ¹H,¹³C HSQC\n\nA shift list is a Vector{ChemicalShift}, so residues can be selected with filter before building peaks, e.g. for the methyl region of an ILV-labelled sample:\n\nilv = filter(s -> s.residuename in (\"ILE\", \"LEU\", \"VAL\"), shifts)\npeaklist(ilv, H1, C13)\n\nPeaks are labelled by residue and atom (L23H-N, D5C-F6N, V9HG2-CG2), so a predicted list can be overlaid on a spectrum with nmrplot!, or saved with savepeaks as the starting point for an analysis in NMRAnalysis.jl. BMRB shifts were measured under the deposited sample conditions and referencing, so expect systematic offsets; register! finds and removes the overall offset (see Registering a peak list onto a spectrum):\n\npks = loadbmrb(15000, H1, N15)\nregister!(pks, spec)","title":"Assigned chemical shifts from the BMRB"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"NMRData objects can store various metadata associated with the spectrum and each of the dimensions.\n\nMetadata are stored as dictionaries using symbols as keys (e.g. :ns). They can be accessed using the metadata function, or directly from an NMRData object using a dictionary-style lookup. Metadata associated with axes are accessed by providing an additional reference, either as a dimension number or type (e.g. F1Dim, F2Dim etc.).\n\nmetadata(nmrdata, key) # spectrum metadata\nnmrdata[key]\n\nmetadata(nmrdata, dimension, key) # axis metadata\nnmrdata[dimension, key]","title":"Metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Labels","page":"Metadata","text":"","title":"Labels"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Acquisition-data","page":"Metadata","text":"When spectra are loaded, the contents of the acqus file  are parsed (as are the acqu2s files etc. too, if present). These can be accessed with :acqus and :acqu2s keys, etc. For convenience though, the additional function acqus is provided to access acquisition data directly.","title":"Acquisition data"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Auxiliary-files","page":"Metadata","text":"acqus can also be used to access the contents of auxiliary files (and if not present, nothing will be returned). Note that NMRTools will perform automatic unit conversion as follows:\n\n:vclist: variable loopcounter\n:vdlist: variable delays, in seconds\n:valist: variable amplitude, in dB (converted from Watts if necessary)\n:vplist: variable pulse lengths, in seconds\n:fq1list up to :fq8list: frequency lists – see the Frequency lists page for more information.","title":"Auxiliary files"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Frequency-list-API","page":"Metadata","text":"Frequency lists can be specified on the spectrometer in a number of ways - in Hz, in ppm, and relative to the spectrometer frequency or the base frequency (0 ppm). Frequency lists are therefore stored in NMRTools as FQList structures which encode this additional information.\n\nRaw numerical data can be accessed using the data() function, but it is recommended to use ppm and hz functions to access frequency data safely. See the Frequency lists page for detailed examples.","title":"Frequency list API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Standard-metadata:-spectra","page":"Metadata","text":"Key Description\n:acqusfilename path to acqus file\n:acqus contents of acqus file, as a dictionary\n:acqu2s, :acqu3s contents of acqu2s/acqu3s files (if present)\n:date experiment timestamp (completion time)\n:experimentfolder path to experiment\n:field field strength, in T\n:filename spectrum filename or template\n:format input file format (:nmrpipe, :pdata, …)\n:label short label (first line of title pdata file), used for captions\n:ndim number of dimensions\n:noise RMS noise level (see estimatenoise)\n:nuclei set of Nucleus values\n:ns number of scans\n:pulseprogram pulse programme (PULPROG)\n:rg receiver gain\n:solvent solvent string, e.g. \"H2O+D2O\"\n:temperature temperature, in K\n:title spectrum title (contents of title pdata file)\n:topspin TopSpin version used for acquisition","title":"Standard metadata: spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/metadata/#Standard-metadata:-frequency-and-time-dimensions","page":"Metadata","text":"Keys marked † are set on time dimensions by loadfid and carried over to the frequency dimension by ft.\n\nKey Description\n:aq acquisition time, in seconds\n:bf base frequency, in Hz\n:firstpoint † scaling factor applied to the t = 0 point\n:grpdly † digital filter group delay, in dwell times (direct dimension; 0 after ft)\n:label short label\n:mcindex imaginary unit of the dimension (1 for im1, …), nothing if none\n:npoints final number of real data points in dimension (after extraction)\n:nucleus Nucleus of the dimension\n:offsethz carrier offset from bf, in Hz\n:offsetppm carrier offset from bf, in ppm\n:phase total phase correction applied, (p0=…, p1=…) in degrees, p0 at the first point\n:pseudodim flag indicating non-frequency domain data (false for frequency domain)\n:quadrature † quadrature scheme of an indirect dimension, e.g. :states_tppi\n:referenceoffset applied referencing, in ppm\n:region extracted region, expressed as a range in points, otherwise missing\n:sf carrier frequency, in Hz\n:swhz spectrum width, in Hz\n:swppm spectrum width, in ppm\n:td number of complex points acquired, including LP\n:tdzf number of complex points when FT executed, including LP and ZF\n:window WindowFunction encoding applied apodization","title":"Standard metadata: frequency and time dimensions"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.metadata-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"metadata(nmrdata, key)\nmetadata(nmrdata, dim, key)\nmetadata(nmrdimension, key)\n\nReturn the metadata for specified key, or nothing if not found. Keys are passed as symbols.\n\nExamples (spectrum metadata)\n\n:ns: number of scans\n:ds: number of dummy scans\n:rg: receiver gain\n:ndim: number of dimensions\n:title: spectrum title (contents of title pdata file)\n:filename: spectrum filename\n:pulseprogram: title of pulse program used for acquisition\n:experimentfolder: path to experiment\n:noise: RMS noise level\n\nExamples (dimension metadata)\n\n:pseudodim: flag indicating non-frequency domain data\n:npoints: final number of (real) data points in dimension (after extraction)\n:td: number of complex points acquired\n:tdzf: number of complex points when FT executed, including LP and ZF\n:bf: base frequency, in Hz\n:sf: carrier frequency, in Hz\n:offsethz: carrier offset from bf, in Hz\n:offsetppm: carrier offset from bf, in ppm\n:swhz: spectrum width, in Hz\n:swppm: spectrum width, in ppm\n:region: extracted region, expressed as a range in points, otherwise missing\n:window: WindowFunction indicating applied apodization\n:referenceoffset: referencing (in ppm) applied to the dimension\n\nSee also estimatenoise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.label-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"label(nmrdata)\nlabel(nmrdata, dim)\nlabel(nmrdimension)\n\nReturn a short label associated with an NMRData structure or an NMRDimension. By default, for a spectrum this is obtained from the first line of the title file. For a frequency dimension, this is normally something of the form 1H chemical shift (ppm).\n\nSee also label!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.label"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#NMRTools.NMRBase.label!-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"label!(nmrdata, labeltext)\nlabel!(nmrdata, dim, labeltext)\nlabel!(nmrdimension, labeltext)\n\nSet the label associated with an NMRData structure or an NMRDimension.\n\nSee also label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.label!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.units-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"units(nmrdata)\nunits(nmrdata, dim)\nunits(nmrdimension)\n\nReturn the physical units associated with an NMRData structure or an NMRDimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.units"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#NMRTools.NMRBase.acqus-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"acqus(nmrdata)\nacqus(nmrdata, key)\nacqus(nmrdata, key, index)\n\nReturn data from a Bruker acqus file, or nothing if it does not exist. Keys can be passed as symbols or strings. If no key is specified, a dictionary is returned representing the entire acqus file.\n\nIf present, the contents of auxilliary files such as vclist and vdlist can be accessed using this function.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> acqus(expt, :pulprog)\n\"zgesgp\"\njulia> acqus(expt, \"TE\")\n276.9988\njulia> acqus(expt, :p, 1)\n9.2\njulia> acqus(expt, \"D\", 1)\n0.1\njulia> acqus(expt, :vclist)\n11-element Vector{Int64}:\n[...]\n\nSee also metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.acqus"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/metadata/#NMRTools.NMRIO.FQList-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"FQList(values, unit::Symbol, relative::Bool)\nFQList(values, unit::Symbol, relative::Bool, bf, sfo)\n\nRepresents a frequency list. unit can be :Hz or :ppm, and relative indicates whether the frequency is given relative to SFO (true) or BF (false).\n\nbf and sfo optionally record the reference base and carrier frequencies (in Hz) of the spectrometer channel this list belongs to. When present, the list can be converted to ppm or Hz on its own — without a detected frequency axis — using the single-argument ppm and hz methods. See withreference to attach a reference from a channel dictionary.\n\nRaw values can be extracted using the data function, or (better) as absolute chemical shifts (in ppm) or relative offsets (in Hz) using ppm and hz functions.\n\nSee also: ppm, hz, withreference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.FQList"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"ppm(axis)\n\nReturn the ppm values for points along a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\nppm(offset, axis)\n\nReturn the chemical shifts for a given offset or list of offsets along a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\nppm(peak)\nppm(peak, n)\n\nThe position of a peak in ppm: a tuple ordered like the spectrum's dimensions, or the nth coordinate on its own.\n\n\n\n\n\nppm(f::FQList, ax::FrequencyDimension)\n\nReturn frequency list values in ppm (in absolute terms, i.e. relative to 0 ppm).\n\nSee also: hz\n\n\n\n\n\nppm(f::FQList)\n\nReturn frequency list values in ppm (in absolute terms, i.e. relative to 0 ppm), using the reference frequencies (:bf, :sfo) stored on the list. Throws an error if no reference is attached — see withreference.\n\nSee also: hz\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/metadata/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-reference-metadata","page":"Metadata","text":"hz(p::Power, ref_p::Power, ref_Hz)\n\nConvert power to radiofrequency strength in Hz using a reference power and known Hz value.\n\nUses the relationship: Hz = ref_Hz * 10^(-ΔdB/20) where ΔdB is the power difference.\n\nArguments\n\np::Power: Power to convert\nref_p::Power: Reference power with known Hz value\nref_Hz: Radiofrequency strength in Hz at the reference power\n\nReturns\n\nRadiofrequency strength in Hz for power p\n\n\n\n\n\nhz(p::Power, ref_p::Power, ref_pulselength, ref_pulseangle_deg)\n\nConvert power to radiofrequency strength in Hz using reference pulse parameters.\n\nArguments\n\np::Power: Power to convert\nref_p::Power: Reference power\nref_pulselength: Reference pulse length in microseconds\nref_pulseangle_deg: Reference pulse flip angle in degrees\n\nReturns\n\nRadiofrequency strength in Hz for power p\n\n\n\n\n\nhz(axis)\n\nReturn the offsets (in Hz) for points along a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\nhz(δ, axis)\n\nReturn the offset (in Hz) for a chemical shift (or list of shifts) on a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\nhz(f::FQList, ax::FrequencyDimension)\n\nReturn frequency list values as offsets relative to the spectrometer frequency, in Hz.\n\nSee also: ppm\n\n\n\n\n\nhz(f::FQList)\n\nReturn frequency list values as offsets relative to the spectrometer carrier frequency, in Hz, using the reference frequencies (:bf, :sfo) stored on the list. Throws an error if no reference is attached — see withreference.\n\nSee also: ppm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/timeunits/#Times-and-frequencies","page":"Times and frequencies","text":"For maximum consistency, in NMRTools all time and frequency data are stored as seconds and Hz. This includes:\n\npulse lengths\ndelays\nvdlists and vplists\nspectrometer frequencies (bf, sf)","title":"Times and frequencies"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/timeunits/#Examples","page":"Times and frequencies","text":"using NMRTools # hide\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\") # hide\n@info \"Pulse length p1, in s\" acqus(spec, :p, 1)\n\n@info \"Delay d1, in s\" acqus(spec, :d, 1)\n\n@info \"Base frequency for channel 1, in Hz\" spec[1, :bf]","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/samples/#Sample-metadata","page":"Sample metadata","text":"Sample metadata can be automatically loaded when present, following the schema defined at nmrsamples.github.io. This provides structured information about the physical sample, buffer composition, NMR tube characteristics, and personnel involved in the experiment.\n\nSample metadata is stored as JSON files in the experiment directory and is automatically associated with spectra based on timestamps:\n\n# Load spectrum — sample metadata loaded automatically if present\nspec = loadnmr(\"path/to/experiment\")\n\n# Check if sample metadata is available\nhassample(spec)\n# true\n\n# Get the path to the matched sample JSON file\nsamplefile(spec)\n# \"/nmr/projects/lysozyme/2025-08-23_163924_lysozyme.json\"","title":"Sample metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/samples/#Accessing-sample-metadata","page":"Sample metadata","text":"Use the sample function to access sample metadata:\n\n# Get the NMRSample object\nsample(spec)\n# NMRSample(\"/nmr/projects/lysozyme/2025-08-23_163924_lysozyme.json\")\n\nNavigate nested fields by passing keys:\n\n# Get sample label\nsample(spec, :sample, :label)\n# \"lysozyme\"\n\n# Get user names\nsample(spec, \"people\", \"users\")\n# [\"jsmith\"]\n\n# Get buffer solvent\nsample(spec, :buffer, :solvent)\n# \"95% H2O / 5% D2O\"\n\n# Get component list\nsample(spec, \"sample\", \"components\")\n# [Dict(\"name\" => \"HEWL\", \"concentration\" => 0.69, \"unit\" => \"mM\", ...)]\n\n# Get component names (key mapped over array elements)\nsample(spec, :sample, :components, :name)\n# [\"HEWL\", \"gadodiamide\"]\n\nKeys can be strings or symbols and are case-insensitive. If a key is not found at any level, nothing is returned.\n\nWhen a key is applied to an array (such as components), it is mapped over each element and a filtered array of results is returned.\n\nFor isotopic_labelling and solvent, if the stored value is \"custom\", the corresponding custom_labelling or custom_solvent field is returned automatically:\n\n# Returns \"custom_labelling\" value if isotopic_labelling == \"custom\"\nsample(spec, :sample, :components, :isotopic_labelling)\n# [\"2H,13C-Met\", \"unlabelled\"]\n\n# Returns \"custom_solvent\" value if solvent == \"custom\"\nsample(spec, :buffer, :solvent)\n# \"95% H2O / 5% D2O + glycerol\"","title":"Accessing sample metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/samples/#Schema","page":"Sample metadata","text":"For the complete schema specification, including all available fields and their meanings, see nmrsamples.github.io.","title":"Schema"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/samples/#Scanning-directories","page":"Sample metadata","text":"For workflows that process many experiments at once, use the scanning API to avoid loading full binary data:\n\n# Scan all experiments in a directory (metadata only, no binary data loaded)\nexpts = scanexperiments(\"/nmr/projects/lysozyme/\")\n\n# Check sample association\nhassample(expts[1])\n# true\n\nsample(expts[1], \"sample\", \"label\")\n# \"lysozyme\"\n\n# Scan all sample files in the same directory (sample JSONs live alongside experiment folders)\nsamples = scansamples(\"/nmr/projects/lysozyme/\")\n\n# Find the sample matching a specific experiment\ns = findsample(expts[1])\ns = findsample(expts[1], samples)   # faster — uses pre-scanned list\n\n# Find all experiments for a given sample\ngroup = findexperiments(samples[1], expts)","title":"Scanning directories"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Working-with-NMR-data","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"NMR measurements are arrays of data, with additional numerical data associated with each dimension, or axis. Within NMRTools, these data are stored as NMRData structures, which encapsulate the data, axis information, and metadata on acquisition or processing.","title":"Working with NMR data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Loading-NMR-data","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"NMR data are loaded using the loadnmr function. This handles processed Bruker experiments and NMRPipe-format data.\n\n# load bruker experiment number 1 from a directory '2D_HN'\n# by default, NMRTools will load bruker processed data from proc 1\nspec2d = loadnmr(\"exampledata/2D_HN/1\")\n\n# load a different processed spectrum\nspec1d = loadnmr(\"exampledata/2D_HN/1/pdata/101\")\n\n# load data from NMRPipe format, using a template\nspec3d = loadnmr(\"exampledata/pseudo3D_HN_R2/1/ft/test%03d.ft2\")\n\ntip: Tip\nloadnmr will attempt to locate and parse acquisition metadata, such as acqus files. If the spectrum file is located elsewhere (for example, if you are loading a file that was processed with NMRPipe), then you can specify the path to the experiment folder using the experimentfolder keyword argument.\n\nWhen spectra are loaded, a simple algorithm runs to estimate the noise level, which is often used for subsequent plotting commands.","title":"Loading NMR data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Manipulating-spectrum-data","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"NMRData structures encapsulate a standard Julia array. This can be accessed using the data command. Through multiple dispatch, most operations work directly on NMRData variables as if they are regular arrays, with the added benefit that axis information and metadata are preserved. Data can be sliced and accessed like a regular array using the usual square brackets:\n\nspec1d[100:105]\nspec2d[3:4, 10:14]\n\nValue-based selectors can also be used to locate data using chemical shifts. Three selectors are defined:\n\nAt(x): select data precisely at the specified value\nNear(x): select data at the nearest matching position\nx .. y: select the range of data between x and y (closed interval)\n\nFor example:\n\nspec1d[8.2 .. 8.3] # select between 8.2 and 8.3 ppm\nspec2d[Near(8.25), 123 .. 124] # select near 8.25 ppm in the first dimension\n                               # and between 123 and 124 ppm in the second dimension\n\nWhen data are sliced, new NMRData structures are created and their axes are updated to match the new data size.\n\nwarning: Warning\nWhen NMRData structures are sliced, copied, or otherwise modified, they inherit the same dictionary of metadata as the original variable. This means that any changes to metadata will affect both variables. To resolve this, make a deepcopy of the variable. Note also that any acquisition metadata might not reflect the correct shape of the data any more.","title":"Manipulating spectrum data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Accessing-multicomplex-(real/imaginary)-data","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"NMR data can be loaded with all quadrature components using the allcomponents=true keyword:\n\nspec1d = loadnmr(\"expt/110\", allcomponents=true)\nspec2d = loadnmr(\"expt/111\", allcomponents=true)\n\nComplex multidimensional data is represented using MulticomplexNumbers.jl. This algebra provides a natural representation of the multiple real and imaginary components associated with NMR data (Delsuc 1988), and facilitates phase correction and bookkeeping of individual real and imaginary components.\n\nA 2D point has four components, rr + ir·im1 + ri·im2 + ii·im1·im2, where im1 belongs to the direct dimension and im2 to the indirect one. They can be extracted with component, numbered in that order; realest(spec2d) gives the purely real part rr:\n\nfig = Figure(size=(600, 600))\nnmrplot(fig[1,1:2], [real(spec1d), imag(spec1d)])  # 1D plot\nspec2d = spec2d[-0.5 .. 1.2, 81 .. 90] / 2\nnmrplot(fig[2,1], component.(spec2d, 1), title=\"component 1 (rr)\")  # 2D plots\nnmrplot(fig[2,2], component.(spec2d, 2), title=\"component 2 (ir)\")  # imaginary in the direct dimension\nnmrplot(fig[3,1], component.(spec2d, 3), title=\"component 3 (ri)\")  # imaginary in the indirect dimension\nnmrplot(fig[3,2], component.(spec2d, 4), title=\"component 4 (ii)\")\n\nImaginary components are the complex conjugates of TopSpin's (2ir, 2ri, …), following the NMRTools sign convention.\n\n(Image: Multicomplex data)","title":"Accessing multicomplex (real/imaginary) data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Accessing-axis-data","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"Information on data dimensions is stored in NMRDimension structures. These can be accessed with the dims function:\n\n# get the first dimension of this two-dimensional experiment\nusing NMRTools # hide\nspec2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN\"); # hide\ndims(spec2d, 1)\n\nNMRDimensions can be treated like vectors (one-dimensional arrays) for most purposes, including indexing and slicing. Value-based selectors can also be used, as for spectrum data. Like spectrum data, the underlying numerical data can be accessed if needed using the data function.\n\nA hierarchy of types is defined for NMR dimensions, reflecting the variety of different experiments:\n\nNMRDimension\nFrequencyDimension: with specific types F1Dim to F4Dim\nNonFrequencyDimension\nTimeDimension:\nTrelaxDim: for relaxation times\nTkinDim: for kinetic evolution times\nT1Dim to T4Dim: for general frequency evolution periods\nGradientDimension: for e.g. diffusion measurements, with specific types G1Dim to G4Dim\nUnknownDimension: with specific types X1Dim to X4Dim","title":"Accessing axis data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Chemical-shift-referencing","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"The reference function corrects the referencing of a spectrum by specifying a known chemical shift: you provide an old => new pair indicating where a reference peak currently appears and where it should appear.\n\nusing NMRTools, Plots #hide\n\n# Load example data\nspec2d_original = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\n\n# Reference the 1H dimension: peak at 4.72 ppm should be at 4.70 ppm\nspec2d = reference(spec2d_original, F2Dim, 4.72 => 4.70)\n\nlabel!(spec2d_original, \"unreferenced\")\nlabel!(spec2d, \"referenced\")\nplot([spec2d_original, spec2d])\nsavefig(\"plot-offset.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe dimension can be specified as a dimension index (1, 2, ...), a dimension type (F1Dim, F2Dim, ...), or a nucleus (H1, N15, ...).\n\nBy default, reference also applies indirect referencing to the other frequency dimensions using IUPAC Xi ratios, so correcting the ¹H reference of an HSQC corrects its ¹⁵N or ¹³C axis too. For aqueous samples, reference(spec) references to the water signal using the sample temperature. See Chemical Shift Referencing for these options and the Xi ratio tables.","title":"Chemical shift referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Accessing-metadata","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"NMRData objects contain metadata on processing and acquisition parameters that are populated automatically upon loading a spectrum. Entries are divided into spectrum metadata - associated with the experiment in general - and axis metadata, that are associated with a particular dimension of the data.\n\nMetadata entries are labelled by symbols such as :ns or :pulseprogram. Entries can be accessed using the metadata function, or directly as a dictionary-style lookup:\n\nspec2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN\"); # hide\nmetadata(spec2d, :ns)\nspec2d[:title]\n\nAcquisition parameters from Bruker acqus files are also parsed when loading data, and can be accessed using the acqus function. Parameters are specified either as lower-case symbols or strings (not case-sensitive). An index can be specified for arrayed parameters such as pulse lengths or delays.\n\nacqus(spec2d, \"TE\")\nacqus(spec2d, :p, 1)\n\nAxis metadata can be accessed by providing an additional label, which can either be numerical or the appropriate NMRDimension type, such as F1Dim etc:\n\nmetadata(spec2d, F2Dim, :label)\nspec2d[2, :bf]\nspec2d[F2Dim, :window]\n\nSee the Metadata overview for more on metadata, including spectrometer channels, power levels, frequency lists and sample information.","title":"Accessing metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/data/#Saving-data","page":"Working with NMR data","text":"Frequency-domain spectra can be written as Bruker processed data with savenmr, and time-domain data as Bruker fid or ser files with savefid, for example to view a spectrum processed in NMRTools in TopSpin:\n\nsavenmr(\"myexperiment/1\", spec; procno=101)   # writes myexperiment/1/pdata/101\n\nPeak lists are saved with savepeaks (see Peaks and peak lists).","title":"Saving data"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coherences/#Coherences-and-isotope-data","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"NMRTools defines commonly used nuclei and provides a framework for identifying single- and multiple-quantum coherences associated with them. Reference data on their gyromagnetic ratios, and spin quantum numbers, is also defined and accessible through simple functions.","title":"Coherences and isotope data"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coherences/#Nuclei","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"","title":"Nuclei"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coherences/#Coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"","title":"Coherences"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coherences/#Reference-data","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"","title":"Reference data"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Nucleus-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"Nucleus\n\nEnumeration of common nuclei associated with biomolecular NMR. Nuclei are named e.g. H1, C13.\n\nDefined nuclei: H1, H2, C12, C13, N14, N15, F19, P31.\n\nSee also spin, gyromagneticratio, Coherence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Nucleus"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Coherence-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"Coherence\n\nAbstract supertype for representing coherences.\n\nSee also SQ, MQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Coherence"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.SQ-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"SQ(nucleus::Nucleus, label==\"\")\n\nRepresentation of a single quantum coherence on a given nucleus.\n\nSee also Nucleus, MQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.SQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.MQ-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"MQ(coherences, label==\"\")\n\nRepresentation of a multiple-quantum coherence. Coherences are specified as a tuple of tuples, of the form (nucleus, coherenceorder)\n\nExamples\n\njulia> MQ(((H1,1), (C13,-1)), \"ZQ\")\nMQ(((H1, 1), (C13, -1)), \"ZQ\")\n\njulia> MQ(((H1,3), (C13,1)), \"QQ\")\nMQ(((H1, 3), (C13, 1)), \"QQ\")\n\nSee also Nucleus, SQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.MQ"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.coherenceorder-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"coherenceorder(coherence)\n\nCalculate the total coherence order.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> coherenceorder(SQ(H1))\n1\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,1),(C13,1))))\n2\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,1),(C13,-1))))\n0\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,3),(C13,1))))\n4\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,0),)))\n0\n\nSee also Nucleus, SQ, MQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.coherenceorder"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.spin-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"spin(n::Nucleus)\n\nReturn the spin quantum number of nucleus n, or nothing if not defined.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> spin(H1)\n1//2\n\nSee also Coherence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.spin"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.gyromagneticratio-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"gyromagneticratio(n::Nucleus)\ngyromagneticratio(c::Coherence)\n\nReturn the gyromagnetic ratio in Hz/T of a nucleus, or calculate the effective gyromagnetic ratio of a coherence. This is equal to the product of the individual gyromagnetic ratios with their coherence orders.\n\nReturns nothing if not defined.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(H1)\n2.6752218744e8\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(SQ(H1))\n2.6752218744e8\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(MQ(((H1,1),(C13,1))))\n3.3480498744e8\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(MQ(((H1,0),)))\n0.0\n\nSee also Nucleus, Coherence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.gyromagneticratio"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/coherences/#NMRTools.NMRBase.xi_ratio-reference-coherences","page":"Coherences and isotope data","text":"xi_ratio(n::Nucleus; aqueous=true)\n\nReturn the Xi ratio for nucleus n, used for indirect chemical shift referencing.\n\nThe Xi ratio is defined as the ratio of the reference frequency of nucleus X to the 1H reference frequency (TMS or DSS). This enables indirect referencing of heteronuclear dimensions from a single 1H reference.\n\nArguments\n\nn::Nucleus: The nucleus for which to retrieve the Xi ratio\naqueous=true: If true, use DSS-based ratios (aqueous solutions).                 If false, use TMS-based ratios (organic solvents).\n\nReturns\n\nThe Xi ratio as a Float64, or nothing if not defined for this nucleus.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> xi_ratio(C13)\n0.25144953\n\njulia> xi_ratio(C13; aqueous=true)\n0.25144953\n\njulia> xi_ratio(C13; aqueous=false)\n0.2514502\n\njulia> xi_ratio(N15)\n0.101329118\n\nReferences\n\nMarkley et al., 1998, Pure Appl. Chem. 70, 117-142\nWishart et al., 1995, J. Biomol. NMR 6, 135-140\nMaurer and Kalbitzer, 1996, J. Magn. Reson. B113, 177-178\nIUPAC recommendations 2001/2008\n\nSee also reference, Nucleus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.xi_ratio"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/fqlists/#Frequency-lists","page":"Frequency lists","text":"Frequency lists are used in NMR experiments to specify arrays of frequencies for selective excitation, CEST, or other variable-frequency experiments. On Bruker spectrometers, these are stored in files like fq1list through fq8list.\n\nNMRTools represents frequency lists as FQList structures that preserve encoding information (Hz vs ppm, relative vs absolute) and provide safe conversion functions.","title":"Frequency lists"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/fqlists/#Accessing-frequency-lists","page":"Frequency lists","text":"Frequency lists are automatically imported when loading data:\n\n# Load CEST experiment\nspec = loadnmr(\"path/to/cest/experiment\")\n\n# Get frequency list\nfqlist = acqus(spec, :fq1list)","title":"Accessing frequency lists"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/fqlists/#Extracting-frequency-values","page":"Frequency lists","text":"Use ppm() and hz() to safely extract frequency values. These functions handle all unit conversions automatically.","title":"Extracting frequency values"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/fqlists/#Get-chemical-shifts-(in-ppm)","page":"Frequency lists","text":"dim = dims(spec, F2Dim)\nppm_values = ppm(fqlist, dim)","title":"Get chemical shifts (in ppm)"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/fqlists/#Get-offsets-from-carrier-(in-Hz)","page":"Frequency lists","text":"dim = dims(spec, F2Dim)\noffset_hz = hz(fqlist, dim)\n\ntip: Tip\nAlways use ppm() and hz() rather than accessing raw values directly. These functions handle all encoding schemes correctly.","title":"Get offsets from carrier (in Hz)"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/fqlists/#Creating-frequency-lists","page":"Frequency lists","text":"Construct FQList objects programmatically:\n\nusing NMRTools\n\n# Frequency list in Hz, relative to carrier\nfq_rel = FQList([100.0, 200.0, 300.0], :Hz, true)\n\n# Frequency list in ppm, absolute (chemical shifts)\nfq_abs = FQList([7.5, 8.0, 8.5], :ppm, false)","title":"Creating frequency lists"},{"category":"section","location":"indexes/#Index","page":"Index","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"indexes/#NMRBase","page":"Index","text":"Modules = [NMRTools.NMRBase]","title":"NMRBase"},{"category":"section","location":"indexes/#NMRIO","page":"Index","text":"Modules = [NMRTools.NMRIO]","title":"NMRIO"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Plotting-with-Plots","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"NMRTools contains recipes for plotting common types of spectra, using the Plots package.\n\nFor Makie-based plotting with nmrplot and nmrplot!, see Plotting with Makie.","title":"Plotting with Plots"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Plotting-a-1D-spectrum","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Load and plot an example 1D ¹⁹F spectrum using the loadnmr and plot commands:\n\nusing NMRTools, Plots\nspec = exampledata(\"1D_19F\")\nplot(spec)\nsavefig(\"plot-1D.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nBy default, plots are titled using the label generated when the data are loaded, which in turn comes from the first line of the title file. Titles can be removed by specifying title=\"\" in the plot command (and the title can be changed in the same manner).\n\nThe plot colour can be modified by specifying e.g. c=:black in the plot command.\n\nplot(spec, title=\"\", c=:black)\nsavefig(\"plot-1D-black.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plotting a 1D spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Zooming-in-/-setting-plot-limits","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"The plot range can be set using the usual xlims argument or command, e.g. passing xlims=[-124,-122] as an option to the plot command.\n\nAlternatively, the region of interest can be selected before plotting using the NMRTools .. selector:\n\nplot(spec[-124 .. -123])\nsavefig(\"plot-1D-zoom.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nThere are two advantages of this approach. If xlims are set, the y axis will be scaled to fit the entire spectrum, including regions that are not actually displayed — this may not show your data at its best. If the data are selected before plotting, the y axis is scaled according only to the selected region. For large spectra, pre-selecting a subset also reduces figure sizes.","title":"Zooming in / setting plot limits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Overlaying-multiple-1D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Multiple experiments can be loaded from a list of filenames using the map function.\n\n# create a list of bruker experiment directories\nfilenames = [\"my_experiment/1\",\n             \"my_experiment/2\",\n             \"my_experiment/3\",\n             \"my_experiment/4\",\n             \"my_experiment/5\",\n             \"my_experiment/6\",\n             \"my_experiment/7\",\n             \"my_experiment/8\",\n             \"my_experiment/9\",\n             \"my_experiment/10\",\n             \"my_experiment/11\"]\nspectra = map(loadnmr, filenames)\n\nThis creates a vector of NMRData objects containing the individual spectra. To plot this series, pass the list to the plot function:\n\nspectra = exampledata(\"1D_19F_titration\"); # hide\nplot(spectra, xlims=(-125, -122))\nsavefig(\"plot-19F-titration.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nnote: Note\nBy default, spectra are normalized according to the number of scans and receiver gain determined automatically from the spectrum metadata; this can be disabled with the normalize=false option\n\ntip: Tip\nLegends are produced from the first line of the spectrum title file. The legend can be disabled using the legend=nothing option. To re-label a spectrum, use label!(spectrum, \"new label\") (or for a list of experiments, the i-th spectrum can be relabelled with label!(spectra[i], \"new label\")).\n\nStacked views can also be produced using the vstack=true option. By default, spectra are normalized according to the number of scans and receiver gain determined automatically from the spectrum metadata\n\nplot(spectra, xlims=(-125, -122), legend=:topright, vstack=true)\nsavefig(\"plot-19F-stack.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe spacing of stacked views can be adjusted by passing a scaling factor to the vstack=X option. By default, vstack=true uses a scaling factor of one. Use a larger number to increase the separation between spectra.\n\nplot(spectra, xlims=(-125, -122), legend=:topright, vstack=5)\nsavefig(\"plot-19F-stack-space.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Overlaying multiple 1D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Plotting-2D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"2D spectra can be loaded and plotted in the same way as for 1D experiments:\n\nspec2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\nplot(spec2d)\nxlims!(6, 9.5)\nsavefig(\"plot-2D.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nAs for 1Ds, plots are titled using the label generated when the data are loaded. Positive and negative contour levels are generated starting from five times the noise level.\n\nContour levels can be adjusted by multiplying or dividing the spectrum by a scaling factor — the noise level stored within the spectrum metadata is not updated, so the contour levels will change accordingly.\n\nThe plot colour can also be modified by specifying e.g. c=:purple in the plot command. By default, the requested colour will be used to generate a lighter shade for negative contours. Negative contours can be turned off with negcontours=false:\n\nplot(spec2d / 3, c=:purple, negcontours=false)\nxlims!(6, 9.5); nothing # hide\nsavefig(\"plot-2D-purple.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nNegative contour colours can be specified separately with negcolor:\n\nplot(spec2d, c=:black, negcolor=:red)\nxlims!(6, 9.5); nothing # hide\nsavefig(\"plot-2D-blackred.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nSpectra can also be plotted in other formats, e.g. heatmaps:\n\nheatmap(spec2d[8 .. 8.5, 120 .. 125] / spec2d[:noise], cbar=:right, cbtitle=\"SNR\")\nsavefig(\"plot-2D-heatmap.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plotting 2D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Overlaying-multiple-2D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Multiple 2D experiments can be loaded from a list of filenames using the map function.\n\nfilenames = [\"titration_experiment/1\",\n             \"titration_experiment/2\",\n             \"titration_experiment/3\",\n             \"titration_experiment/4\",\n             \"titration_experiment/5\",\n             \"titration_experiment/6\",\n             \"titration_experiment/7\",\n             \"titration_experiment/8\",\n             \"titration_experiment/9\",\n             \"titration_experiment/10\",\n             \"titration_experiment/11\"]\nspectra2d = map(loadnmr, filenames)\n\nThese can be plotted by passing the list of spectra to the plot function, with automatic normalisation for varying numbers of scans and receiver gain:\n\nspectra2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN_titration\"); # hide\nplot(spectra2d / 2, legend=:topleft, xlims=(8.1,8.6), ylims=(119.5,124.5))\nsavefig(\"plot-2D-titration.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nA gradient of colours will automatically be generated when spectra are plotted in this way, and a legend generated from spectrum labels.\n\nA list of colours can be passed for plotting. This will cycle if there are more spectra than colours in the list.\n\nplot(spectra2d[[1,11]], c=[:blue, :red], xlims=(8.1,8.6), ylims=(119.5,124.5))\nsavefig(\"plot-2D-lims.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Overlaying multiple 2D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Normalization","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Normalization controls how spectra are scaled when plotted, ensuring fair comparisons between experiments acquired with different parameters. Normalization primarily makes a difference when plotting multiple spectra together. For single spectra, the normalization factor only affects the absolute y-axis values, not the appearance of the plot.\n\nFor 1D spectra, the normalize keyword argument controls automatic scaling:\n\nplot(..., normalize=true) (default): Spectra are normalized according to the number of scans and receiver gain, calculated internally via the scale command. If concentration metadata is available, it will also be included in the normalization.\nplot(..., normalize=false): Each spectrum is plotted with its raw intensity values, without any automatic scaling adjustments.\n\nFor 2D spectra, normalization affects both the intensity scaling and the contour level calculation:\n\nplot(..., normalize=true) (default): Spectra are normalized by the scale factor (accounting for number of scans and receiver gain), and contour levels are set to 5× the noise level. When plotting multiple spectra together, contour levels will be set based on 5× the noise level of the first spectrum, ensuring consistent contour spacing across all spectra in the series.\nplot(..., normalize=false): Each spectrum is plotted individually with contour levels relative to its own noise level (5× noise). This can result in different contour spacing between spectra in a series.\nplot(..., normalize=reference_spectrum): All spectra are scaled to match the reference spectrum. Contour levels are set based on the reference spectrum's noise level. The reference spectrum does not need to be included in the plot itself, which can be useful for creating animations with consistent scaling across frames.\n\ntip: Tip\nIt is usually easiest to plot a series of 2D spectra by passing a list of spectra in a single plot call, rather than adding them to a plot one-by-one using the plot! command. Alternatively, a reference spectrum should be specified to ensure consistent normalization.\n\nExample: Using a reference spectrum for normalization\n\n# Load a series of 2D spectra\nspectra2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN_titration\")\n\n# Plot spectra 2-5, but normalize to spectrum 1\nplot(spectra2d[2:5], normalize=spectra2d[1])","title":"Normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Plotting-pseudo-2D-data","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Plot recipes are available for pseudo-2D data like diffusion, relaxation or kinetics.\n\n# load a diffusion measurement, processed in topspin using xf2\ndiffusiondata = exampledata(\"pseudo2D_XSTE\")\n\n# set the gradient strengths - which varied from 2% to 98% of the max, over 10 points\ndiffusiondata = setgradientlist(diffusiondata, LinRange(0.02, 0.98, 10))\n\n# generate a 3D plot of the data\nplot(diffusiondata, xlims=(6,10))\nsavefig(\"plot-diff-3D.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nBy default, the plot command will generate a 3D plot for pseudo-2D experiments. Heatmaps can also be generated using the heatmap command. In this example, we have selected the range to plot directly, rather than using the xlims option.\n\nheatmap(diffusiondata[7..9,:])\nsavefig(\"plot-diff-heatmap.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plotting pseudo-2D data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Colours","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Colours can be specified using named colour symbols from the Colors.jl library, which is included with Plots.jl. The following named colours are commonly used:\n\ncolours = [:black, :red, :dodgerblue, :limegreen, :lightgrey,:orange, :hotpink, :purple]\nps = [plot(spec[-124.5 .. -123.5], c=c, title=string(c), titlefontsize=9,\n           legend=false, xaxis=false, yaxis=false, ticks=nothing, border=:none)\n      for c in colours]\nplot(ps..., layout=(2, 4), size=(600, 200))\nsavefig(\"plot-colours.svg\"); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nAny valid Plots.jl colour specification can be used, including:\n\nNamed symbols: :black, :red, :dodgerblue, :limegreen, :lightgrey, :orange, :hotpink, :purple and hundreds more\nRGB values: RGB(0.2, 0.4, 0.8)\nHex strings: \"#4A8BC4\"\n\nFor 2D spectra, the c argument sets the colour of the positive contours. Negative contours default to a lighter shade of the same colour, but can be set independently with negcolor:\n\nplot(spec2d, c=:navy, negcolor=:red)","title":"Colours"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Saving-plots","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"All plots can be saved as high quality vector graphics, or as PNG files, using the savefig command.\n\nsavefig(\"myspectrum.pdf\")\nsavefig(\"myspectrum.svg\")\nsavefig(\"myspectrum.png\")","title":"Saving plots"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#Plot-options-reference","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"","title":"Plot options reference"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#1D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Keyword Type Default Description\ntitle String from spectrum label Plot title. Pass \"\" to suppress.\nc / color Color auto Line colour.\nnormalize Bool true Scale by number of scans and receiver gain.\nvstack Bool or Number false Stack spectra vertically. Pass a number to adjust spacing.\nxlims Tuple auto Chemical shift range to display (ppm).\nlegend Symbol auto Legend position (e.g. :topright). Pass nothing to suppress.","title":"1D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/plotting/#2D-spectra","page":"Plotting with Plots","text":"Keyword Type Default Description\ntitle String from spectrum label Plot title. Pass \"\" to suppress.\nc / color Color auto Positive contour colour.\nnegcontours Bool true Show negative contours.\nnegcolor Color lighter shade of c Negative contour colour.\nnormalize Bool or NMRData true Scale by scans/gain, or normalize relative to a reference spectrum.\nxlims Tuple auto Chemical shift range in the direct dimension (ppm).\nylims Tuple auto Chemical shift range in the indirect dimension (ppm).\nlegend Symbol auto Legend position. Pass nothing to suppress.","title":"2D spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#NMRTools","page":"API","text":"","title":"NMRTools"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#NMRBase","page":"API","text":"","title":"NMRBase"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#NMRIO","page":"API","text":"","title":"NMRIO"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#SchemaMigrate","page":"API","text":"","title":"SchemaMigrate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.nmrplot","page":"API","text":"nmrplot(spec; kwargs...)\nnmrplot(gridpos, spec; kwargs...)\nnmrplot!(ax, spec; kwargs...)\n\nPlot an NMR spectrum with conventions appropriate for NMR data (reversed chemical-shift axes, hidden y-decorations on 1D, ±contours for 2D).\n\nRequires Makie (or a Makie backend such as GLMakie, CairoMakie, WGLMakie) to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.nmrplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.nmrplot!","page":"API","text":"nmrplot!(ax, spec; kwargs...)\n\nMutating variant of nmrplot — plots into an existing Axis, overriding axis orientation/labels to keep NMR conventions intact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.nmrplot!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._EDGEFLOOR","page":"API","text":"systematic phase uncertainty assumed for an edge observation (degrees)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._EDGEFLOOR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.AbstractNMRData","page":"API","text":"AbstractNMRData <: DimensionalData.AbstractDimArray\n\nAbstract supertype for objects that wrap an array of NMR data, and metadata about its contents.\n\nAbstractNMRDatas inherit from AbstractDimArray from DimensionalData.jl. They can be indexed as regular Julia arrays or with DimensionalData.jl Dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.AbstractNMRData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ChemicalShift","page":"API","text":"ChemicalShift\n\nOne assigned chemical shift: an atom of a residue, its nucleus, and its shift.\n\nfield meaning\nentity which molecule in the assembly (BMRB Entity_assembly_ID)\nresidue residue number (BMRB Seq_ID)\nresiduename residue type, e.g. \"LEU\"\natom IUPAC atom name, e.g. \"HD11\"\nnucleus e.g. H1, C13\nshift chemical shift (ppm)\nerror uncertainty (ppm), NaN if not reported\n\nA shift list is a Vector{ChemicalShift}, read with loadbmrb and turned into the peaks expected in a given experiment with peaklist. Use filter to select residues before building peaks, e.g. filter(s -> s.residuename in (\"ILE\", \"LEU\", \"VAL\"), shifts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ChemicalShift"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Coherence","page":"API","text":"Coherence\n\nAbstract supertype for representing coherences.\n\nSee also SQ, MQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Coherence"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ComplexLineshape","page":"API","text":"ComplexLineshape\n\nReturn a complex-valued lineshape when used in calculations\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ComplexLineshape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Cos2Window","page":"API","text":"Cos2Window(tmax=Inf)\n\nApodization by a cosine-squared function, cos²(πt / 2tmax), falling from 1 at t = 0 to 0 at tmax. When tmax is left at Inf, apodize sets it to the acquisition time. Equivalent to TopSpin's QSINE with SSB = 2.\n\nCos²Window is an alias, kept for compatibility.\n\nSee also CosWindow, SineWindow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Cos2Window"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.CosWindow","page":"API","text":"CosWindow(tmax)\n\nApodization by a pure cosine function. Acquisition time is tmax.\n\nSee also Cos2Window, SineWindow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.CosWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Cos²Window","page":"API","text":"Alias of Cos2Window, kept for compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Cos²Window"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ExponentialWindow","page":"API","text":"ExponentialWindow(lb, tmax)\n\nExponential window function, with a line broadening of lb Hz. Acquisition time is tmax.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ExponentialWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.FieldDimension","page":"API","text":"FieldDimension <: NonFrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for RF field strength dimensions used in NMRData objects. Used for spinlock power in R1rho dispersion experiments. Concrete type SpinlockDim is generated for use in creating objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.FieldDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.FrequencyDimension","page":"API","text":"FrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for frequency dimensions used in NMRData objects. Concrete types F1Dim, F2Dim, F3Dim and F4Dim are generated for use in creating objects.\n\nSee also NonFrequencyDimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.FrequencyDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.GaussWindow","page":"API","text":"GaussWindow(expHz, gaussHz, center, tmax)\n\nAbstract representation of Lorentz-to-Gauss window functions, applying an inverse exponential of expHz Hz, and a gaussian broadening of gaussHz Hz, with maximum at center (between 0 and 1). Acquisition time is tmax.\n\nSpecialises to LorentzToGaussWindow when center is zero, otherwise GeneralGaussWindow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.GaussWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.GradientDimension","page":"API","text":"GradientDimension <: NonFrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for gradient-encoded dimensions used in NMRData objects. Concrete types G1Dim, G2Dim, G3Dim and G4Dim are generated for use in creating objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.GradientDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.HasNonFrequencyDimension","page":"API","text":"@traitdef HasNonFrequencyDimension{D}\n\nA trait indicating whether the data object has a non-frequency domain dimension.\n\nExample\n\n@traitfn f(x::X) where {X; HasNonFrequencyDimension{X}} = \"This spectrum has a non-frequency domain dimension!\"\n@traitfn f(x::X) where {X; SimpleTraits.Not{HasNonFrequencyDimension{X}}} = \"This is a pure frequency-domain spectrum!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.HasNonFrequencyDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.LineshapeComplexity","page":"API","text":"LineshapeComplexity\n\nAbstract type to specify calculation of RealLineshape or ComplexLineshape in function calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.LineshapeComplexity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.MQ","page":"API","text":"MQ(coherences, label==\"\")\n\nRepresentation of a multiple-quantum coherence. Coherences are specified as a tuple of tuples, of the form (nucleus, coherenceorder)\n\nExamples\n\njulia> MQ(((H1,1), (C13,-1)), \"ZQ\")\nMQ(((H1, 1), (C13, -1)), \"ZQ\")\n\njulia> MQ(((H1,3), (C13,1)), \"QQ\")\nMQ(((H1, 3), (C13, 1)), \"QQ\")\n\nSee also Nucleus, SQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.MQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRData","page":"API","text":"NMRData <: AbstractNMRData\nNMRData(A::AbstractArray{T,N}, dims; kw...)\nNMRData(A::AbstractNMRData; kw...)\n\nA generic AbstractNMRData for NMR array data. It holds memory-backed arrays.\n\nKeywords\n\ndims: Tuple of NMRDimensions for the array.\nname: Symbol name for the array, which will also retreive named layers if NMRData   is used on a multi-layered file like a NetCDF.\nmissingval: value representing missing data, normally detected from the file. Set manually   when you know the value is not specified or is incorrect. This will not change any   values in the NMRData, it simply assigns which value is treated as missing.\n\nInternal Keywords\n\nIn some cases it is possible to set these keywords as well.\n\ndata: can replace the data in an AbstractNMRData\nrefdims: Tuple of position Dimensions the array was sliced from, defaulting to ().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRDimension","page":"API","text":"NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for all axes used in NMRData objects.\n\nSee also FrequencyDimension and NonFrequencyDimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRExperiment","page":"API","text":"NMRExperiment\n\nA lightweight, metadata-only descriptor for a Bruker NMR experiment folder. Does not load any binary data. Upgrade to a full NMRData via loadnmr.\n\nSee also: scanexperiments, findsample, loadnmr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRExperiment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRSample","page":"API","text":"NMRSample\n\nA lightweight wrapper around an NMR sample JSON file. Holds the file path and parsed metadata. Access fields via sample.\n\nSee also: scansamples, findsample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRSample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRToolsError","page":"API","text":"NMRToolsError(message)\n\nAn error arising in NMRTools.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRToolsError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NonFrequencyDimension","page":"API","text":"NonFrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for non-frequency dimensions used in NMRData objects. Sub-types include TimeDimension, GradientDimension,  and UnknownDimension.\n\nSee also FrequencyDimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NonFrequencyDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Nucleus","page":"API","text":"Nucleus\n\nEnumeration of common nuclei associated with biomolecular NMR. Nuclei are named e.g. H1, C13.\n\nDefined nuclei: H1, H2, C12, C13, N14, N15, F19, P31.\n\nSee also spin, gyromagneticratio, Coherence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Nucleus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NullWindow","page":"API","text":"NullWindow(tmax)\n\nNo apodization applied. Acquisition time is tmax.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NullWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.OffsetDimension","page":"API","text":"OffsetDimension <: NonFrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for frequency offset dimensions used in NMRData objects. Used for CEST saturation offsets, R1rho off-resonance offsets, etc. Concrete type OffsetDim is generated for use in creating objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.OffsetDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Peak","page":"API","text":"Peak{N}\n\nA peak in an N-dimensional frequency-domain spectrum: where it is, how big it is, how broad it is, and what it is called.\n\nA peak records what was picked, not what a fit produced. Positions are in ppm and linewidths are full widths at half maximum in Hz, both ordered like the dimensions of the spectrum the peak came from, so for a 2D spectrum the first coordinate is the direct dimension (x) and the second the indirect dimension (y). Linewidths are kept in Hz because, unlike ppm, they mean the same thing on every nucleus and at every field; the corresponding relaxation rate is R2 = π * linewidth.\n\nUnknown quantities are NaN: a peak read from a Sparky list has a position and label but no intensity or linewidth.\n\nA peak list is simply a Vector{Peak{N}}, so sort, filter, push!, deleteat! and indexing all work as usual. Peak lists are also Tables.jl tables, so DataFrame(pks) works directly.\n\nExamples\n\np = Peak(8.21, 120.4; label=\"G10\")      # a 2D peak with only a position and label\nppm(p)                                    # (8.21, 120.4)\np = Peak(p; label=\"G10N\")                 # a copy with a new label\n\npks = detectpeaks(spec)                   # Vector{Peak{2}}\nsort(pks; by=intensity, rev=true)\n\nSee also detectpeaks, findpeak, findnearest, ppm, intensity, linewidth, loadpeaks and savepeaks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Peak"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Peak-Tuple{Peak}","page":"API","text":"Peak(p::Peak; position, intensity, linewidth, label)\n\nA copy of p with any of its fields replaced, e.g. Peak(p; label=\"G10\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Peak"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Pole","page":"API","text":"Pole\n\nOne damped complex exponential (a Lorentzian line) from a harmonic-inversion model of a spectrum, as returned by estimatepoles.\n\nFields\n\nshift: chemical shift (ppm)\nlinewidth: full width at half height (Hz)\namplitude: complex amplitude. abs(amplitude) is the line integral (spectrum intensity × points) and angle(amplitude) its phase, which is zero for a positive absorption line\nheight: absorption peak height (spectrum intensity units)\nsnr: height divided by the noise level\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Pole"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.Power","page":"API","text":"Power{T}\n\nA type to represent NMR pulse powers that can be described either in Watts (W) or as dB attenuation (dB). Internally stores the dB value as type T.\n\nConstructors\n\nPower(value, :dB) - Create from dB attenuation value\nPower(value, :W) - Create from Watts value\n\nGetters\n\ndb(p::Power) - Get dB attenuation value\nwatts(p::Power) - Get Watts value\n\nExamples\n\n# Create power from dB attenuation\np1 = Power(30.0, :dB)\ndb(p1)    # 30.0\nwatts(p1) # 0.001\n\n# Create power from Watts\np2 = Power(0.5, :W)\nwatts(p2) # 0.5\ndb(p2)    # ≈ 3.01\n\n# Works with integers and other numeric types\np3 = Power(10, :W)\ndb(p3)    # -10.0\n\n# Zero watts is represented as 120 dB attenuation\np4 = Power(0.0, :W)\ndb(p4)    # 120.0\n\nConversion\n\nConverting W to dB: -10 * log10(plW) Converting dB to W: 10^(-dB/10)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.Power"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.RealLineshape","page":"API","text":"RealLineshape\n\nReturn a real-valued lineshape when used in calculations\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.RealLineshape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.SQ","page":"API","text":"SQ(nucleus::Nucleus, label==\"\")\n\nRepresentation of a single quantum coherence on a given nucleus.\n\nSee also Nucleus, MQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.SQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.SineWindow","page":"API","text":"SineWindow(offset, endpoint, power, tmax)\n\nAbstract window function representing multiplication by sine/cosine functions. Acquisition time is tmax.\n\nleftsinleft(\n    picdotmathrmoffset +\n    fracleft(mathrmend - mathrmoffsetright)pi tmathrmtmax\n    right)right^mathrmpower\n\nSpecialises to CosWindow, Cos2Window or GeneralSineWindow.\n\nArguments\n\noffset: initial value is sin(mathrmoffsetcdotpi) (0 to 1)\nendpoint: initial value is sin(mathrmendpointcdotpi) (0 to 1)\npow: sine exponent\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.SineWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.TimeDimension","page":"API","text":"TimeDimension <: NonFrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for time dimensions used in NMRData objects. Concrete types T1Dim, T2Dim, T3Dim and T4Dim are generated for time-domains representing frequency evolution, and TrelaxDim and TkinDim are generated for representing relaxation and real-time kinetics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.TimeDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.UnknownDimension","page":"API","text":"UnknownDimension <: NonFrequencyDimension <: NMRDimension\n\nAbstract supertype for unknown, non-frequency dimensions used in NMRData objects. Concrete types X1Dim, X2Dim, X3Dim and X4Dim are generated for use in creating objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.UnknownDimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.UnknownWindow","page":"API","text":"UnknownWindow(tmax)\n\nUnknown apodization applied. Acquisition time is tmax.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.UnknownWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.WindowFunction","page":"API","text":"WindowFunction\n\nAbstract type to represent apodization functions.\n\nWindow functions are represented by subtypes of the abstract type WindowFunction, each of which contain appropriate parameters to specify the particular function applied. In addition, the acquisition time tmax is also stored (calculated at the point the window function is applied, i.e. after linear prediction but before zero filling).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.WindowFunction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.abs-Tuple{NMRData{var\"#s1683\", N, D, R, A} where {var\"#s1683\"<:Multicomplex, N, D<:Tuple, R<:Tuple, A<:AbstractArray{var\"#s1683\", N}}}","page":"API","text":"abs(spec::NMRData)\n\nReturn the magnitude of each point of a multicomplex spectrum, as Float64 data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.abs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.angle-Tuple{Pole}","page":"API","text":"phase of a pole (radians); zero for a positive absorption line\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.complex-Union{Tuple{NMRData{var\"#s1683\", N, D, R, A} where {var\"#s1683\"<:Multicomplex{T, 1, 2}, N, D<:Tuple, R<:Tuple, A<:AbstractArray{var\"#s1683\", N}}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"API","text":"complex(spec::NMRData)\n\nConvert a first-order multicomplex spectrum (Multicomplex{T,1,2}) to Julia Complex{T}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.complex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.stack-Union{Tuple{Vector{D}}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}} where {T, N, D<:(AbstractNMRData{T, N})}","page":"API","text":"stack(expts::Vector{NMRData})\n\nCombine a collection of equally-sized NMRData into one larger array, by arranging them along a new dimension, of type UnknownDimension.\n\nThrows a DimensionMismatch if data are not of compatible shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.stack"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.metadata","page":"API","text":"metadata(nmrdata, key)\nmetadata(nmrdata, dim, key)\nmetadata(nmrdimension, key)\n\nReturn the metadata for specified key, or nothing if not found. Keys are passed as symbols.\n\nExamples (spectrum metadata)\n\n:ns: number of scans\n:ds: number of dummy scans\n:rg: receiver gain\n:ndim: number of dimensions\n:title: spectrum title (contents of title pdata file)\n:filename: spectrum filename\n:pulseprogram: title of pulse program used for acquisition\n:experimentfolder: path to experiment\n:noise: RMS noise level\n\nExamples (dimension metadata)\n\n:pseudodim: flag indicating non-frequency domain data\n:npoints: final number of (real) data points in dimension (after extraction)\n:td: number of complex points acquired\n:tdzf: number of complex points when FT executed, including LP and ZF\n:bf: base frequency, in Hz\n:sf: carrier frequency, in Hz\n:offsethz: carrier offset from bf, in Hz\n:offsetppm: carrier offset from bf, in ppm\n:swhz: spectrum width, in Hz\n:swppm: spectrum width, in ppm\n:region: extracted region, expressed as a range in points, otherwise missing\n:window: WindowFunction indicating applied apodization\n:referenceoffset: referencing (in ppm) applied to the dimension\n\nSee also estimatenoise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.units","page":"API","text":"units(nmrdata)\nunits(nmrdata, dim)\nunits(nmrdimension)\n\nReturn the physical units associated with an NMRData structure or an NMRDimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.Lookups.units"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.label","page":"API","text":"label(nmrdata)\nlabel(nmrdata, dim)\nlabel(nmrdimension)\n\nReturn a short label associated with an NMRData structure or an NMRDimension. By default, for a spectrum this is obtained from the first line of the title file. For a frequency dimension, this is normally something of the form 1H chemical shift (ppm).\n\nSee also label!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DimensionalData.Dimensions.label-Tuple{Peak}","page":"API","text":"label(peak)\n\nThe label of a peak, e.g. an assignment such as \"G10\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DimensionalData.Dimensions.label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#MulticomplexNumbers.realest-Tuple{NMRData{var\"#s1683\", N, D, R, A} where {var\"#s1683\"<:Multicomplex, N, D<:Tuple, R<:Tuple, A<:AbstractArray{var\"#s1683\", N}}}","page":"API","text":"realest(spec::NMRData)\n\nThe real spectrum: the purely real component of every point of a multicomplex spectrum, real in every dimension, as an NMRData with Float64 elements. This is the final step of a processing pipeline.\n\nreal and imag act elementwise as defined by MulticomplexNumbers: they take the coefficients of the highest-order imaginary unit, so for 2D data real(spec) is still complex in the direct dimension. For 1D data real and realest agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MulticomplexNumbers.realest"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._acqtime-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"acquisition time (s) after t = 0 for a time dimension, used as the default window tmax\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._acqtime"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._amplitudes-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"least-squares complex amplitudes cₖ such that x[n] ≈ Σ cₖ·polesₖ^(n-1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._amplitudes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._apply_indirect_referencing-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_apply_indirect_referencing(spec, referenced_dims; aqueous=:auto)\n\nApply indirect referencing using Xi ratios to all frequency dimensions not in referenced_dims.\n\nThe effective 1H frequency is back-calculated from the referenced dimension(s), and Xi ratios are used to calculate the expected frequencies for other nuclei.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._apply_indirect_referencing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._bestp0-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"optimal zero-order phase (radians, modulo π) for first-order phase p1r\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._bestp0"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._buildfreqdim-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_buildfreqdim(d::TimeDimension, di, n) -> FrequencyDimension\n\nConstruct the FrequencyDimension (F1Dim…F4Dim, chosen by the array position di) produced by Fourier transforming time dimension d to n points. The chemical-shift axis is computed from :bf, :swhz and :offsetppm, decreasing (high ppm first) to match the convention used elsewhere in NMRTools. All time-dimension metadata is carried over, including :mcindex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._buildfreqdim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._buildtimedim-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_buildtimedim(d::FrequencyDimension, di, n) -> TimeDimension\n\nInverse of _buildfreqdim: construct the TimeDimension (T1Dim…T4Dim, chosen by the array position di) obtained by inverse Fourier transforming frequency dimension d to n points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._buildtimedim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._edgeobservations-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"API","text":"_edgeobservations(s, poles, δ, bf, edges, noise) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nPhase observations from the two edges of the complex trace s: for each edge, the region of edges × the spectrum (at least 16 points) at that end, excluding points within four linewidths of a pole. The observation is the phase at which the real part of the region's sum vanishes, with the region's centre as a fraction from the first point and the signal-to-noise ratio of the sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._edgeobservations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._evalmodel-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractRange}","page":"API","text":"evaluate Σ cₖ·polesₖ^(n-1) over the index range ns, iterating the powers\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._evalmodel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._extendtimedim-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"extend a time dimension to n points, continuing the time axis at the same dwell\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._extendtimedim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._fourierdims-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"_fourierdims(spec) -> Vector{Int}\n\nReturn the array-dimension indices of every dimension that carries an :mcindex (i.e. the TimeDimensions / FrequencyDimensions that participate in Fourier transformation). Non-Fourier axes such as TrelaxDim or X2Dim have :mcindex == nothing and are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._fourierdims"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._frequencydims-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"array-dimension indices of the Fourier dimensions that are already in the frequency domain\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._frequencydims"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._groupcentre-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"amplitude-weighted chemical shift of a group of poles\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._groupcentre"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._groupdelaypoints-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"digital-filter group delay (points) still to be removed from a dimension by ft; 0 if none\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._groupdelaypoints"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._groupintegralphases-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"_groupintegralphases(s, groups, δ, bf) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nPhase of each line group measured from the spectrum: the integral of the complex trace s over a window centred on the group, extending four times its largest linewidth beyond its outer lines but no more than halfway to the neighbouring groups, less a straight complex baseline through the means of the window's outer points (which removes the tails of distant lines).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._groupintegralphases"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._halfwrap-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"wrap an angle to (-π/2, π/2]: the residual of a phase taken modulo π\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._halfwrap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._hankelpoles-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Integer}","page":"API","text":"_hankelpoles(x, K; L=0) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nEstimate up to K signal poles zₖ of the complex vector x by ESPRIT: SVD of the Hankel matrix, then the eigenvalues of the shift-invariance operator on the truncated left singular subspace. L (Hankel rows) is capped at _HANKEL_LMAX; the model order K sets the eigenproblem size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._hankelpoles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._hsvdsubtract-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"_hsvdsubtract(x; dt, targethz, bandhz, ncoef) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nFit x with ncoef damped exponentials and subtract those whose frequency lies within bandhz of targethz (both in Hz). Frequency-selective solvent/peak removal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._hsvdsubtract"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lineshape","page":"API","text":"_lineshape(ω, R2, ωaxis, window, complexity)\n\nInternal function to calculate a resonance lineshape with frequency ω and relaxation rate R2, calculated at frequencies ωaxis and with apodization according to the specified window function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lineshape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lowpassestimate-Tuple{AbstractArray, Integer, AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"API","text":"_lowpassestimate(data, di, w, fl) -> Array\n\nLow-pass estimate of data along array dimension di by a moving average with (relative) weights w over a ±fl window, truncated and renormalised at the edges. Real-weighted, so it works directly on multicomplex arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lowpassestimate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lowpasswindow-Tuple{Integer, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"relative weights of a symmetric low-pass window of half-width fl (:boxcar/:sine/:sine²)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lowpasswindow"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lpcoefficients-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Integer, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"_lpcoefficients(x, order, direction) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nLeast-squares LP coefficients a of the given order for complex vector x. For :forward, x[n] ≈ Σⱼ aⱼ x[n-j]; for :backward, x[n] ≈ Σⱼ aⱼ x[n+j]. Solved by QR (D \\ d).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lpcoefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lpextrapolate-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Integer, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"_lpextrapolate(x, a, npred, direction) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nExtend x by npred points by iterating the LP recurrence with coefficients a: :forward appends Σⱼ aⱼ x[end-j+1], :backward prepends Σⱼ aⱼ x[j].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lpextrapolate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lpmatrix-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"_lpmatrix(mat; npred, order, direction, rootfix, pool, nselect) -> Matrix\n\nExtend every column of mat by npred points. With pool (default), one shared set of coefficients is estimated from the nselect highest-energy columns and applied to all columns by recurrence; otherwise each column is fitted independently (matching nmrglue).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lpmatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lppredict-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"_lppredict(x; npred, order, direction, rootfix) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nLinear-prediction extension of x by npred points, extrapolating by recurrence. Forward extension uses the Zhu–Bax backward-fit-and-reflect coefficients (stable); backward extension uses a direct backward fit with root reflection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lppredict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._lprepair-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"_lprepair(x; nrepair, order, rootfix) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nReplace the first nrepair points of x by backward prediction from the remaining points (length unchanged).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._lprepair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._maptransform-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Multicomplex{T, N, C}}, Integer, Integer, Any}} where {T, N, C}","page":"API","text":"_maptransform(A, di, mcindex, matkernel) -> Array{<:Multicomplex}\n\nApply the matrix kernel matkernel — mapping the (L, ncols) complex matrix of imₖ-plane vectors (one per column) to a (L2, ncols) matrix, possibly with L2 ≠ L — to the multicomplex array A along array dimension di, where mcindex is that dimension's imaginary unit. Uses the same zero-copy reinterpretation as _mcfft!; a fresh output array is built so the kernel is free to change the length along di.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._maptransform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._mcfft!-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Multicomplex{T, N, C}}, Integer, Any}} where {T, N, C}","page":"API","text":"_mcfft!(A, unit, di; inverse=false)\n\nIn-place Fourier transform of a multicomplex array A along array dimension di, treating the imaginary unit unit (the dimension's :mcindex) as the complex i. Reinterprets the multicomplex memory as Complex (via MulticomplexNumbers' unsafe reinterpretation, exactly as its FFTW extension does), applies FFTW.fft!/ifft!, then reinterprets back. ifft! includes the 1/N normalisation so that _mcfft!(inverse=true) inverts _mcfft!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._mcfft!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._percolumn-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"wrap a per-column complex-vector kernel f as a matrix kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._percolumn"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._perdim-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_perdim(setting, di)\n\nThe value of a processing setting for array dimension di: setting itself, or, if it is a dictionary, its entry for di keyed by dimension number or dimension type (T1Dim and F1Dim both mean dimension 1). Returns default when a dictionary has no entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._perdim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._phasegroups-Tuple{AbstractVector{Pole}, Real, Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"_phasegroups(poles, bf, coupling, maxwidthratio) -> Vector{Vector{Pole}}\n\nGroup poles (sorted by decreasing shift) for phasing: drop poles broader than maxwidthratio times the SNR-weighted median linewidth that have a narrower pole within their linewidth, then merge neighbours closer than coupling Hz plus their mean linewidth. bf is the spectrometer frequency (Hz).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._phasegroups"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._pivotindex-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"0-based fractional point index at which the dimension axis equals pivot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._pivotindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._polyfromroots-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"coefficients (descending, leading first) of ∏(z − rₖ)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._polyfromroots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._polyroots-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"roots of the monic-normalised polynomial with descending coefficients pc\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._polyroots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._pooledcoeffs-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"API","text":"shared extension coefficients for direction (Zhu–Bax for forward), pooled over columns\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._pooledcoeffs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._pooledfit-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Complex}, Any, Integer, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"stacked least-squares LP coefficients (order m, direction fitdir) from columns sel of mat\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._pooledfit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._rebuilddims-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_rebuilddims(spec, newdata, newdims)\n\nRebuild spec with new data and dimensions, going through the NMRData(A, dims; …) constructor so that DimensionalData's format runs on the new dimensions (a plain rebuild bypasses formatting and leaves freshly-built dimensions as unformatted AutoLookups).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._rebuilddims"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._reference_with_offset-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_reference_with_offset(spec, dim_no, offset; indirect=true, aqueous=:auto)\n\nInternal function to apply a reference offset and optionally indirect referencing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._reference_with_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._referencetrace-Tuple{NMRData, Integer}","page":"API","text":"_referencetrace(spec, di) -> Vector{ComplexF64}\n\nComplex trace along array dimension di of spec, in the imaginary unit given by that dimension's :mcindex. Other imaginary units take their real component. Other dimensions are reduced so that every signal contributes: a time dimension contributes its first point, a frequency dimension is summed over the points that carry signal (see _signalindices), and any other dimension is summed.\n\nA frequency dimension is not summed over all its points: that sum is its first time point, which is dominated by the solvent and baseline rather than the cross peaks, and it adds the noise of every point while signal comes from only a few.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._referencetrace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._reflectroots-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"_reflectroots(a, direction) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nStabilise LP coefficients a by reflecting the roots of the prediction polynomial across the unit circle: for :forward extension, roots with |z|>1 (growing) are mapped to 1/conj(z) (inside); for :backward, roots with |z|<1 are pushed outside. Coefficients are rebuilt from the reflected roots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._reflectroots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._removegroupdelay!-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Multicomplex}, Integer, Any, Real}","page":"API","text":"_removegroupdelay!(A, unit, di, g)\n\nRemove a digital-filter group delay of g points from the spectrum A (immediately after the FFT along array dimension di, before fftshift) by multiplying by the linear phase ramp exp(2πi·g·k/N), with k the signed frequency index. This is the Fourier shift theorem: it moves the time origin forward by g points, so build-up points stored before t = 0 end up at negative time, as in TopSpin and NMRPipe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._removegroupdelay!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._replacedimtuple-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"return a copy of a dimension tuple with the di-th element replaced by newdim\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._replacedimtuple"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._reshape_along-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"reshape a vector v so it broadcasts along array dimension di of an nd-dimensional array\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._reshape_along"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._resultant-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"weighted doubled-angle resultant for first-order phase p1r (radians)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._resultant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._scanp1-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"API","text":"_scanp1(φ, f, w, p1range, p1expected, λ) -> Float64\n\nFirst-order phase (radians) maximising the doubled-angle resultant within p1range (degrees), less a penalty λ/nₑ·((p1 − p1expected)/180)², where nₑ = (Σw)²/Σw² is the effective number of groups. The caller scales λ by the observed phase scatter, so the penalty acts as a prior whose influence falls as the fit becomes better determined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._scanp1"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._selectcols-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Integer}","page":"API","text":"indices of the nselect highest-energy columns of mat (or all, if fewer)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._selectcols"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._setdimmetadata-Tuple{Any, Any, Vararg{Pair}}","page":"API","text":"_setdimmetadata(spec, di, pairs...)\n\nReturn spec with the metadata of dimension di updated by pairs (key => value). The dimension is rebuilt with a copy of its metadata, so the input spectrum is left unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._setdimmetadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._shifthz-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"resolve a shift spec (:carrier or a ppm value) to a frequency offset (Hz) from the carrier\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._shifthz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._signalindices-Tuple{AbstractArray, Integer}","page":"API","text":"_signalindices(v, j; factor=3) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices along dimension j of the array v at which the largest magnitude over the other dimensions exceeds factor times its median. Most points of a frequency dimension carry only noise, so the median measures the noise maximum and the selected points are those with peaks. All indices are returned if none qualify.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._signalindices"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._tracenoise-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}}","page":"API","text":"_tracenoise(s; blocksize=64, q=0.2) -> Float64\n\nrms noise (per real component) of a complex trace. The trace is split into blocks (at most blocksize points, fewer for short traces, so that there are at least 16), a straight line is removed from the real and imaginary parts of each block, and the noise is taken from a low quantile q of the block standard deviations, so that blocks containing signal (including the dispersive tails of an unphased spectrum) do not inflate it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._tracenoise"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._usesolventfilter-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"whether to apply solvent suppression: solvent is true, a shift, or :auto for ¹H in H₂O\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._usesolventfilter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._withnoise-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"copy of spec with its own metadata and a fresh noise estimate\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._withnoise"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._wrap180-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"wrap an angle in degrees to (-180, 180]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._wrap180"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase._zhubaxforward-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Integer, Bool}","page":"API","text":"_zhubaxforward(x, order, rootfix) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nForward-extension LP coefficients by the Zhu–Bax scheme: fit backward LP, treat the roots of its prediction polynomial (Σⱼ bⱼwʲ = 1) as the candidate poles, reflect any with |z|>1 to 1/conj(z) so the forward extrapolation cannot grow, and rebuild forward coefficients. This separates signal poles (inside the unit circle) from noise poles more cleanly than a direct forward fit, and is the variant NMR usually prefers. With rootfix=false, falls back to a plain forward least-squares fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase._zhubaxforward"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.acqus-Tuple{AbstractNMRData}","page":"API","text":"acqus(nmrdata)\nacqus(nmrdata, key)\nacqus(nmrdata, key, index)\n\nReturn data from a Bruker acqus file, or nothing if it does not exist. Keys can be passed as symbols or strings. If no key is specified, a dictionary is returned representing the entire acqus file.\n\nIf present, the contents of auxilliary files such as vclist and vdlist can be accessed using this function.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> acqus(expt, :pulprog)\n\"zgesgp\"\njulia> acqus(expt, \"TE\")\n276.9988\njulia> acqus(expt, :p, 1)\n9.2\njulia> acqus(expt, \"D\", 1)\n0.1\njulia> acqus(expt, :vclist)\n11-element Vector{Int64}:\n[...]\n\nSee also metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.acqus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.annotations-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"annotations(nmrdata)\nannotations(nmrdata, key)\nannotations(nmrdata, key, index)\nannotations(nmrdata, key1, key2)\nannotations(nmrdata, keys...)\n\nReturn annotation data from pulse programme, or nothing if it does not exist. Keys can be passed as strings or symbols. If no key is specified, the entire annotations dictionary is returned.\n\nDotted names are automatically split and dereferenced, e.g. annotations(spec, \"cest.duration\") is equivalent to annotations(spec, \"cest\", \"duration\").\n\nThis function provides nested access to annotations stored in metadata[:annotations]. Multiple keys can be chained to access nested dictionaries or specific array elements.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> annotations(spec, \"title\")\n\"19F CEST\"\n\njulia> annotations(spec, \"dimensions\")\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"cest.offset\"\n \"f1\"\n\njulia> annotations(spec, \"dimensions\", 1)\n\"cest.offset\"\n\njulia> annotations(spec, \"cest\", \"duration\")\n0.5\n\njulia> annotations(spec, \"cest.duration\")\n0.5\n\njulia> annotations(spec, :reference_pulse)\n1-element Vector{Dict{String, Any}}:\n Dict(\"channel\" => \"f1\", \"pulse\" => 9.2e-6, \"power\" => -3.0)\n\njulia> annotations(spec, \"calibration.duration.start\")\n0.001\n\nSee also metadata, acqus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.annotations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.apod","page":"API","text":"apod(spec::NMRData, dimension, zerofill=true)\n\nReturn the time-domain apodization function for the specified axis, as a vector of values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.apod"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.apodize-Tuple{NMRData, Any, WindowFunction}","page":"API","text":"apodize(spec, [dim], [window]; inverse=false)\n\nMultiply the time-domain data by a window function. With no dimension argument every Fourier time dimension is apodized; with no window, each dimension's :window metadata is used (no apodization if it has none).\n\nThe window's length is set from the acquisition time when it is not given, so Cos2Window() or GeneralSineWindow(0.5, 1, 2) can be used without a tmax. The window starts at t = 0; digital-filter build-up points before t = 0 are left unwindowed. The window applied is recorded in the dimension's :window metadata.\n\nKeywords\n\ninverse: divide by the window instead, undoing a previous apodization.\n\nExamples\n\nloadfid(\"expt/1\") |> apodize(ExponentialWindow(5.0)) |> zerofill |> ft\nloadfid(\"hsqc/1\") |> apodize(T1Dim, ExponentialWindow(5.0)) |> apodize(T2Dim, Cos2Window())\n\nSee also WindowFunction, zerofill.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.apodize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.autophase-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"autophase(spec, [dim]; kw...)\n\nEstimate the phase correction of dimension dim with estimatephase and apply it with phase. Without dim, every frequency dimension is phased in turn, direct dimension first; a dimension in which no lines are found is left unphased, with a warning. The total correction applied is recorded in each dimension's :phase metadata, and the estimate is logged at debug level. Keywords are passed to estimatephase, for example order=0 for zero-order only, or exclude=[4.6 .. 4.9] to ignore water; without dim they apply to every dimension.\n\nFor multidimensional data the correction is estimated from a single trace along dim (see estimatepoles) and applied to the whole dataset.\n\nspec = loadfid(\"expt/1\") |> apodize(ExponentialWindow(1.0)) |> zerofill |> ft |> autophase |> realest\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.autophase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.autophase0-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"autophase0(spec, [dim]; exclude=())\n\nCorrect the zero-order phase of dimension dim of a frequency-domain spectrum, or without dim of every frequency dimension in turn. p0 is chosen so that the integral of the dispersion mode over the spectrum is zero and the integral of the absorption mode is positive: it is the phase of the integral of the complex spectrum, which equals the phase of the first point of the FID. Points in the chemical-shift intervals exclude, for example [4.6 .. 4.9], are left out of the integral. No first-order correction is applied.\n\nThe integral includes everything in the spectrum, so baseline offsets, broad background signals and corrupted first FID points all bias the result. For multidimensional data the correction is estimated from a single trace along dim (see estimatepoles) and applied to the whole dataset. The correction is recorded in the dimension's :phase metadata.\n\nSee also autophase, phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.autophase0"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.autoprocess-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"autoprocess(fid; window=Cos2Window(), zerofill=2, solvent=:auto, phase=:auto, rebuild=false)\nautoprocess(; kw...)\n\nProcess time-domain data from loadfid into a spectrum in one step. The second form returns a function for use with |>. The steps, in order:\n\nWith rebuild=true, rebuild points missing at the start of a FID that begins after t = 0 (as loadfid reports for data modified by TopSpin's cryoproc1d), by backward linearpredict.\nSuppress the water signal in the direct dimension with solventfilter.\nIn each Fourier dimension: apodize, zerofill and ft.\nPhase each frequency dimension, direct dimension first, with autophase or given phases. A dimension in which autophase finds no lines is left unphased, with a warning.\n\nThe result is a Multicomplex spectrum, so it can be phased further; take real for the real spectrum. Non-Fourier dimensions (relaxation delays, offsets, …) are left unchanged.\n\nKeywords\n\nwindow: window function, for example ExponentialWindow(2.0) or GeneralSineWindow(0.33, 1, 2).\nzerofill: zero-filling factor (1 for none).\nsolvent: :auto suppresses water in ¹H spectra whose solvent contains H₂O; true suppresses a signal at the carrier, a number suppresses one at that chemical shift (ppm), and false skips suppression.\nphase: :auto runs autophase; a tuple (p0, p1), or a single p0, applies those phases (degrees); nothing skips phasing.\nrebuild: rebuild points missing after t = 0 by backward linear prediction. Missing points leave a ripple around strong lines; rebuilding them removes it, but can add a broad baseline distortion when the FID contains fast-decaying signals, so it is off by default.\n\nwindow, zerofill and phase can also be given per dimension as a dictionary keyed by dimension number or type (T1Dim and F1Dim both mean the first dimension). Dimensions missing from a dictionary get the default.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadfid(\"expt/1\") |> autoprocess\nspec = loadfid(\"expt/1\") |> autoprocess(window=ExponentialWindow(1.0), solvent=false)\nspec = autoprocess(loadfid(\"hsqc/1\"); window=Dict(T1Dim => Cos2Window(), T2Dim => Cos2Window()),\n                   phase=Dict(F2Dim => (0.0, 0.0)))\n\nSee also autophase, apodize, zerofill, ft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.autoprocess"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.channels","page":"API","text":"channels(nmrdata)\nchannels(nmrdata, label)\nchannels(nmrdata, nucleus)\nchannels(nmrdata, string)\n\nAccess spectrometer channels. channels returns a dictionary of all channels keyed by label (:f1…:f8), or nothing if no channel information is available. channel returns a single channel dictionary (or nothing if not found).\n\nEach channel dictionary describes a physical RF channel independently of which dimensions were detected, with keys :label, :nucleus, :bf (base frequency, Hz), :sfo (carrier frequency, Hz), :offsethz and :offsetppm (carrier offset from :bf).\n\nA channel can be looked up by label (channels(spec, :f2)), by Nucleus (channels(spec, C13)), or by a string giving either (channels(spec, \"f2\"), channels(spec, \"13C\")). When several channels share a nucleus, the lowest-numbered channel is returned and a warning is issued.\n\nThis is the recommended way to obtain the frequency of a nucleus that has no detected axis, e.g. the carbon channel in a ¹³C-edited experiment read out on ¹H.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> channels(spec, C13)[:bf]\n1.5078e8\n\njulia> channels(spec, :f1)[:nucleus]\nH1\n\nSee also acqus, metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.channels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.coherenceorder","page":"API","text":"coherenceorder(coherence)\n\nCalculate the total coherence order.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> coherenceorder(SQ(H1))\n1\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,1),(C13,1))))\n2\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,1),(C13,-1))))\n0\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,3),(C13,1))))\n4\n\njulia> coherenceorder(MQ(((H1,0),)))\n0\n\nSee also Nucleus, SQ, MQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.coherenceorder"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.data-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"data(nmrdata, dim)\n\nReturn the numerical data associated with the specified dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.data-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"data(nmrdata)\n\nReturn the numerical data associated with the specified NMRData.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.data-Tuple{NMRDimension}","page":"API","text":"data(nmrdimension)\n\nReturn the numerical data associated with an NMR dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.db-Tuple{Power}","page":"API","text":"db(p::Power)\n\nGet the power level in dB attenuation.\n\nExample\n\np = Power(20.0, :dB)\ndb(p)  # 20.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.db"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.decimate","page":"API","text":"decimate(data, n dims=1)\ndecimate(n)\n\nDecimate NMR data into n-point averages along the specified dimension. Note that data are averaged and not summed. Noise metadata is not updated.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.decimate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.detectpeaks-Union{Tuple{NMRData{T, N, D, R, A} where {D<:Tuple, R<:Tuple, A<:AbstractArray{T, N}}}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}} where {T, N}","page":"API","text":"detectpeaks(spec; snr=10, threshold=nothing, sign=:both) -> Vector{Peak{N}}\ndetectpeaks(; kwargs...)\n\nFind the peaks in a frequency-domain spectrum of any dimensionality.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nA peak is a local maximum (or, for negative peaks, minimum) over its immediate neighbours in every dimension whose height exceeds threshold, which by default is snr times the noise level (estimated with estimatenoise if it has not been already). Positions and heights are refined to better than the digital resolution by parabolic interpolation, and linewidths are estimated from the half-height crossings in each dimension (NaN where an overlapping peak or the edge of the spectrum gets in the way). Heights are measured from the baseline, taken as the median of the spectrum, so a small offset does not produce spurious peaks; the reported intensities are heights above that baseline.\n\nsign may be :both, :positive or :negative. Peaks are returned strongest first and are labelled X1, X2, … to match NMRAnalysis.jl.\n\nEvery dimension must be a FrequencyDimension. To pick peaks in one plane of a pseudo-2D experiment, index it first, e.g. detectpeaks(spec[:, 1]).\n\nExamples\n\npks = detectpeaks(spec)                     # positive and negative peaks above 10σ\npks = detectpeaks(spec; snr=20, sign=:positive)\npks = detectpeaks(spec; threshold=1e6)      # an absolute threshold\n\nSee also findpeak, Peak.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.detectpeaks"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.estimatenoise-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"estimatenoise(nmrdata) -> NMRData\n\nEstimate the rms noise level in the data and update :noise metadata. Returns the NMRData object with noise estimate stored in metadata.\n\nIf called on an Array of data, each item will be updated.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nData are sorted into numerical order, and the highest and lowest 12.5% of data are discarded (so that 75% of the data remain). These values are then fitted to a truncated gaussian distribution via maximum likelihood analysis.\n\nThe log-likelihood function is:\n\nlog L(mu sigma) = sum_ilog P(y_i mu sigma)\n\nwhere the likelihood of an individual data point is:\n\nlog P(ymusigma) =\n    logfrac\n        phileft(fracx-musigmaright)\n    \n        sigma cdot leftPhileft(fracb-musigmaright) -\n            Phileft(fraca-musigmaright)right\n\nand phi(x) and Phi(x) are the standard normal pdf and cdf functions.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadnmr(\"experiment/1/pdata/1\")\nspec = estimatenoise(spec)  # Estimate and store noise level\nspec[:noise]  # Access the noise estimate\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.estimatenoise"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.estimatephase","page":"API","text":"estimatephase(spec, [dim]; order=1, p1=0, maxdelay=20e-6, pivot=nothing, snr=10,\n              coupling=20, maxwidthratio=3, maxresidual=30, λ=20, edges=0.01, kw...)\n    -> NamedTuple\nestimatephase([dim]; kwargs...)\n\nEstimate the zero- and first-order phase correction (degrees) for dimension dim (default: the direct dimension) of a frequency-domain spectrum. Returns (p0, p1, pivot, residual, nlines, rejected, edges), where phase(spec, dim, p0, p1; pivot) applies the correction, residual is the weighted rms phase error (degrees) of the observations used, nlines the number of line groups used, rejected lists the line groups left out of the fit as (shift, residual, relsnr), strongest first, where relsnr is the signal-to-noise ratio relative to the strongest group used, and edges lists the edge observations as (edge, snr, residual, used). A large residual, a small number of groups, or a strong rejected group means the estimate should not be trusted. autophase warns when strong groups are rejected.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nLines are found with estimatepoles. Poles within a coupling constant of each other are grouped, because the lines of a multiplet can carry antiphase dispersive components that cancel only in the sum. Each group's phase is the argument of the integral of the spectrum over a window around the group (up to four linewidths beyond its outer lines, and no more than halfway to the next group), less a linear baseline through the ends of the window. Poles much broader than the typical linewidth are ignored when they overlap a narrower line, since they then model its tails or the baseline. The correction is the weighted circular regression of group phase against position, taken modulo 180° so that negative peaks are not treated as phase errors. Groups with a residual above maxresidual degrees are discarded and the fit repeated. The 180° ambiguity is resolved by a majority vote of the groups, so that most lines are positive.\n\nThe edges of the spectrum add two observations. With the correct phase the real part is flat far from the lines, while the imaginary part carries the dispersion tails of the strong lines; the phase at which the real part of each edge region vanishes is used as a further observation, weighted by its signal-to-noise ratio. These are most informative for spectra with strong or broad signals, such as protein spectra with residual water. They are skipped when their signal-to-noise ratio is below snr, and used only if they agree with the fit to the line groups within three times the combined uncertainty of the groups' scatter and the edge's own 2° (and maxresidual), so that they refine the line-based estimate rather than override it. Observations are weighted by inverse variance, combining their signal-to-noise ratio with a systematic uncertainty: the scatter of the line groups about the fit (a scaled median absolute residual, so that a few noise groups do not inflate it), or 2° for an edge.\n\nKeywords\n\norder: 1 fits p0 and p1; 0 fits p0 only, with the first-order phase fixed at p1.\np1: expected first-order phase (degrees). Close to zero once the digital-filter group delay has been removed.\nmaxdelay: largest timing error (seconds) to allow for. A first-order phase is a shift of the time origin: an error of τ gives a first-order phase of 360°·SW·τ across the spectral width SW, so the search covers p1 ± 360·SW·maxdelay degrees. The default of 20 µs covers the residual dead-time and receiver delays of most experiments; it gives ±100° for a 14 kHz ¹H spectrum and ±950° for a 130 kHz ¹⁹F spectrum.\npivot: chemical shift (ppm) at which p0 is reported. Defaults to the first point, as for TopSpin's PHC0/PHC1.\nsnr: minimum signal-to-noise ratio of lines used.\ncoupling: poles closer than this (Hz, plus their mean linewidth) are grouped as one multiplet.\nmaxwidthratio: poles broader than this multiple of the median linewidth are ignored when a narrower line lies within their linewidth.\nmaxresidual: groups further than this (degrees) from the fitted phase are discarded.\nλ: strength of the prior pulling the first-order phase towards p1. It is scaled by the variance of the phase residuals and divided by the effective number of observations, so it matters when few observations determine the fit or their phases scatter, and not when many agree closely. Increase it when the lines cover only a small part of the spectrum, so p1 is poorly determined.\nedges: fraction of the spectrum at each end used as an edge observation; 0 disables them. Points within four linewidths of a line are excluded.\nOther keywords (exclude, bandwidth, …) are passed to estimatepoles. Use exclude to drop solvent or buffer lines.\n\nSee also autophase, phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.estimatephase"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.estimatepoles","page":"API","text":"estimatepoles(spec, [dim]; bandwidth=:auto, ncoef=16, snr=5, maxlinewidth=nothing, exclude=())\n    -> Vector{Pole}\nestimatepoles([dim]; kwargs...)\n\nModel a frequency-domain spectrum along dim (default: the direct dimension) as a sum of Lorentzian lines, by band-wise harmonic inversion (HSVD), and return one Pole per line, sorted by decreasing chemical shift. The spectrum may be unphased: the phase of each line is part of the result.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nThe spectrum is split into overlapping bands of bandwidth points. Each band is inverse Fourier transformed to a short time signal (using only the part covering acquired, not zero-filled, points) and fitted with ncoef damped exponentials. Poles are kept from the central half of each band only, to avoid edge artefacts; bands wrap around the ends of the spectrum, which is periodic. Bands with no signal above the threshold are skipped.\n\nFor multidimensional data, the trace along dim takes the first point of every other time dimension, sums every other frequency dimension over the points that carry signal, and sums any remaining dimensions.\n\nKeywords\n\nbandwidth: band size in points. :auto uses n ÷ 32 (a power of two between 256 and 4096) for an n-point spectrum.\nncoef: number of exponentials fitted per band.\nsnr: minimum signal-to-noise ratio (peak height / noise) for a pole to be kept.\nmaxlinewidth: largest linewidth (Hz) kept; broader poles model baseline rather than lines. Defaults to an eighth of the band width in Hz.\nexclude: chemical-shift intervals (ppm) to ignore, for example [4.6 .. 4.9] to drop water.\n\nSee also estimatephase, autophase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.estimatepoles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.extract-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"extract(dim, range)\nextract(dim1 => range1, dim2 => range2, ...)\n\nReturn a function that selects a region of a spectrum, for use with |>. dim is a dimension type (for example F1Dim) and range an interval (for example 6.0 .. 10.0). The noise estimate (:noise) of the selected region is refreshed.\n\nOutside a pipeline, index the spectrum directly with a chemical shift selector, as spec[F1Dim(6.0 .. 10.0)] or spec[6.0 .. 10.0, :].\n\nloadfid(\"1/ser\") |> ft |> extract(F1Dim, 6.0 .. 10.0) |> phase(F1Dim, -50) |> realest\nloadfid(\"2/ser\") |> ft |> extract(F1Dim => 6.0 .. 10.0, F2Dim => 105 .. 135)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.extract"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.finddim-Tuple{Any, Nucleus}","page":"API","text":"finddim(spec, nucleus::Nucleus)\n\nFind the dimension index in an NMRData object that corresponds to the given nucleus. Returns the dimension index (1-based) or nothing if not found.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadnmr(\"hsqc.ft2\")\nfinddim(spec, H1)   # Returns 1 for a 1H-15N HSQC\nfinddim(spec, N15)  # Returns 2 for a 1H-15N HSQC\n\nSee also shiftdim, reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.finddim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.findnearest-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Peak{N}}, Any}} where N","page":"API","text":"findnearest(peaks, position; scale=1) -> Union{Int,Nothing}\n\nThe index of the peak in peaks nearest to position (a tuple in ppm, or a number for 1D peaks), or nothing if the list is empty. This is what selecting a peak with a mouse click means; the index can be passed straight to deleteat! or used to replace the peak.\n\nscale weights the dimensions before distances are compared, as a tuple or a single number, since a ppm of ¹⁵N is not a ppm of ¹H. A typical choice for an ¹H,¹⁵N spectrum is scale=(1, 0.2); a GUI can pass the inverse of the visible range in each dimension so that distances are measured as they appear on screen.\n\nExamples\n\ni = findnearest(pks, (8.2, 120.0); scale=(1, 0.2))\ndeleteat!(pks, i)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.findnearest"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.findpeak-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{NMRData{T, N, D, R, A} where {D<:Tuple, R<:Tuple, A<:AbstractArray{T, N}}, Any}} where {T, N}","page":"API","text":"findpeak(spec, position; label=\"\") -> Peak\nfindpeak(position; label=\"\")\n\nThe peak nearest to position (a tuple in ppm, ordered like the dimensions of spec), found by climbing from the closest data point to the top of the hill it sits on, or to the bottom of the valley if the spectrum is negative there. The position, height and linewidths are refined just as in detectpeaks.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nThis is what snapping a mouse click onto a peak means.\n\nExamples\n\np = findpeak(spec, (8.2, 120.0); label=\"G10\")\np = findpeak(spec1d, 4.7)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.findpeak"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.frequencyshift-Tuple{NMRData, Any, Real}","page":"API","text":"frequencyshift(spec, dim, Δ)\nfrequencyshift(dim, Δ)\n\nShift time-domain data in frequency by Δ Hz, by multiplying the FID by exp(-imₖ·2π·Δ·t): a signal at +Δ Hz moves to the carrier. solventfilter uses this to suppress signals away from the carrier; it is also useful for re-referencing in the time domain. The second form returns a function for use with |>.\n\nExamples\n\nfid |> frequencyshift(T1Dim, 250.0) |> ft\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.frequencyshift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ft-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"ft(spec, [dim]; inverse=false)\n\nFourier transform one dimension, or with no dimension argument every Fourier dimension in order (direct dimension first). A time dimension becomes the corresponding frequency dimension, or the reverse with inverse=true. The transform uses the dimension's imaginary unit (:mcindex), applies no normalisation, as TopSpin does, and refreshes the noise estimate.\n\nThe spectrum is ordered from high to low chemical shift, as for data loaded with loadnmr, and ft(ft(spec); inverse=true) recovers spec.\n\nA digital-filter group delay recorded by loadfid (:grpdly) is removed after the transform with a linear phase ramp, which moves the filter's build-up points to negative time. The frequency dimension has no group delay left, so an inverse transform gives a FID whose first point is at t = 0.\n\nKeywords\n\ninverse: inverse transform from the frequency domain to the time domain.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadfid(\"expt/1\") |> apodize(Cos2Window()) |> zerofill |> ft\nspec2 = ft(spec, F1Dim; inverse=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.gyromagneticratio","page":"API","text":"gyromagneticratio(n::Nucleus)\ngyromagneticratio(c::Coherence)\n\nReturn the gyromagnetic ratio in Hz/T of a nucleus, or calculate the effective gyromagnetic ratio of a coherence. This is equal to the product of the individual gyromagnetic ratios with their coherence orders.\n\nReturns nothing if not defined.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(H1)\n2.6752218744e8\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(SQ(H1))\n2.6752218744e8\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(MQ(((H1,1),(C13,1))))\n3.3480498744e8\n\njulia> gyromagneticratio(MQ(((H1,0),)))\n0.0\n\nSee also Nucleus, Coherence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.gyromagneticratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hasannotations-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"hasannotations(nmrdata) -> Bool\n\nCheck if the NMRData object has any annotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hasannotations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hasnonfrequencydimension-Union{Tuple{NMRData{T, N, A, R, A1} where {R<:Tuple, A1<:AbstractArray{T, N}}}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}} where {T, N, A}","page":"API","text":"hasnonfrequencydimension(spectrum)\n\nReturn true if the spectrum contains a non-frequency domain dimension.\n\nExample\n\njulia> y2=loadnmr(\"exampledata/2D_HN/test.ft2\");\njulia> hasnonfrequencydimension(y2)\nfalse\njulia> y3=loadnmr(\"exampledata/pseudo3D_HN_R2/ft/test%03d.ft2\");\njulia> hasnonfrequencydimension(y3)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hasnonfrequencydimension"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hassample-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"hassample(nmrdata) -> Bool\n\nCheck if the NMRData object has any associated sample metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hassample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-Tuple{FrequencyDimension}","page":"API","text":"hz(axis)\n\nReturn the offsets (in Hz) for points along a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-Tuple{Power, Power, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"hz(p::Power, ref_p::Power, ref_pulselength, ref_pulseangle_deg)\n\nConvert power to radiofrequency strength in Hz using reference pulse parameters.\n\nArguments\n\np::Power: Power to convert\nref_p::Power: Reference power\nref_pulselength: Reference pulse length in microseconds\nref_pulseangle_deg: Reference pulse flip angle in degrees\n\nReturns\n\nRadiofrequency strength in Hz for power p\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-Tuple{Power, Power, Any}","page":"API","text":"hz(p::Power, ref_p::Power, ref_Hz)\n\nConvert power to radiofrequency strength in Hz using a reference power and known Hz value.\n\nUses the relationship: Hz = ref_Hz * 10^(-ΔdB/20) where ΔdB is the power difference.\n\nArguments\n\np::Power: Power to convert\nref_p::Power: Reference power with known Hz value\nref_Hz: Radiofrequency strength in Hz at the reference power\n\nReturns\n\nRadiofrequency strength in Hz for power p\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-Tuple{Union{Number, AbstractArray{<:Number}}, FrequencyDimension}","page":"API","text":"hz(δ, axis)\n\nReturn the offset (in Hz) for a chemical shift (or list of shifts) on a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.intensity-Tuple{Peak}","page":"API","text":"intensity(peak)\n\nThe height of a peak at its maximum, in the units of the spectrum it was picked from, or NaN if unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.isaqueous-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"isaqueous(spec) -> Bool\n\nDetermine whether a spectrum was acquired in an aqueous solvent.\n\nArguments\n\nspec: NMRData object\n\nChecks against the metadata field :solvent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.isaqueous"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.label!","page":"API","text":"label!(nmrdata, labeltext)\nlabel!(nmrdata, dim, labeltext)\nlabel!(nmrdimension, labeltext)\n\nSet the label associated with an NMRData structure or an NMRDimension.\n\nSee also label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.label!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.linearpredict-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"linearpredict(spec, [dim]; npoints=nothing, factor=2, order=:auto, direction=:forward,\n              replace=0, rootfix=true, pool=true)\n\nExtend a time dimension by linear prediction, or rebuild its first points.\n\nLinear prediction models each FID as a sum of decaying oscillations, using the autoregressive coefficient method of nmrPipe and nmrglue, and extrapolates it by recurrence. Forward extension uses the Zhu–Bax scheme: it fits backward coefficients, reflects the roots of the prediction polynomial inside the unit circle, and builds forward coefficients from them, which keeps the extrapolation stable.\n\nWith no dimension argument, every indirect Fourier dimension is extended. Prediction uses the FID from t = 0; digital-filter build-up points before t = 0 are left unchanged.\n\nKeywords\n\nnpoints: length after prediction. Defaults to factor times the current length.\nfactor: length multiplier used when npoints is not given.\norder: number of prediction coefficients. :auto uses clamp(td ÷ 2, 8, 32).\ndirection: :forward appends points; :backward prepends them and moves the start of the time axis back. Backward extension rebuilds points missing at the start of a FID that begins after t = 0 (see loadfid).\nreplace: if greater than 0, replace that many points from t = 0 by backward prediction, keeping the length unchanged. Overrides npoints and direction.\nrootfix: reflect unstable roots so that the extrapolation cannot grow.\npool: estimate one set of coefficients from the strongest vectors and apply it to all of them. Vectors along one dimension share the same frequencies, so this is valid and much faster than fitting each vector separately (pool=false, as nmrglue does).\n\nUse on time-domain data, before apodize. The :td metadata is updated.\n\nExamples\n\nfid |> linearpredict(T2Dim) |> apodize |> zerofill |> ft     # double the indirect dimension\nlinearpredict(fid, T1Dim; replace=2)                          # rebuild the first two points\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.linearpredict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.lineshape-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, LineshapeComplexity}","page":"API","text":"lineshape(axis, δ, R2, complexity=RealLineshape())\n\nReturn a simulated real- or complex-valued spectrum for a resonance with chemical shift δ and relaxation rate R2, using the parameters and window function associated with the specified axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.lineshape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.linewidth-Tuple{Peak}","page":"API","text":"linewidth(peak)\nlinewidth(peak, n)\n\nThe full width at half maximum of a peak in Hz: a tuple ordered like the spectrum's dimensions, or the nth dimension's on its own. NaN where unknown. The corresponding transverse relaxation rate is π * linewidth(peak, n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.linewidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.missingval","page":"API","text":"missingval(x)\n\nReturns the value representing missing data in the dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.missingval"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.nucleus-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"nucleus(label::AbstractString) -> Nucleus\n\nParse a nucleus from a string label, returning the corresponding Nucleus enum value.\n\nThe function accepts common NMR nucleus notation formats:\n\nMass number followed by element symbol: \"1H\", \"13C\", \"15N\", \"19F\", \"31P\"\nElement symbol followed by mass number: \"H1\", \"C13\", \"N15\", \"F19\", \"P31\"\n\nExamples\n\nnucleus(\"19F\")  # returns F19\nnucleus(\"1H\")   # returns H1\nnucleus(\"13C\")  # returns C13\nnucleus(\"15N\")  # returns N15\n\nThrows ArgumentError if the nucleus is not recognised or not defined in the Nucleus enum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.nucleus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.pcls-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"pcls(A, y)\n\nCompute the phase-constrained least squares solution:\n\ny = A x e^iphi\n\nfollowing the algorithm of Bydder (2010) Lin Alg & Apps.\n\nReturns the tuple (x, ϕ), containing the component amplitudes and global phase.\n\nIf passed a matrix Y, the function will return a matrix of component amplitudes and a list of global phases corresponding to each row.\n\nArguments\n\nA: (m,n) complex matrix with component spectra\ny: (m,) complex vector containing the observed spectrum\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.pcls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.peaklist-Tuple{AbstractVector{ChemicalShift}, Vararg{AbstractString}}","page":"API","text":"peaklist(shifts, atoms...) -> Vector{Peak{N}}\npeaklist(shifts, H1, X) -> Vector{Peak{2}}\npeaklist(shifts, X) -> Vector{Peak{1}}\n\nThe peaks expected in an N-dimensional experiment, assembled from a list of assigned chemical shifts (see loadbmrb). Dimensions are given in the order of the spectrum, direct dimension first.\n\nBy atom name. Each argument names an atom, optionally with a residue offset relative to the residue that the peak belongs to: \"CA-1\" is the Cα of the preceding residue. A peak is produced for each residue where every atom has an assigned shift. \"CO\" and \"HN\" are accepted for the BMRB names \"C\" and \"H\".\n\nBy nucleus. Passing H1 and another nucleus gives one peak for every directly bonded pair of atoms with assigned shifts: every N–H for an ¹⁵N HSQC, including side-chain NH and NH₂ groups, or every C–H for a ¹³C HSQC. Methyl and methylene protons with identical shifts give a single peak.\n\nA 1D list. Passing a single nucleus gives one peak for every shift of that nucleus, e.g. the expected lines of a ¹³C or ¹⁹F 1D spectrum.\n\nPeaks are labelled by residue and atom, e.g. L23H-N, K22C-L23N or L23HD1-CD1. Positions come from the shift list, while intensities and linewidths are NaN.\n\nExamples\n\nshifts = loadbmrb(15000)              # download BMRB entry 15000\n\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\")              # backbone amide ¹H,¹⁵N HSQC\npeaklist(shifts, H1, N15)               # all NH groups, including side chains\npeaklist(shifts, H1, C13)               # ¹H,¹³C HSQC\npeaklist(shifts, C13)                   # every ¹³C shift, as a 1D list\npeaklist(shifts, \"C-1\", \"N\")            # CON: C′ of residue i-1 with N of residue i\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\", \"C-1\")       # HNCO\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\", \"CA\")        # the i peaks of an HNCA\npeaklist(shifts, \"H\", \"N\", \"CA-1\")      # and the i-1 peaks\n\nilv = filter(s -> s.residuename in (\"ILE\", \"LEU\", \"VAL\"), shifts)\npeaklist(ilv, H1, C13)                  # ILV methyl (and other CH) peaks\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.peaklist"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.phase","page":"API","text":"phase(spec, dim, p0, p1=0; pivot=nothing)\nphase(dim, p0, p1=0; pivot=nothing)\n\nApply a zero-order (p0) and first-order (p1) phase correction, in degrees, along dimension dim. The phase applied to point n of N (counting from 0 at the first, highest-ppm point) is θ = p0 + p1·n/N, multiplied in as exp(-imₖ·θ) with the dimension's imaginary unit. This is TopSpin's convention, so p0 and p1 are its PHC0 and PHC1. Works in the time or frequency domain. The second form returns a function for use with |>.\n\nFor a frequency dimension the total correction applied so far is recorded in the dimension's :phase metadata as (p0=…, p1=…), with p0 referred to the first point, and the noise estimate is refreshed.\n\nKeywords\n\npivot: position at which only p0 acts, in ppm for a frequency dimension or seconds for a time dimension. Defaults to the first point.\n\nExamples\n\nspec |> phase(F1Dim, 128.5) |> realest\nphase(spec, F1Dim, 40.0, -20.0; pivot=4.7)\n\nSee also autophase, autophase0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.phase"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm-Tuple{FrequencyDimension}","page":"API","text":"ppm(axis)\n\nReturn the ppm values for points along a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm-Tuple{Peak}","page":"API","text":"ppm(peak)\nppm(peak, n)\n\nThe position of a peak in ppm: a tuple ordered like the spectrum's dimensions, or the nth coordinate on its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm-Tuple{Union{Number, AbstractArray{<:Number}}, FrequencyDimension}","page":"API","text":"ppm(offset, axis)\n\nReturn the chemical shifts for a given offset or list of offsets along a frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.reference-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"reference(spec, dims, pairs; indirect=true, aqueous=:auto)\nreference(dims, pairs; kwargs...)\n\nReference multiple spectrum dimensions simultaneously.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nArguments\n\nspec: NMRData object to reference\ndims: Collection of dimensions to reference\npairs: Collection of old_shift => new_shift pairs (same length as dims)\nindirect=true: If true, reference remaining frequency dimensions using Xi ratios\naqueous=:auto: Solvent type for Xi ratio selection\n\nExamples\n\n# Reference both 1H and 15N dimensions\nspec2 = reference(spec, [F1Dim, F2Dim], [4.7 => 4.8, 120.0 => 119.5])\nspec2 = reference(spec, (H1, N15), (4.7 => 4.8, 120.0 => 119.5))\n\nSee also xi_ratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.reference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.reference-Tuple{Any, Any, Pair{<:Number, <:Number}}","page":"API","text":"reference(spec, dim, pair::Pair; indirect=true, aqueous=:auto)\nreference(dim, pair::Pair; kwargs...)\n\nReference a spectrum dimension by specifying an old and new chemical shift.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nArguments\n\nspec: NMRData object to reference\ndim: Dimension to reference (Int, dimension type like F1Dim, or Nucleus like H1)\npair: A pair old_shift => new_shift specifying the reference point\nindirect=true: If true, reference other frequency dimensions using Xi ratios\naqueous=:auto: Solvent type for Xi ratio selection (:auto, true, or false)\n\nExamples\n\n# Reference 1H dimension\nspec2 = reference(spec, 1, -0.12 => 0.0)\nspec2 = reference(spec, F1Dim, -0.12 => 0.0)\nspec2 = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0.0)\n\n# Reference without indirect referencing of other dimensions\nspec2 = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0.0; indirect=false)\n\nSee also shiftdim, xi_ratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.reference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.reference-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"reference(spec; indirect=true, aqueous=:auto, temperature=nothing)\nreference(; kwargs...)\n\nReference a spectrum to water based on the sample temperature.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nThis method is used when no explicit reference shift is provided. It calculates the expected water chemical shift from the temperature and applies a correction relative to the Bruker default of 4.7 ppm.\n\nArguments\n\nspec: NMRData object to reference\nindirect=true: If true, also reference heteronuclear dimensions using Xi ratios\ntemperature=nothing: Temperature in Kelvin. If nothing, reads from metadata.\n\nReturns\n\nNew NMRData object with referenced chemical shifts\n\nOnly works for aqueous solvents (D2O, H2O, H2O+D2O).\n\nSee also reference(spec, dim, pair), xi_ratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.reference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.referencepulse-Tuple{AbstractNMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"referencepulse(spec, nucleus) -> (pulse_length, power)\n\nGet the reference pulse calibration for a given nucleus from pulse programme annotations. Returns a tuple of (pulse_length, power) or nothing if not found.\n\nThe reference pulse is the calibrated 90° hard pulse used as the basis for all power  calculations on that channel.\n\nArguments\n\nspec: NMRData object with annotations\nnucleus: Nucleus symbol or string (e.g., :19F, \"19F\", :1H, \"1H\")\n\nReturns\n\n(pulse_length, power): Tuple where pulse_length is in μs and power is a Power object\nnothing: If no reference pulse found for the specified nucleus\n\nExamples\n\njulia> referencepulse(spec, \"19F\")\n(13.29, Power(-9.03 dB, 8.0 W))\n\njulia> referencepulse(spec, :1H)\n(9.2, Power(-3.0 dB, 32.0 W))\n\nSee also annotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.referencepulse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.register!-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Peak{N}}, NMRData{T, N, D, R, A} where {D<:Tuple, R<:Tuple, A<:AbstractArray{T, N}}}} where {T, N}","page":"API","text":"register!(peaks, spec; maxshift=nothing) -> peaks\nregister(peaks, spec; maxshift=nothing) -> Vector{Peak{N}}\nregister!(spec; maxshift=nothing)\nregister(spec; maxshift=nothing)\n\nTranslate a peak list onto a spectrum: find the single offset, applied to every peak, that best matches the list to the signals observed in spec, and move the peaks by it. register returns a moved copy and leaves peaks unchanged. The offset found is reported with @info.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nThis corrects for referencing differences, e.g. between a list predicted from BMRB shifts (see loadbmrb) and a spectrum recorded under different conditions. It does not move peaks individually.\n\nThe match is scored as sum_i log(1 + S(delta_i + Delta)  sigma): the absolute intensity of the spectrum S at each shifted peak position, so that negative peaks count as much as positive ones, in units of the noise sigma. The logarithm gives diminishing returns for very intense signals, so that many peaks landing on signal outweigh one peak landing on a huge one. To tolerate small disagreements between individual peaks, each point of the spectrum takes the largest absolute intensity among its immediate neighbours.\n\nEvery whole-point offset within maxshift is tried, so the match is global rather than the nearest local optimum, and the best is then refined to a fraction of a point. maxshift is the largest offset considered in each dimension in ppm, as a tuple or a single number, and defaults to 10% of the spectral width. Set it to 0 in a dimension to hold that dimension fixed.\n\nExamples\n\npks = loadbmrb(15000, H1, N15)\nregister!(pks, spec)                        # shift the BMRB list onto the HSQC\nregister!(pks, spec; maxshift=(0.3, 2.0))   # search at most ±0.3 ppm ¹H, ±2 ppm ¹⁵N\nregister!(pks, spec; maxshift=(0.3, 0))     # shift ¹H only\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.register!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.replacedimension-Tuple{NMRData, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"replacedimension(nmrdata, olddimnumber, newdim)\nreplacedimension(olddimnumber, newdim)\n\nReturn a new NMRData, in which the numbered axis is replaced by a new Dimension.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.replacedimension"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.resolvedim-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"resolvedim(spec, dim_ref) -> Int\n\nResolve a dimension reference to a dimension index. Accepts:\n\nInt: dimension index (returned as-is)\nDimension type (e.g., F1Dim): finds first matching dimension\nNucleus (e.g., H1, C13): finds dimension with matching nucleus\n\nReturns the dimension index or throws an error if not found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.resolvedim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.sample-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"sample(nmrdata)\nsample(nmrdata, keys...)\n\nReturn the sample metadata associated with the NMRData object, or nothing if not available. Sample metadata follows the schema defined at https://github.com/waudbylab/nmr-sample-schema.\n\nWhen keys are provided, navigates nested dictionaries using the given keys. Keys can be strings or symbols and are automatically converted to lowercase strings for consistent access.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> sample(spec)\nDict{String, Any} with 6 entries:\n  \"nmr_tube\" => Dict{String, Any}(\"diameter\"=>\"5 mm\", \"type\"=>\"regular\", \"sample_volume_uL\"=>600)\n  \"buffer\"   => Dict{String, Any}(\"solvent\"=>\"10% D2O\", \"reference_unit\"=>\"%w/v\", \"chemical_shift_reference\"=>\"none\")\n  [...]\n\njulia> sample(spec, \"notes\")\n\"here's a note!\"\n\njulia> sample(spec, :people, :users)\n1-element Vector{Any}:\n \"Chris\"\n\njulia> sample(spec, \"sample\", \"components\")\n2-element Vector{Any}:\n Dict{String, Any}(\"name\" => \"HEWL\", \"isotopic_labelling\" => \"unlabelled\", \"unit\" => \"mM\", \"concentration\" => 10)\n Dict{String, Any}(\"name\" => \"gadodiamide\", \"isotopic_labelling\" => \"unlabelled\", \"unit\" => \"mM\", \"concentration\" => 1)\n\njulia> sample(spec, :sample, :components, :name)\n2-element Vector{Any}:\n \"HEWL\"\n \"gadodiamide\"\n\nWhen the final key is applied to an array (such as components), the key is mapped over each element and a filtered array is returned.\n\nFor isotopic_labelling and solvent, if the stored value is \"custom\", the corresponding custom_labelling or custom_solvent field is returned automatically.\n\nSee also metadata, hassample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.sample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.samplefile-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"samplefile(x) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nReturn the path to the JSON sample file associated with x, or nothing if no sample has been matched. Works with NMRData and NMRExperiment.\n\nSee also sample, hassample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.samplefile"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.scale-Tuple{AbstractNMRData}","page":"API","text":"scale(d::AbstractNMRData)\n\nReturn a scaling factor for the data combining the number of scans, receiver gain, and, if specified, the sample concentration.\n\nmathrmscale = mathrmns cdot mathrmrg cdot mathrmconc\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.setgradientlist-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"setgradientlist(A::NMRData, [dimnumber], relativegradientlist, Gmax=nothing)\nsetgradientlist([dimnumber], relativegradientlist, Gmax=nothing)\n\nReturn a new NMRData with a gradient axis containing the passed values. If no maximum strength is specified, a default gradient strength of 0.55 T m⁻¹ will be set, but a warning raised for the user.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nIf a dimension number is specified, that dimension will be replaced. If not, the function will search for a unique non-frequency dimension, and replace that. If there are multiple non-frequency dimensions, the dimension number must be specified explicitly, and the function will throw an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.setgradientlist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.setkinetictimes-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"setkinetictimes(A::NMRData, [dimnumber], tvals, units=\"\")\nsetkinetictimes([dimnumber], tvals, units=\"\")\n\nReturn a new NMRData with a kinetic time axis containing the passed values (and optionally, units). If a dimension number is specified, that dimension will be replaced with a TkinDim. If not, the function will search for a unique non-frequency dimension, and replace that. If there are multiple non-frequency dimensions, the dimension number must be specified explicitly, and the function will throw an error.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.setkinetictimes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.setoffsets","page":"API","text":"setoffsets(A::NMRData, dimnumber, offsets, units=\"ppm\")\n\nReturn a new NMRData with an offset axis (for CEST, R1rho off-resonance, etc.). Dimension number must be specified explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.setoffsets"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.setrelaxtimes-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"setrelaxtimes(A::NMRData, [dimnumber], tvals, units=\"\")\nsetrelaxtimes([dimnumber], tvals, units=\"\")\n\nReturn a new NMRData with a relaxation time axis containing the passed values (and optionally, units). If a dimension number is specified, that dimension will be replaced with a TrelaxDim. If not, the function will search for a unique non-frequency dimension, and replace that. If there are multiple non-frequency dimensions, the dimension number must be specified explicitly, and the function will throw an error.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.setrelaxtimes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.setspinlockfield","page":"API","text":"setspinlockfield(A::NMRData, dimnumber, fields, units=\"Hz\")\n\nReturn a new NMRData with a spinlock field strength axis. Dimension number must be specified explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.setspinlockfield"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.shiftdim-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"shiftdim(data::NMRData, dim_ref, offset)\nshiftdim(dim_ref, offset)\n\nAdd an offset to a frequency dimension in an NMRData object. The dimension can be specified as a numerical index, a dimension type like F1Dim, or a Nucleus enum (e.g., H1, C13).\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nThe metadata is copied using replacedimension, and an entry is added or updated in the dimension metadata to record the offset change.\n\nExamples\n\nspec2 = shiftdim(spec, 1, 0.5)       # Using dimension index\nspec2 = shiftdim(spec, F1Dim, 0.5)   # Using dimension type\nspec2 = shiftdim(spec, H1, 0.5)      # Using nucleus\n\nSee also reference, finddim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.shiftdim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.solventfilter-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"solventfilter(spec, [dim]; shift=:carrier, method=:lowpass, width=40.0, shape=:sine,\n              band=nothing, ncoef=:auto)\n\nSuppress a solvent signal, or any narrow signal, in the time domain. With no dimension argument the direct dimension is filtered.\n\nKeywords\n\nshift: :carrier, or the chemical shift (ppm) of the signal to remove. A signal away from the carrier is moved to it with frequencyshift, filtered, and moved back.\nmethod: :lowpass subtracts a low-pass filtered copy of the FID (the convolution difference method of nmrPipe's SOL). It is fast and suits multidimensional data. :hsvd fits the FID with decaying oscillations and subtracts those within band of the target, which disturbs nearby peaks less but fits each FID separately, so it suits 1D data.\nwidth: approximate width (Hz) of the region suppressed by :lowpass. A larger value suppresses a wider region.\nshape: low-pass window for :lowpass: :boxcar, :sine or :sine².\nband: width (Hz) of the region removed by :hsvd. Defaults to width.\nncoef: number of oscillations fitted by :hsvd. :auto uses clamp(td ÷ 16, 16, 48).\n\nUse on time-domain data, before apodize.\n\nExamples\n\nfid |> solventfilter |> apodize |> zerofill |> ft\nsolventfilter(fid; shift=4.7, method=:hsvd, band=60)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.solventfilter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.spin-Tuple{Nucleus}","page":"API","text":"spin(n::Nucleus)\n\nReturn the spin quantum number of nucleus n, or nothing if not defined.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> spin(H1)\n1//2\n\nSee also Coherence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.spin"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.watershift-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"watershift(temperature_K)\n\nCalculate the chemical shift of water at a given temperature using the empirical formula:\n\nδ(H₂O) = 7.83 − T / 96.9\n\nwhere T is the temperature in Kelvin.\n\nArguments\n\ntemperature_K: Temperature in Kelvin\n\nReturns\n\nChemical shift of water in ppm\n\nReferences\n\nBruker uses 4.7 ppm as the default water reference when locking (with SR=0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.watershift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.watts-Tuple{Power}","page":"API","text":"watts(p::Power)\n\nGet the power level in Watts.\n\nExample\n\np = Power(20.0, :dB)\nwatts(p)  # 0.01\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.watts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.xi_ratio-Tuple{Nucleus}","page":"API","text":"xi_ratio(n::Nucleus; aqueous=true)\n\nReturn the Xi ratio for nucleus n, used for indirect chemical shift referencing.\n\nThe Xi ratio is defined as the ratio of the reference frequency of nucleus X to the 1H reference frequency (TMS or DSS). This enables indirect referencing of heteronuclear dimensions from a single 1H reference.\n\nArguments\n\nn::Nucleus: The nucleus for which to retrieve the Xi ratio\naqueous=true: If true, use DSS-based ratios (aqueous solutions).                 If false, use TMS-based ratios (organic solvents).\n\nReturns\n\nThe Xi ratio as a Float64, or nothing if not defined for this nucleus.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> xi_ratio(C13)\n0.25144953\n\njulia> xi_ratio(C13; aqueous=true)\n0.25144953\n\njulia> xi_ratio(C13; aqueous=false)\n0.2514502\n\njulia> xi_ratio(N15)\n0.101329118\n\nReferences\n\nMarkley et al., 1998, Pure Appl. Chem. 70, 117-142\nWishart et al., 1995, J. Biomol. NMR 6, 135-140\nMaurer and Kalbitzer, 1996, J. Magn. Reson. B113, 177-178\nIUPAC recommendations 2001/2008\n\nSee also reference, Nucleus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.xi_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.zerofill-Tuple{NMRData, Any}","page":"API","text":"zerofill(spec, [dim]; factor=2)\n\nAppend zeros to the end of the FID, multiplying the number of points by the integer factor. With no dimension argument every Fourier time dimension is zero-filled. The :tdzf metadata is updated.\n\nExamples\n\nfid |> zerofill |> ft                   # double every Fourier dimension\nfid |> zerofill(T1Dim; factor=4) |> ft\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.zerofill"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRExperiment-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"NMRExperiment(experimentfolder::String) -> NMRExperiment\n\nConstruct a metadata-only descriptor from a Bruker NMR experiment folder. Reads acqus, title, and pulse programme (text files only) — no binary data loaded. Annotations are parsed from the pulse programme YAML but parameter references are not resolved (full resolution happens in loadnmr).\n\nSee also: scanexperiments, loadnmr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRExperiment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRSample-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"NMRSample(path::String) -> NMRSample\n\nLoad an NMR sample JSON file and return an NMRSample descriptor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.NMRSample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.FQList","page":"API","text":"FQList(values, unit::Symbol, relative::Bool)\nFQList(values, unit::Symbol, relative::Bool, bf, sfo)\n\nRepresents a frequency list. unit can be :Hz or :ppm, and relative indicates whether the frequency is given relative to SFO (true) or BF (false).\n\nbf and sfo optionally record the reference base and carrier frequencies (in Hz) of the spectrometer channel this list belongs to. When present, the list can be converted to ppm or Hz on its own — without a detected frequency axis — using the single-argument ppm and hz methods. See withreference to attach a reference from a channel dictionary.\n\nRaw values can be extracted using the data function, or (better) as absolute chemical shifts (in ppm) or relative offsets (in Hz) using ppm and hz functions.\n\nSee also: ppm, hz, withreference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.FQList"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-Tuple{FQList, FrequencyDimension}","page":"API","text":"hz(f::FQList, ax::FrequencyDimension)\n\nReturn frequency list values as offsets relative to the spectrometer frequency, in Hz.\n\nSee also: ppm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.hz-Tuple{FQList}","page":"API","text":"hz(f::FQList)\n\nReturn frequency list values as offsets relative to the spectrometer carrier frequency, in Hz, using the reference frequencies (:bf, :sfo) stored on the list. Throws an error if no reference is attached — see withreference.\n\nSee also: ppm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.hz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm-Tuple{FQList, FrequencyDimension}","page":"API","text":"ppm(f::FQList, ax::FrequencyDimension)\n\nReturn frequency list values in ppm (in absolute terms, i.e. relative to 0 ppm).\n\nSee also: hz\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm-Tuple{FQList}","page":"API","text":"ppm(f::FQList)\n\nReturn frequency list values in ppm (in absolute terms, i.e. relative to 0 ppm), using the reference frequencies (:bf, :sfo) stored on the list. Throws an error if no reference is attached — see withreference.\n\nSee also: hz\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRBase.ppm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._addsampleinfo_metadata!-Tuple{Dict{Symbol, Any}}","page":"API","text":"_addsampleinfo_metadata!(md::Dict{Symbol,Any})\n\nSearch for a matching sample JSON in the experiment parent directory and store the result as an NMRSample under md[:sample]. Operates on a raw metadata dict.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._addsampleinfo_metadata!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._annotate_metadata!-Tuple{Dict{Symbol, Any}}","page":"API","text":"_annotate_metadata!(md::Dict{Symbol,Any})\n\nParse pulse programme annotations from a raw metadata dict and store them under :annotations. Only parses the raw YAML — skips parameter resolution and programmatic list expansion, which require loaded data. Suitable for use with NMRExperiment descriptors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._annotate_metadata!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._apply_dimension_annotations-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"_apply_dimension_annotations(spec::NMRData) -> NMRData\n\nApply semantic dimension types based on the dimensions array in annotations. The dimensions array provides explicit ordering - index i in the array corresponds to dimension i in the data. Returns new NMRData with updated dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._apply_dimension_annotations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._buildchannels-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"_buildchannels(acqusdic) -> Dict{Symbol,Any}\n\nBuild a dictionary of spectrometer channels from a parsed acqus dictionary, keyed by channel label (:f1…:f8). Each entry is itself a dictionary describing the nucleus and frequencies on that channel, independent of which dimensions were detected. Only channels with an assigned nucleus (nucN present and not \"off\") are included.\n\nFrequencies (:bf, :sfo) are in Hz — replacefrequencies! has already run by the time the acqus dictionary is built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._buildchannels"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._copyparameters-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_copyparameters(src, dest)\n\nCopy the parameter files of experiment folder src into dest: every regular file except the binary data (fid, ser), plus the lists folder. Processed data (pdata) are not copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._copyparameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._directcomplex-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_directcomplex(raw, td) -> Array{ComplexF64}\n\nTrim the direct-dimension padding (to td real points) and deinterleave the real/imaginary points into a complex array — the im1 quadrature of the directly detected dimension — with td÷2 points along the direct axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._directcomplex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._find_dimension_path","page":"API","text":"_find_dimension_path(key, annotations, prefix=\"\")\n\nFind the dotted path to a key in the annotations dictionary. Returns the path that should match entries in the dimensions array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._find_dimension_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._find_frequency_dim_for_channels-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"_find_frequency_dim_for_channels(spec, channel) -> Union{Int, Nothing}\n\nFind the frequency dimension index that corresponds to the given channel. Channel can be \"f1\", \"f2\", etc. or a nucleus string like \"1H\", \"19F\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._find_frequency_dim_for_channels"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._fnmode-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"Bruker FnMODE for a quadrature scheme\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._fnmode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._groupdelay","page":"API","text":"_groupdelay(acqusdic) -> Float64\n\nThe Bruker digital-filter group delay (points) of the direct dimension: the number of points of filter build-up (\"lead-in\") that precede time zero in the stored FID. The data are not modified; the lead-in stays in the FID at negative times and ft removes the delay with a linear phase ramp after the Fourier transform. Which value applies is set by the acquisition parameters:\n\nDIGMOD = 0 (analogue filtering): no group delay.\nDSPFVS 20–23: GRPDLY. For baseopt acquisitions (DIGMOD = 3) it is an integer and the spectrometer has already corrected the initial points of the FID.\nDSPFVS 10–19 (older data): the delay should be looked up from DECIM and DSPFVS. This is not implemented; GRPDLY is used if valid, otherwise no delay is applied, with a warning.\nDSPFVS outside 10–23: not a firmware version Bruker defines. TopSpin's cryoproc1d, for example, sets DSPFVS = 0 after rewriting the FID with its start removed: the filter build-up and some points after time zero. The number of points removed is found by comparing TD in acqu (the original) and acqus; the points beyond GRPDLY were after time zero, so the stored FID starts that many dwells after t = 0. This is returned as a negative delay, so that ft restores the time origin. A warning reports the missing points. If acqu does not show how many were removed, no delay is applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._groupdelay"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._indirectstorageorder-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_indirectstorageorder(acqusdic, ndim) -> Vector{Int}\n\nReturn the acqus-file indices (2:ndim) of the indirect dimensions, ordered from the fastest-varying (innermost) loop to the slowest (outermost) as stored in the ser file. For 3D this depends on the Bruker AQSEQ parameter: AQSEQ=0 (3-2-1) nests acqu2s inside acqu3s; AQSEQ=1 (3-1-2) swaps them. 1D/2D have a single (or no) indirect dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._indirectstorageorder"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._is_programmatic_pattern-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"_is_programmatic_pattern(value) -> Bool\n\nCheck if a value is a programmatic list pattern. Must be a Dict with either:\n\n\"type\" key and either \"step\" or \"end\" key (linear/log patterns)\n\"counter\" and \"scale\" keys (counter-based pattern)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._is_programmatic_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._isbaseopt-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"baseopt acquisition (DIGMOD = 3): the spectrometer has corrected the initial points of the FID\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._isbaseopt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._mainmetadata-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"assemble the top-level NMRData metadata from the loaded text metadata and the constructed dims\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._mainmetadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._minimalacqus-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"_minimalacqus(dest, spec, axisdims, tds)\n\nWrite minimal acqus/acqu2s/… files into dest describing spec. axisdims are the dimensions in storage order (direct first) and tds the corresponding TD values (points on disk).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._minimalacqus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._modifiedfidoffset-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_modifiedfidoffset(acqusdic, expfolder) -> Int\n\nFor a FID rewritten with its start removed (for example by cryoproc1d), the number of complex points after time zero that were removed: the drop in TD from acqu (the original) to acqus, less the group delay GRPDLY. Zero if this cannot be determined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._modifiedfidoffset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._patchjdx-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"API","text":"_patchjdx(text, pairs) -> String\n\nSet single-valued parameters in the text of a JCAMP-DX parameter file, replacing the existing ##$KEY= value line or inserting one before ##END=.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._patchjdx"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._pdatafiles-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API","text":"_pdatafiles(ndim)\n\nNames of the pdata files of an ndim-dimensional spectrum, in the order of the multicomplex components: file k (counting from 0) has an i for each set bit of k, the direct dimension first, so 2ir is the coefficient of im1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._pdatafiles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._procspars-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"API","text":"procs parameters for dimension d of size n\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._procspars"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._readfid-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_readfid(datafile, acqusdic, nrows) -> Matrix\n\nRead a raw Bruker fid/ser file into a (paddedtd, nrows) matrix of the on-disk numeric type, handling endianness (BYTORDA) and data type (DTYPA). The directly-detected dimension is zero-padded on disk to a block boundary; the padded row length is recovered from the file size and the number of stored FIDs (nrows), so no block-size assumption is needed. Padding is trimmed by the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._readfid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._recombine_axis-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_recombine_axis(y, k, scheme) -> Array{<:Multicomplex}\n\nRecombine the interleaved real/imaginary records of a phase-sensitive indirect dimension (array axis k) into an extra multicomplex order, halving that axis. scheme selects States, States-TPPI or echo/anti-echo (Rance–Kay) recombination. The new imaginary unit encodes that dimension's quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._recombine_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._resolve_parameter-Tuple{String, NMRData}","page":"API","text":"_resolve_parameter(param_str::String, spec::NMRData) -> Union{Nothing, Any}\n\nAttempt to resolve a parameter string using the acqus data. Returns the resolved value or nothing if not a recognized parameter.\n\nHandles parameter patterns (case-insensitive):\n\nIndexed parameters: p1, pl1, d20, cnst8, gpz6, etc.\nNucleus assignments: f1, f2, f3, f4\nAny other parameter names that exist in acqus (including lists)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._resolve_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._resolve_programmatic_pattern-Tuple{String, Dict, Dict, NMRData, Vector}","page":"API","text":"_resolve_programmatic_pattern(key, pattern, root_annotations, spec, dimensions)\n\nResolve a single programmatic list pattern to a vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._resolve_programmatic_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._resolve_programmatic_recursive!-Tuple{Dict, Dict, NMRData, Vector}","page":"API","text":"_resolve_programmatic_recursive!(obj, root_annotations, spec, dimensions)\n\nRecursively traverse annotations to find and resolve programmatic list patterns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._resolve_programmatic_recursive!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._scalefirstpoint!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"scale the t = 0 point of each axis in axistype_pairs (point firstindex[axis], default 1); returns the factor used for each axis\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._scalefirstpoint!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._setmcindex!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"_setmcindex!(spec)\n\nLink each frequency dimension of a loaded spectrum to its imaginary unit (:mcindex), in order: im1 for the direct dimension, im2 for the next frequency dimension, and so on, matching the components of data loaded with allcomponents=true and spectra produced by ft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._setmcindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._sourceexperiment-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"source Bruker experiment folder of spec, or nothing if unknown or missing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._sourceexperiment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._splitunit-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_splitunit(y, k, scheme) -> Array\n\nInverse of _recombine_axis: split the highest imaginary unit of y back into interleaved real/imaginary records along array axis k, doubling that axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._splitunit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._timedim-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"_timedim(aq, di, mcindex) -> TimeDimension\n\nConstruct the TimeDimension at array position di (naming it T1Dim…T4Dim) from a Bruker acquisition-parameter dictionary aq (acqus, acqu2s, …), linking it to imaginary unit mcindex. Frequencies are already in Hz (replacefrequencies! has run). The time axis is t = ((0:td-1) .- delay) / sw, so points of digital-filter lead-in (delay points) have negative times.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._timedim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._wdwpars-Tuple{ExponentialWindow}","page":"API","text":"window function → (WDW, LB, SSB) procs parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._wdwpars"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._writejdx-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"write a minimal JCAMP-DX parameter file from key => value pairs (keys as Bruker names)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._writejdx"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO._writetitle-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"write a pdata title file from the spectrum's title or label\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO._writetitle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.addsampleinfo-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"addsampleinfo(expt::NMRData) -> NMRData\n\nSearch for and load sample metadata from JSON files in the experiment folder. Returns the NMRData object with sample information added to metadata under :sample as an NMRSample.\n\nSample files are JSON files following the schema at https://github.com/waudbylab/nmr-sample-schema. The function matches samples based on creation/ejection timestamps against the experiment date.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadnmr(\"experiment/1/pdata/1\")\nspec = addsampleinfo(spec)  # Load matching sample metadata\nsample(spec, \"notes\")  # Access sample information\n\nSee also sample, hassample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.addsampleinfo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.annotate-Tuple{NMRData}","page":"API","text":"annotate(spec::NMRData) -> NMRData\n\nParse pulse programme annotations and store them in the spectrum metadata. Returns the NMRData object with annotations added to metadata.\n\nAnnotations are embedded within pulse programme comments marked with ;@ at the start of lines, following YAML format with schema version 0.0.2.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadnmr(\"experiment/1/pdata/1\")\nspec = annotate(spec)  # Parse and store annotations\nannotations(spec, \"title\")  # Access parsed annotations\n\nSee also annotations, hasannotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.annotate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.exampledata-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"exampledata(name::String)\n\nLoad example NMR data provided with NMRTools.jl\n\nArguments\n\nname::String : Name of example data set to load. Call exampledata() to list available data.\n\nReturns\n\nNMR data object(s) corresponding to the requested example data set.\n\nExample\n\ndata = exampledata(\"1D_19F_titration\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.exampledata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.findexperiments-Tuple{NMRSample}","page":"API","text":"findexperiments(sample::NMRSample, dir::String; kw...) -> Vector{NMRExperiment}\nfindexperiments(sample::NMRSample, candidates::Vector{NMRExperiment}) -> Vector{NMRExperiment}\n\nFind all experiments matching sample based on the experiment date falling within the sample's creation–ejection timestamp window.\n\nWith a directory path, calls scanexperiments(dir; kw...) then filters.\nWith a pre-scanned Vector{NMRExperiment}, filters without any disk I/O.\n\nSee also: scanexperiments, findsample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.findexperiments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.findsample-Tuple{NMRExperiment}","page":"API","text":"findsample(expt::NMRExperiment) -> Union{NMRSample, Nothing}\nfindsample(expt::NMRExperiment, dir::String) -> Union{NMRSample, Nothing}\nfindsample(expt::NMRExperiment, candidates::Vector{NMRSample}) -> Union{NMRSample, Nothing}\n\nFind the sample that matches expt based on experiment date falling within the sample's creation–ejection timestamp window.\n\nWith no second argument, searches the parent directory of the experiment folder.\nWith a directory path, searches that directory for JSON sample files.\nWith a pre-scanned Vector{NMRSample}, filters without any disk I/O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.findsample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.getformat-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"getformat(filename)\n\nTake an input filename and return either :ucsf, :nmrpipe, :pdata (bruker processed), :bruker_fid (raw fid/ser, handled by loadfid), or :unknown after checking whether the filename matches any known format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.getformat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadbmrb-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"loadbmrb(id::Integer; list=nothing) -> Vector{ChemicalShift}\nloadbmrb(filename; list=nothing) -> Vector{ChemicalShift}\nloadbmrb(id_or_filename, dims...; list=nothing) -> Vector{Peak{N}}\n\nDownload the assigned chemical shifts of BMRB entry id, or read them from a local NMR-STAR (v3) file.\n\nGiven dimensions as well, the shifts are assembled straight into the peak list of an experiment, exactly as peaklist does: H1, N15 for a ¹⁵N HSQC, H1, C13 for a ¹³C HSQC, or atom names such as \"H\", \"N\", \"CO-1\" for an HNCO.\n\nAn entry may hold several shift lists, e.g. under different sample conditions. The first is used unless list selects another by its ID. Shifts of nuclei NMRTools does not know about (such as ¹⁷O) are skipped.\n\nExamples\n\nloadbmrb(15000, H1, N15)                 # ¹⁵N HSQC peak list, side chains included\nloadbmrb(15000, H1, C13)                 # ¹³C HSQC peak list\nloadbmrb(15000, \"H\", \"N\")                # backbone amides only\nloadbmrb(15000, \"CO-1\", \"N\")             # CON\n\nshifts = loadbmrb(15000)                 # the shifts themselves, to filter or reuse\nshifts = loadbmrb(\"bmr15000_3.str\")      # a local NMR-STAR file\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadbmrb"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadfid-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"loadfid(filename; removefilter=true, firstpoint=nothing) -> NMRData\n\nLoad raw Bruker time-domain data (fid for 1D, ser for nD) as an NMRData with Multicomplex elements and TimeDimension axes, ready for processing. filename is the fid or ser file or the experiment folder.\n\nPhase-sensitive indirect dimensions (States, States-TPPI, echo/anti-echo) are combined into extra imaginary units, so a 2D experiment has Multicomplex{Float64,2,4} elements and even 1D data use Multicomplex{Float64,1,2}. Each time dimension records its imaginary unit in :mcindex (T1Dim → 1, T2Dim → 2, …). Non-Fourier dimensions such as a relaxation series become X2Dim/X3Dim with :mcindex == nothing. Intensities match TopSpin's: the raw data are scaled by 2^NC as TopSpin scales them, and ft applies no normalisation.\n\nKeywords\n\nremovefilter: account for the digital filter's group delay. The filter's build-up points stay in the FID, on a time axis that starts GRPDLY dwell times before zero, and the delay is recorded in the direct dimension's :grpdly metadata. ft removes it with a linear phase ramp, as TopSpin and NMRPipe do. With false, the time axis starts at zero and no delay is recorded.\nfirstpoint: scaling factor for the t = 0 point of each time dimension, as a number for all dimensions or a dictionary keyed by dimension type, for example Dict(T2Dim => 1.0). The default is 0.5, except in the direct dimension of baseopt data (DIGMOD = 3), where the spectrometer already gives the t = 0 point its half weight, and 1.0 is used.\n\nFIDs modified by TopSpin's cryoproc1d (DSPFVS outside 10–23) have had their first points removed. loadfid compares TD in acqu and acqus to find how many points after t = 0 are missing, starts the time axis there, and warns. The missing points leave a ripple around strong lines; linearpredict with direction=:backward can rebuild them.\n\nAnnotations (annotate) and sample metadata (addsampleinfo) are applied as for loadnmr.\n\nExamples\n\nfid = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\")\nspec = fid |> apodize(Cos2Window()) |> zerofill |> ft |> autophase |> realest\n\nSee also autoprocess, savefid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadfid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadjdx-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"loadjdx(filename::String) -> Dict{Symbol, Any}\n\nLoad a JCAMP-DX (.jdx) file and return a dictionary of parsed parameters.\n\nPower levels are converted to Power objects, durations to seconds, and frequency lists to FQList objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadjdx"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadmetadata-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"loadmetadata(experimentfolder) -> Dict{Symbol,Any}\n\nLoad all text-based metadata from a Bruker experiment folder without reading any binary data (ser, fid, 2rr, etc.). Returns a metadata dictionary compatible with NMRData metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadmetadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmr-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"loadnmr(filename, experimentfolder=nothing)\n\nMain function for loading NMR data. experimentfolder contains the path to an experiment directory, for identification of metadata, if the filename is not directly within an experiment.\n\nReturns an NMRData structure, or throws an NMRToolsError is there is a problem.\n\nExamples\n\nnmrPipe import:\n\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_1H/1/test.ft1\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/test.ft1\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/2D_HN/1/test.ft2\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/pseudo2D_XSTE/1/test.ft1\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/pseudo3D_HN_R2/1/ft/test%03d.ft2\");\n\nBruker pdata import:\n\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/pdata/1\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/pdata/1/\");\n\nucsf (Sparky) import:\n\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/2D_HN/1/hmqc.ucsf\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/pdata/1\");\nloadnmr(\"exampledata/1D_19F/1/pdata/1/\");\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmr-Tuple{NMRExperiment}","page":"API","text":"loadnmr(expt::NMRExperiment; pdatadir=1, allcomponents=false) -> NMRData\n\nLoad the full NMR spectrum for an experiment previously discovered by scanexperiments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmrpipe1d-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"loadnmrpipe1d(filename, md, mdax)\n\nReturn an NMRData array containing spectrum and associated metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmrpipe1d"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmrpipe2d-Tuple{String, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"loadnmrpipe2d(filename, md, mdax)\n\nReturn NMRData containing spectrum and associated metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmrpipe2d"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmrpipe3d-Tuple{String, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"loadnmrpipe3d(filename, md, mdax)\n\nReturn NMRData containing spectrum and associated metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadnmrpipe3d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadpdata","page":"API","text":"loadpdata(filename, allcomponents=false)\n\nLoad processed Bruker NMR data from a pdata folder.\n\nArguments\n\nfilename: Path to a pdata directory or a file within it\nallcomponents: If true, returns Multicomplex data with all components, one imaginary unit per frequency dimension (im1 for the direct dimension), for example 2rr + 2ir*im1 + 2ri*im2 + 2ii*im1*im2 for 2D data (TopSpin's 2ir is imaginary in the direct dimension, 2ri in the indirect one). Default is false (returns real data only).\n\nImaginary components are conjugated relative to TopSpin's files, so that loaded data follow the same convention as spectra produced by ft: TopSpin's PHC0/PHC1 applied with phase give the same spectrum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadpdata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.loadpeaks-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"loadpeaks(filename) -> Vector{Peak{N}}\nloadpeaks(filename, spec) -> Vector{Peak{N}}\n\nRead a peak list: either a peaklist.csv as written by savepeaks and by NMRAnalysis.jl, a hand-made list of label x y lines, or a Sparky .list file.\n\nCSV files are matched by column name, so column order and extra columns do not matter. Linewidths and intensities are read where present and are otherwise NaN. A list in which peaks were tracked plane by plane (with a plane column filled in) is read as one position per peak, taking each peak's first row.\n\nSparky lists name their dimensions w1, w2, … with the indirect dimension first, which is the reverse of NMRTools' order. Passing the spectrum spec lets the axis order be checked against where the shifts actually fall, so a list written the other way round is still read correctly.\n\nExamples\n\npks = loadpeaks(\"out/peaklist.csv\")\npks = loadpeaks(\"hsqc.list\", spec)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.loadpeaks"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parse_annotations-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"parse_annotations(content::String) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nParse pulse programme annotations from content string. Annotations are embedded  within pulse programme comments marked with ;@ at the start of lines.\n\nThe function:\n\nFilters out lines starting with ;@\nStrips the ;@ prefix from each line\nParses the resulting YAML document into a dictionary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parse_annotations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parsefqlist-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"parsefqlist(lines)\n\nReturn contents of the specified fqlist.\n\nfqlists can have several different formats:\n\nfirst line   | reference   | unit\n\n         | sfo         | Hz\n\nsfo hz       | sfo         | Hz sfo ppm      | sfo         | ppm bf hz        | bf          | Hz bf ppm       | bf          | ppm p            | sfo         | ppm P            | bf          | ppm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parsefqlist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parsenmrpipeheader-Tuple{Vector{Float32}}","page":"API","text":"parsenmrpipeheader(header)\n\nPass a 512 x 4 byte array containing an nmrPipe header file, and returns dictionaries of metadata. The nmrPipe header format is defined in fdatap.h.\n\nReturn values\n\nmd: dictionary of spectrum metadata\nmdax: array of dictionaries containing axis metadata\n\nExamples\n\nmd, mdax = parsenmrpipeheader(header)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parsenmrpipeheader"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parsepulseprogramlists!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Example lines to parse: define list<gradient> EA=<EA> define list<gradient> EA3 = { 1.0000 0.8750 } define list<pulse> taulist = <$VPLIST> define list<power> powerlist = <$VALIST> define list<gradient> diff=<Difframp> define list<frequency> F19sat = <$FQ1LIST>\n\nNB gradient files have weird syntax!!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parsepulseprogramlists!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parseucsfaxisheader-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"parse ucsf axis header into a dictionary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parseucsfaxisheader"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parseucsfheader-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"parse ucsf file header into a dictionary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parseucsfheader"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parsevalist-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"return valist contents as Powers\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parsevalist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parsevdlist-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"return vdlist contents in seconds\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parsevdlist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.parsevplist-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"return vplist contents in seconds\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.parsevplist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.replacedurations!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"replacedurations!(dic)\n\nEnsure all durations in the acqus dictionary are in seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.replacedurations!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.replacepowers!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"replacepowers!(dic)\n\nEnsure all powers in the acqus dictionary are stored as Power types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.replacepowers!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.resolve_parameter_references!-Tuple{Dict, NMRData}","page":"API","text":"resolve_parameter_references!(annotations::Dict{String, Any}, spec::NMRData)\n\nRecursively traverse the annotations dictionary and resolve parameter references. References are bare parameter names like p1, pl1, d20, VALIST, etc.\n\nParameters are resolved using the acqus() function to get values from the acqus file.\n\nArguments\n\nannotations::Dict{String, Any}: Annotations dictionary to modify in-place\nspec::NMRData: NMRData object containing acqus metadata\n\nExamples\n\n# Before resolution: {\"pulse\": \"p1\", \"power\": \"pl1\", \"duration\": \"VDLIST\"}\n# After resolution: {\"pulse\": 9.2, \"power\": Power(...), \"duration\": [0.01, 0.02, ...]}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.resolve_parameter_references!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.resolve_programmatic_lists!-Tuple{Dict, NMRData}","page":"API","text":"resolve_programmatic_lists!(annotations::Dict{String, Any}, spec::NMRData)\n\nResolve programmatic list patterns in annotations to actual vectors.\n\nProgrammatic lists are specified as dictionaries with one of two patterns:\n\nPattern 1: Type-based (linear/log)\n\ntype: \"linear\" or \"log\" (logarithmic)\nstart: starting value\nstep: step size (for linear with start/step)\nend: ending value (for linear or log with start/end)\n\nPattern 2: Counter-based\n\ncounter: array of values (typically integers from a loop counter)\nscale: scale factor to multiply each counter element by\n\nFor type-based patterns, the function:\n\nIdentifies fields containing programmatic list patterns ({type, start, step/end})\nFinds the dimension number for that field from the dimensions annotation\nGets the number of points in that dimension\nReplaces the pattern with a correctly sized vector\n\nFor counter-based patterns, the function:\n\nMultiplies each element of the counter array by the scale value\nReturns the resulting vector\n\nExamples\n\n# Linear spacing with start/step\n# {type: linear, start: p9, step: p9} with 10 points → [p9, 2*p9, 3*p9, ...]\n\n# Linear spacing with start/end\n# {type: linear, start: 0.001, end: 0.1} with 10 points → [0.001, 0.012, ..., 0.1]\n\n# Logarithmic spacing with start/end\n# {type: log, start: 0.001, end: 0.1} with 10 points → [0.001, 0.0016, ..., 0.1]\n\n# Counter-based pattern\n# {counter: [0, 1, 2, 3], scale: 0.01} → [0.0, 0.01, 0.02, 0.03]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.resolve_programmatic_lists!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.savefid-Tuple{Any, NMRData}","page":"API","text":"savefid(path, spec; dtype=Float64, overwrite=false) -> String\nsavefid(path; kwargs...)\n\nWrite time-domain data as a raw Bruker experiment in the folder path, as fid (1D) or ser (nD) with acquisition parameter files, and return the path of the data file.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nspec is time-domain data such as that from loadfid, possibly after time-domain processing such as linearpredict. The changes loadfid makes are undone, so the file has the layout TopSpin expects: t = 0 points are unscaled, the 2^NC intensity scaling is removed, phase-sensitive indirect dimensions are re-encoded with their original quadrature scheme (States, States-TPPI or echo/anti-echo), and the dimensions are put back in acquisition order. Digital-filter build-up points stay in the data, so they are written back unchanged. loadfid on the written experiment returns the original data.\n\nParameter files (acqus, acqu2s, the pulse programme, lists, …) are copied from the experiment the data were loaded from, with TD, DTYPA, BYTORDA and GRPDLY updated. Without a source experiment, minimal parameter files are generated from the dimension metadata.\n\nKeywords\n\ndtype: Float64 (DTYPA = 2, as written by Avance NEO spectrometers) or Int32 (DTYPA = 0, readable by older TopSpin versions; data are rounded and must fit in 32 bits).\noverwrite: replace existing data in path.\n\nExamples\n\nfid = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\")\nsavefid(\"extended/1\", linearpredict(fid, T2Dim))\n\nSee also savenmr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.savefid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.savenmr-Tuple{Any, NMRData}","page":"API","text":"savenmr(path, spec; procno=1, overwrite=false) -> String\nsavenmr(path; kwargs...)\n\nWrite a frequency-domain spectrum as Bruker processed data, and return the processed-data folder. path is an experiment folder (data go in path/pdata/<procno>) or a processed-data folder …/pdata/<n>. 1D, 2D and 3D spectra are supported, including pseudo-dimensions.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nThe real part is written to 1r, 2rr or 3rrr. For multicomplex 1D and 2D spectra the imaginary parts are written too (1i; 2ir, 2ri, 2ii), conjugated to TopSpin's sign convention. Data are stored as 32-bit integers, with the NC_proc exponent chosen to keep full precision. The processing parameter files, both current (proc, proc2, …) and status (procs, proc2s, …), record the axes, the window function and the phase correction applied (PHC0, PHC1).\n\nIf the experiment folder has no acquisition parameters, they are copied from the experiment the spectrum was loaded from or, failing that, generated from the dimension metadata, so that loadnmr can read the result.\n\nKeywords\n\nprocno: processed-data number, used when path is an experiment folder.\noverwrite: replace existing processed data.\n\nExamples\n\nspec = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\") |> autoprocess\nsavenmr(\"myexperiment/1\", spec; procno=2)\n\nSee also savefid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.savenmr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.savepeaks-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Peak}}","page":"API","text":"savepeaks(filename, peaks)\nsavepeaks(filename)\n\nWrite a peak list. A filename ending in .list is written in Sparky format; anything else is written as a peaklist.csv that NMRAnalysis.jl reads directly, with positions in ppm, linewidths in Hz and intensities at full precision.\n\nThe forms without the data argument return a function for use with |>.\n\nPeak labels identify peaks in NMRAnalysis, so they must be unique and may not contain commas. Unlabelled peaks are written as X1, X2, … by their position in the list.\n\nExamples\n\npks = detectpeaks(spec)\nsavepeaks(\"peaklist.csv\", pks)\nsavepeaks(\"hsqc.list\", pks)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.savepeaks"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.scanexperiments-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"scanexperiments(dir; recursive=true, pdatadir=1, warn=true) -> Vector{NMRExperiment}\n\nScan dir for Bruker NMR experiment folders and return a vector of lightweight NMRExperiment descriptors sorted by experiment date (oldest first).\n\nA folder is identified as a Bruker experiment if it contains an acqus file. Once identified, the scanner does not recurse into it, so pdata/ subdirectories are never misidentified as experiments.\n\nArguments\n\ndir: Root directory to scan.\nrecursive: If true (default), search subdirectories. If false, only check immediate children of dir.\npdatadir: Only include experiments that have a pdata/<pdatadir>/ subdirectory, i.e. where loadnmr would succeed. Pass nothing to include all experiments regardless of whether processed data exists.\nwarn: If true (default), warn about folders that cannot be parsed.\n\nExamples\n\nexpts = scanexperiments(\"/nmr/projects/lysozyme/\")\n\n# Filter with standard Julia\nhsqc = filter(e -> e[:pulseprogram] == \"hsqcetfpf3gpsi2\", expts)\ncest = filter(e -> \"cest\" in get(annotations(e), \"experiment_type\", []), expts)\n\n# Load one\nspec = loadnmr(expts[1])\n\nSee also: NMRExperiment, scansamples, findexperiments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.scanexperiments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.scansamples-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"scansamples(dir; recursive=false) -> Vector{NMRSample}\n\nScan dir for NMR sample JSON files and return a vector of NMRSample descriptors sorted by creation timestamp. Files that cannot be parsed or have an unsupported schema version are skipped silently.\n\nBy default only the immediate directory is searched (not subdirectories), since sample files are typically stored flat in a single directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.scansamples"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.sumexpts-Tuple","page":"API","text":"sumexpts(inputexpts...; out, weights=[])\n\nSum a collection of Bruker NMR experiments, with optional weighting factors. Data will be truncated to fit the shortest data file.\n\nArguments\n\ninputexpts...: Input experiment names (strings or integers)\nout: Output experiment name (string or integer)\nweights: Optional vector of weights for each input experiment\n\nDetails\n\nExperiment names should be either strings with filenames or integers converted to strings\nIf weights are supplied, the length must match the number of input experiments\nFirst input experiment is used as a template for the output experiment\nFor 1D experiments, 'fid' files are added; for nD experiments, 'ser' files are added\nProcessed data files in pdata/1 are removed, other pdata subdirectories are deleted\nUpdates the title file with information about the summed experiments\nUpdates the number of scans in the acqus file to be the sum of individual experiments\nPrompts before overwriting any existing experiment\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.sumexpts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.topspinversion-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"parsetopspinversion(acqusfilename)\n\nReturn the TopSpin version as a VersionNumber (e.g. v\"4.2.0\").\n\nThis is obtained from the end of the first line of the acqus file, e.g.\n\n##TITLE= Parameter file, TopSpin 4.2.0\n##TITLE= Parameter file, TOPSPIN\t\tVersion 2.1\n##TITLE= Parameter file, TOPSPIN\t\tVersion 3.2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.topspinversion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.withreference-Tuple{FQList, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"withreference(f::FQList, channel) -> FQList\n\nReturn a copy of frequency list f with its reference base and carrier frequencies (:bf, :sfo) populated from a channel dictionary (see channels). This makes the list self-describing, so it can be converted to ppm or Hz without a detected frequency axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.withreference"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.SchemaMigrate","page":"API","text":"Migrate NMR sample metadata to the latest schema version.\n\nCall loadsample(path, migrationspath=MIGRATIONSPATH[]) to load a JSON file and apply migrations. It returns the migrated data as a Dict.\n\nCall updatetolatestschema!(data) with a parsed JSON Dict. It modifies the dict in place and returns it.\n\nThe migration patch file is expected at current/patch.json relative to this module. Override by setting MIGRATIONSPATH[] or passing migrationspath.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.SchemaMigrate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.SchemaMigrate.loadsample","page":"API","text":"loadsample(path, migrations_path=MIGRATIONS_PATH[]) -> Dict\n\nLoad a JSON file from the given path and apply migrations to update it to the latest schema version. Returns the migrated data as a Dict.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.SchemaMigrate.loadsample"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NMRTools.NMRIO.SchemaMigrate.updatetolatestschema!","page":"API","text":"updatetolatestschema!(data, migrations_path=MIGRATIONS_PATH[]) -> Dict\n\nApply migrations to the given data Dict to update it to the latest schema version. The data is modified in place and returned. Migrations are loaded from the given migrationspath (default is MIGRATIONSPATH[]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NMRTools.NMRIO.SchemaMigrate.updatetolatestschema!"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#1D-diffusion-analysis","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"Let's analyse a 15N-edited XSTE measurement of translational diffusion. This experiment was acquired as a single pseudo-2D measurement, with gradient strengths ranging from 2 to 98% of the maximum strength, 0.55 T/m.\n\nFirst, we need to load the required packages. We will use LsqFit for the non-linear least squares fitting, Measurements to handle uncertainties, and Statistics for calculation of means and standard deviations.\n\nusing NMRTools\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LsqFit\nusing Measurements\nusing Statistics\n\nspec = exampledata(\"pseudo2D_XSTE\")","title":"1D diffusion analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#Set-up-parameters","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"The file we have just loaded has an UnknownDimension as the non-frequency dimension. We need to replace this with a GradientDimension and set the gradient strengths that were used. We do this with the setgradientlist function:\n\ngradients = LinRange(0.02, 0.98, size(spec, X2Dim))\nGmax = 0.55 # T/m\n\nspec = setgradientlist(spec, gradients, Gmax)\n\nNext, we extract or set other acquisition parameters required for analysis. In particular, we extract the diffusion pulse length, δ, and the diffusion delay, Δ, from the acqus file. We also specify the chemical shift ranges used for plotting, fitting, and for determination of the noise level.\n\nδ = acqus(spec, :p, 30) * 2    # gradient pulse length = p30/2\nΔ = acqus(spec, :d, 20)        # diffusion delay = d20\nσ = 0.9                        # gradient pulse shape factor (for SMSQ10)\n\ncoherence = SQ(H1)             # coherence for diffusion encoding\nγ = gyromagneticratio(coherence)            # calculate effective gyromagnetic ratio\n\ng = data(spec, G2Dim)          # list of gradient strengths\n\n# select chemical shift ranges for plotting and fitting\nplotrange = 6 .. 10 # ppm\ndatarange = 7.7 .. 8.6 # ppm\nnoiseposition = 10.5 # ppm\nnothing # hide","title":"Set up parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#Plot-the-data","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"To take a quick look at the data, we can plot the experiment either as 3D lines using the waterfall style, or as a heatmap:\n\nfig = Figure()\nnmrplot(fig[1, 1], spec[plotrange, :]; style=:waterfall)\nnmrplot(fig[1, 2], spec[plotrange, :]; style=:heatmap)\nsave(\"tutorial-diffusion-plot.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plot the data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#Calculate-noise-and-peak-integrals","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"Now, we can determine the measurement noise, by taking the standard deviation of integrals across the different gradient points:\n\n# create a selector for the noise, matching the width of the data range\nnoisewidth = datarange.right - datarange.left\nnoiserange = (noiseposition-0.5noisewidth)..(noiseposition+0.5noisewidth)\n\n# integrate over the noise regions and take the standard deviation\n# (calculate the sum over the frequency dimension F1Dim, and use\n# `data` to convert from NMRData to a regular array)\nnoise = sum(spec[noiserange,:], dims=F1Dim) |> data |> std\n\n# calculate the integral of the data region similarly, using vec to convert to a list\nintegrals = sum(spec[datarange,:], dims=F1Dim) |> data |> vec\n\n# normalise noise and integrals by the maximum value\nnoise /= maximum(integrals)\nintegrals /= maximum(integrals)\nnothing # hide","title":"Calculate noise and peak integrals"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#Fitting","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"Now, we can fit the data to the Stejskal-Tanner equation using the LsqFit package.\n\n# model parameters are (I0, D) - scale D by 1e-10 for a nicer numerical value\nmodel(g, p) = p[1] * exp.(-(γ*δ*σ*g).^2 .* (Δ - δ/3) .* p[2] .* 1e-10)\n\np0 = [1.0, 1.0] # rough guess of initial parameters\n\nfit = curve_fit(model, g, integrals, p0) # run the fit\n\n# extract the fit parameters and standard errors\npfit = coef(fit)\nerr = stderror(fit)\nD = (pfit[2] ± err[2]) * 1e-10","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#Plot-the-results","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"Finally, plot the results:\n\nx = LinRange(0, maximum(g)*1.1, 100)\nyfit = model(x, pfit)\n\nfig = Figure()\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1],\n    xlabel=\"G (T m⁻¹)\",\n    ylabel=\"Integrated signal\",\n    title=\"\",\n    xgridvisible=false,\n    ygridvisible=false)\nscatter!(ax1, g, integrals; label=\"observed\")\nerrorbars!(ax1, g, integrals, fill(noise, length(g)))\nlines!(ax1, x, yfit; label=\"fit\")\naxislegend(ax1; position=:rt)\nylims!(ax1, 0, nothing)\n\nap2 = nmrplot(fig[1, 2], spec[plotrange, 1]; title=\"\", color=:lightgray)\nnmrplot!(ap2.axis, spec[datarange, 1]; color=:red)\nhlines!(ap2.axis, [0.0]; color=:grey)\n\nsave(\"tutorial-diffusion-fit.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plot the results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diffusion/#Estimating-the-hydrodynamic-radius","page":"Diffusion analysis","text":"We can use the known viscosity of water as a function of temperature to estimate the hydrodynamic radius from the measured diffusion coefficient. First, we extract the temperature from the spectrum metadata:\n\nT = acqus(spec, :te)\n\nNext, we can create a little function to calculate viscosity for H2O or D2O solvents as a function of temperature:\n\nfunction viscosity(solvent, T)\n\tif solvent==:h2o\n        A = 802.25336\n        a = 3.4741e-3\n        b = -1.7413e-5\n        c = 2.7719e-8\n        gamma = 1.53026\n        T0 = 225.334\n    elseif solvent==:d2o\n        A = 885.60402\n        a = 2.799e-3\n        b = -1.6342e-5\n        c = 2.9067e-8\n        gamma = 1.55255\n        T0 = 231.832\n    else\n        @error \"solvent not recognised (should be :h2o or :d2o)\"\n    end\n\n    DT = T - T0\n    k = 1.38e-23\n\t\n    return A * (DT + a*DT^2 + b*DT^3 + c*DT^4)^(-gamma)\nend\nη = viscosity(:h2o, T)\n\nFinally, we can use the Stokes-Einstein equation to calculate the hydrodynamic radius:\n\nk = 1.38e-23\nrH = k*T / (6π * η * 0.001 * D) * 1e9 # in nm","title":"Estimating the hydrodynamic radius"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/peaks/#Peaks-and-peak-lists","page":"Peaks and peak lists","text":"NMRTools represents a peak with the Peak{N} type: a position in ppm, a height, a linewidth in Hz and a label. A peak list is simply a Vector{Peak{N}}, so the usual tools for vectors (sorting, filtering, indexing, push! and deleteat!) all work, and a peak list is also a Tables.jl table, so DataFrame(pks) works directly.\n\nPeak lists are written in the peaklist.csv format used by NMRAnalysis.jl, so peaks picked here can be loaded straight into an NMRAnalysis analysis, and vice versa.","title":"Peaks and peak lists"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/peaks/#Detecting-peaks","page":"Peaks and peak lists","text":"detectpeaks finds the peaks in a 1D, 2D or higher-dimensional spectrum:\n\nusing NMRTools, CairoMakie\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\npks = detectpeaks(spec; sign=:positive)\n\nA peak is a local maximum whose height exceeds a threshold, by default ten times the noise level. Positions and heights are refined beyond the digital resolution by parabolic interpolation, and the linewidth in each dimension is estimated from the half-height crossings. Peaks are returned strongest first and labelled X1, X2, … to match the labels NMRAnalysis gives new peaks.\n\nThe threshold can be set relative to the noise or absolutely, and negative peaks can be included or picked on their own:\n\ndetectpeaks(spec; snr=20)              # only peaks above 20σ\ndetectpeaks(spec; threshold=1e6)       # an absolute threshold\ndetectpeaks(spec; sign=:negative)      # negative peaks only; the default is :both\n\nTo pick peaks in a pseudo-2D experiment, index one plane first, e.g. detectpeaks(spec[:, 1]).\n\nLike the processing functions, detectpeaks, register and savepeaks have forms for use in a pipeline:\n\npks = loadfid(\"hsqc/1\") |> autoprocess |> realest |> detectpeaks(snr=20)\npks |> savepeaks(\"peaklist.csv\")\nloadbmrb(15000, H1, N15) |> register(spec) |> savepeaks(\"bmrb.csv\")","title":"Detecting peaks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/peaks/#Plotting-peaks","page":"Peaks and peak lists","text":"nmrplot! overlays a peak list on a spectrum. 2D peaks are drawn as labelled crosses:\n\nfig, ax, _ = nmrplot(spec)\nnmrplot!(pks)\nfig\n\nand 1D peaks as vertical lines, with their labels along the top of the axis:\n\nspec1d = exampledata(\"1D_19F\")\nfig, ax, _ = nmrplot(spec1d; xlims=(-123, -124.5))\nnmrplot!(detectpeaks(spec1d))\nfig\n\nWith Plots.jl, plot!(pks) does the same.","title":"Plotting peaks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/peaks/#Working-with-peaks","page":"Peaks and peak lists","text":"Use the accessor functions rather than reaching into the fields:\n\np = pks[1]\nppm(p)            # position, ordered like the spectrum's dimensions\n\nlinewidth(p)      # full width at half maximum in each dimension, in Hz\n\nintensity(p) gives the height and label(p) the label. Linewidths are kept in Hz because they mean the same thing on every nucleus and at every field; the corresponding relaxation rate is π * linewidth(p, n). A quantity that is not known, such as the linewidth of a peak read from a Sparky list, is NaN.\n\nPeaks are immutable, so changing a peak means making a new one. Peak(p; ...) copies a peak with any of its fields replaced, which makes assignment straightforward:\n\npks[1] = Peak(pks[1]; label=\"G10\")\npks[1]\n\nPeaks can also be created by hand, or snapped onto the nearest maximum in a spectrum with findpeak:\n\nfindpeak(spec, (8.5, 120.0); label=\"L23\")\n\nSorting and filtering use the same accessors:\n\nsort(pks; by=intensity, rev=true)           # strongest first\nsort(pks; by=p -> ppm(p, 1), rev=true)      # left to right\nfilter(p -> 7.5 < ppm(p, 1) < 9, pks)       # a region of the spectrum","title":"Working with peaks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/peaks/#Saving-and-loading","page":"Peaks and peak lists","text":"savepeaks writes a peaklist.csv that NMRAnalysis.jl reads directly, and loadpeaks reads it back, along with lists written by NMRAnalysis and simple hand-made lists of label x y lines:\n\nsavepeaks(\"peaklist.csv\", pks)\npks = loadpeaks(\"peaklist.csv\")\n\nThe file has one row per peak, with units in the column names:\n\n# NMRTools.jl peak list\nlabel,plane,x (ppm),y (ppm),lwx (Hz),lwy (Hz),intensity\nG10,,8.5013,120.371,23.1,14.2,2.53e7\n\nx is the direct dimension and y the indirect one. The blank plane column means the position applies to every plane of a series, which is how NMRAnalysis reads it. Labels identify peaks in NMRAnalysis, so they must be unique; unlabelled peaks are written as X1, X2, ….\n\nSparky .list files are written and read too. Sparky lists the indirect dimension first, which is the reverse of NMRTools' order. Passing the spectrum to loadpeaks checks the order against where the shifts actually fall, so a list written the other way round is still read correctly:\n\nsavepeaks(\"hsqc.list\", pks)\npks = loadpeaks(\"hsqc.list\", spec)","title":"Saving and loading"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/peaks/#Registering-a-peak-list-onto-a-spectrum","page":"Peaks and peak lists","text":"A peak list made for one spectrum rarely lines up exactly with another, because of differences in referencing, temperature or sample conditions. This applies to a list carried over from an earlier spectrum of the same sample, and even more to one predicted from assigned chemical shifts (see Assigned chemical shifts from the BMRB). register! finds the single offset that best matches the list to the observed peaks and moves the list by it:\n\nregister!(pks, spec)                        # reports the offset applied\nregister!(pks, spec; maxshift=(0.3, 2.0))   # limit the search, in ppm per dimension\n\nEvery offset within maxshift (by default 10% of the spectral width) is tried, scoring how much signal lies under the shifted peaks. Absolute intensities are used, so negative peaks count as much as positive ones, and the score grows only logarithmically with intensity, so a single very strong peak cannot outweigh many weaker matches. register returns a moved copy instead. Only a uniform translation is applied; individual peaks are not moved.","title":"Registering a peak list onto a spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#NMR-Pulse-Programme-Semantic-Annotation-System","page":"Annotations","text":"warning: Alpha Development Status\nThis annotation system is in early alpha development (currently v0.0.2). The schema, syntax, and functionality are subject to significant changes. Use with caution in production environments.","title":"NMR Pulse Programme Semantic Annotation System"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Motivation","page":"Annotations","text":"Modern NMR pulse programmes are cryptic text files that encode complex experimental procedures, but they lack semantic information about what they actually measure. Analysis software must guess at the meaning of parameters like VALIST or VPLIST, leading to errors and requiring manual interpretation.\n\nThis annotation system embeds structured semantic metadata directly within pulse programme files as comments, enabling:\n\nAutomated Analysis: Software can automatically understand experimental structure\nReproducible Research: Clear provenance and citation information\nKnowledge Preservation: Experimental design captured alongside implementation\nCross-Platform Compatibility: Vendor-agnostic semantic layer\nCollaborative Development: Track contributors and development status\n\nnote: Current Implementation\nBasic annotation parsing is implemented in NMRTools.jl. The system is under active development with ongoing refinement of the schema and parsing capabilities.","title":"Motivation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Design-Principles","page":"Annotations","text":"","title":"Design Principles"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Human-and-Machine-Readable","page":"Annotations","text":"Annotations use simple comment syntax that NMR spectroscopists can read naturally whilst being structured enough for automated parsing.","title":"Human and Machine Readable"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Embedded-and-Immutable","page":"Annotations","text":"Metadata travels with the pulse programme file and cannot drift out of sync with the code.","title":"Embedded and Immutable"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Modular-and-Extensible","page":"Annotations","text":"Experiments are described as combinations of core types and features, allowing new variations without redefining the entire taxonomy.","title":"Modular and Extensible"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Version-Controlled","page":"Annotations","text":"Both individual pulse programmes and the annotation system itself are versioned for reproducibility and compatibility.","title":"Version Controlled"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Annotation-Syntax","page":"Annotations","text":"","title":"Annotation Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Basic-Format","page":"Annotations","text":"Annotations are extensions of Bruker comment lines, marked with ;@. Within these lines, annotations are written in YAML format. The initial ;@ will be stripped when parsing, permitting multi-line entries.\n\n;@ parameter: text value\n;@ parameter1: 0.123\n;@ parameter2: [list, of, items]\n;@ parameter3: {associative: array, key: value}\n;@ parameter4: |\n;@   This is\n;@   a multi-line\n;@   entry.","title":"Basic Format"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Parameter-References","page":"Annotations","text":"Parameter and list variable names are referenced directly without special syntax:\n\n;@ r1rho:\n;@   power: VALIST      # List variable\n;@   duration: VPLIST   # List variable  \n;@   offset: cnst28     # Constant parameter\n\nThe context distinguishes parameter names (letters) from literal values (numbers). NMRTools automatically resolves these references to actual values from the acqus file when loading data.","title":"Parameter References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Dimension-References","page":"Annotations","text":"Dimensions use dotted path notation that references into experiment-specific parameter blocks:\n\n;@ dimensions: [r1rho.power, r1rho.duration, f1]\n;@ r1rho:\n;@   power: VALIST\n;@   duration: VPLIST\n\nThis creates an explicit connection: r1rho.power refers to the power field in the r1rho block, which is controlled by the VALIST parameter.","title":"Dimension References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Using-Annotations-in-NMRTools.jl","page":"Annotations","text":"The complete annotation schema is defined in the pulse programmes repository. See the schema documentation and controlled vocabulary for details.\n\nIn NMRTools.jl, parsed annotation data is accessible via the :annotations metadata field or the annotations() convenience function.","title":"Using Annotations in NMRTools.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Accessing-annotation-data","page":"Annotations","text":"Annotations can be accessed using the annotations() function, which provides convenient nested access to annotation data. Dotted notation is automatically expanded:\n\n# Load a spectrum with annotations\nspec = loadnmr(\"path/to/annotated/experiment\")\n\n# Access all annotations\nall_annotations = annotations(spec)\n\n# Access specific annotation fields\nexperiment_type = annotations(spec, \"experiment_type\")\n\n# Access nested dictionary fields - these are equivalent:\nr1rho_duration = annotations(spec, \"r1rho\", \"duration\")\nr1rho_duration = annotations(spec, \"r1rho.duration\")\n\n# Access array elements by index\nfirst_dimension = annotations(spec, \"dimensions\", 1)\n\n# Deep nesting with dotted notation\ncalibration_start = annotations(spec, \"calibration.duration.start\")\n\nThe annotations() function accepts both string and symbol keys, and returns nothing if the requested field does not exist.","title":"Accessing annotation data"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Helper-Functions","page":"Annotations","text":"","title":"Helper Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#referencepulse(spec,-nucleus)","page":"Annotations","text":"Get the reference pulse calibration for a given nucleus:\n\n# Get 19F reference pulse (returns tuple of pulse length and power)\np1, pl1 = referencepulse(spec, \"19F\")\n\n# Get 1H reference pulse\np3, pl2 = referencepulse(spec, :1H)\n\nReturns nothing if no reference pulse is found for the specified nucleus.","title":"referencepulse(spec, nucleus)"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Example:-19F-R1ρ-on-resonance-experiment","page":"Annotations","text":"This example experiment is annotated as follows (schema v0.0.2):\n\n;@ schema_version: \"0.0.2\"\n;@ sequence_version: \"0.1.0\"\n;@ title: 19F on-resonance R1rho relaxation dispersion\n;@ authors:\n;@   - Chris Waudby <c.waudby@ucl.ac.uk>\n;@   - Jan Overbeck\n;@ created: 2020-01-01\n;@ last_modified: 2025-11-15\n;@ repository: github.com/waudbylab/pulseprograms\n;@ status: beta\n;@ experiment_type: [r1rho, 1d]\n;@ features: [on_resonance, temperature_compensation]\n;@ typical_nuclei: [19F, 1H]\n;@ citation:\n;@   - Overbeck (2020)\n;@ dimensions: [r1rho.power, r1rho.duration, f1]\n;@ acquisition_order: [f1, r1rho.duration, r1rho.power]\n;@ reference_pulse:\n;@   - {channel: f1, pulse: p1, power: pl1}\n;@   - {channel: f2, pulse: p3, power: pl2}\n;@ r1rho:\n;@   channel: f1\n;@   power: VALIST\n;@   duration: VPLIST\n;@   offset: 0\n;@   alignment: hard_pulse\n\nUsing annotated data, we can access the experiment metadata:\n\nusing NMRTools\n\n# Load the annotated experiment\nspec = loadnmr(\"path/to/experiment\")\n\n# Access experiment metadata\nannotations(spec, \"title\")           # \"19F on-resonance R1rho relaxation dispersion\"\nannotations(spec, \"experiment_type\") # [\"r1rho\", \"1d\"]\nannotations(spec, \"features\")        # [\"on_resonance\", \"temperature_compensation\"]\n\n# Access nested r1rho parameters (dotted notation works)\nannotations(spec, \"r1rho.power\")     # Vector of power values resolved from VALIST\nannotations(spec, \"r1rho.duration\")  # Vector of duration values resolved from VPLIST\nannotations(spec, \"r1rho\", \"channel\") # \"f1\"\nannotations(spec, \"r1rho\", \"offset\")  # 0 (on-resonance)\n\n# Access dimension information\nannotations(spec, \"dimensions\")       # [\"r1rho.power\", \"r1rho.duration\", \"f1\"]\nannotations(spec, \"dimensions\", 1)    # \"r1rho.power\"\n\n# Get reference pulse calibration\np1, pl1 = referencepulse(spec, \"19F\")","title":"Example: 19F R1ρ on-resonance experiment"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Additional-Examples","page":"Annotations","text":"","title":"Additional Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#CEST-Experiment","page":"Annotations","text":";@ schema_version: \"0.0.2\"\n;@ sequence_version: \"1.0.0\"\n;@ title: 19F CEST\n;@ experiment_type: [cest, 1d]\n;@ typical_nuclei: [19F]\n;@ dimensions: [cest.offset, f1]\n;@ acquisition_order: [f1, cest.offset]\n;@ reference_pulse:\n;@   - {channel: f1, pulse: p1, power: pl1}\n;@ cest:\n;@   channel: f1\n;@   power: pl8\n;@   duration: d18\n;@   offset: FQ1LIST","title":"CEST Experiment"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Diffusion-Experiment","page":"Annotations","text":";@ schema_version: \"0.0.2\"\n;@ sequence_version: \"2.0.1\"\n;@ title: 1H STE diffusion\n;@ experiment_type: [diffusion, 1d]\n;@ features: [ste, watergate]\n;@ typical_nuclei: [1H]\n;@ dimensions: [diffusion.gradient_strength, f1]\n;@ acquisition_order: [f1, diffusion.gradient_strength]\n;@ reference_pulse:\n;@   - {channel: f1, pulse: p1, power: pl1}\n;@ diffusion:\n;@   type: bipolar\n;@   coherence: [f1, 1]\n;@   big_delta: d20\n;@   little_delta: p31\n;@   tau: d17\n;@   gradient_strength: {type: linear, start: cnst1, end: cnst2, scale: gpz6}\n;@   gradient_shape: gpnam6","title":"Diffusion Experiment"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Resources","page":"Annotations","text":"Pulse Programme Repository\nSchema Documentation\nControlled Vocabulary (VOCABULARY.md)\nDecision Log - Design rationale and schema evolution","title":"Resources"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Automatic-Dimension-Annotation","page":"Annotations","text":"When loading annotated data with loadnmr, NMRTools automatically applies semantic dimension types based on the dimensions array in the annotations. This eliminates manual calls to dimension setter functions.","title":"Automatic Dimension Annotation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Supported-Dimension-Types","page":"Annotations","text":"Annotation Pattern Dimension Type Units Example\n*.duration TrelaxDim seconds r1rho.duration, relaxation.duration\n*.power SpinlockDim Hz r1rho.power\n*.offset OffsetDim ppm cest.offset\n*.g G1Dim/G2Dim T/m diffusion.g","title":"Supported Dimension Types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Automatic-Conversions","page":"Annotations","text":"The annotation system automatically performs unit conversions:\n\nPower → Hz: Power values (from VALIST) are converted to RF field strength in Hz using the reference pulse calibration\nFQList → ppm: Frequency lists (from FQ1LIST etc.) are converted from Hz offsets to absolute ppm values. The channel of the block is resolved against the spectrometer channel model: if the channel's nucleus has a detected axis, its referencing is used; otherwise the channel's own base/carrier frequencies are used. This means an offset list still converts to ppm when the manipulated nucleus is never detected — for example a ¹³C CEST profile read out on a ¹H axis.\nGradient scaling: Gradient values are scaled by gmax to give actual field strengths","title":"Automatic Conversions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Example","page":"Annotations","text":"# Load annotated R1rho data\nspec = loadnmr(\"path/to/r1rho/experiment\")\n\n# Dimensions are automatically typed based on annotations\ndims(spec)\n# (F1Dim(...), SpinlockDim(...), TrelaxDim(...))\n\n# Access dimension values directly\ncollect(dims(spec, SpinlockDim))  # RF field strengths in Hz\ncollect(dims(spec, TrelaxDim))   # Spinlock durations in seconds\n\n# Metadata is also set\nspec[SpinlockDim, :units]  # \"Hz\"\nspec[TrelaxDim, :units]    # \"s\"\nspec[TrelaxDim, :delay_type]  # :r1rho","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/annotations/#Manual-Dimension-Setting","page":"Annotations","text":"If annotations are not available, dimensions can be set manually using:\n\nsetrelaxtimes - Set relaxation/delay dimension\nsetspinlockfield - Set spinlock field strength dimension\nsetoffsets - Set offset dimension\nsetgradientlist - Set gradient dimension","title":"Manual Dimension Setting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/relaxation/#1D-relaxation-analysis","page":"1D relaxation analysis","text":"Let's analyse a measurement of 1H T2 relaxation, acquired as a single pseudo-2D spectrum. First, we need to load the required packages. We will use LsqFit for the non-linear least squares fitting, Measurements to handle uncertainties, and Statistics for calculation of means and standard deviations.\n\nData have been processed in Topspin (using xf2), so can be loaded using the loadnmr function.\n\nusing NMRTools\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LsqFit\nusing Measurements\nusing Statistics\n\nspec = exampledata(\"pseudo2D_T2\")\nnothing # hide","title":"1D relaxation analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/relaxation/#Set-up-parameters","page":"1D relaxation analysis","text":"The experiment uses a vclist to encode the relaxation time. The contents of this list are automatically parsed when the spectrum is loaded, and can be accessed with the acqus command:\n\nacqus(spec, :vclist)\n\nEach loop corresponds to a delay of 4 ms, so from this we can calculate a list of relaxation times. The spectrum we have just loaded has an UnknownDimension as the non-frequency dimension. We need to replace this with a TrelaxDimension that encodes the relaxation delays, and we can do this with the setrelaxtimes function:\n\nτ = acqus(spec, :vclist) * 4e-3\nspec = setrelaxtimes(spec, τ, \"s\")\nnothing # hide\n\nNext, we specify the chemical shift ranges used for plotting, fitting, and for determination of the noise level.\n\nplotrange = 0.7 .. 1.0 # ppm\ndatarange = 0.8 .. 0.9 # ppm\nnoiseposition = -2 # ppm\nnothing # hide","title":"Set up parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/relaxation/#Plot-the-data","page":"1D relaxation analysis","text":"To take a quick look at the data, we can plot the experiment either as 3D lines using the waterfall style, or as a heatmap:\n\nfig = Figure()\nnmrplot(fig[1, 1], spec[plotrange, :]; style=:waterfall)\nnmrplot(fig[1, 2], spec[plotrange, :]; style=:heatmap)\nsave(\"tutorial-relax-plot.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plot the data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/relaxation/#Calculate-noise-and-peak-integrals","page":"1D relaxation analysis","text":"Now, we can determine the measurement noise, by taking the standard deviation of integrals across the different gradient points:\n\n# create a selector for the noise, matching the width of the data range\nnoisewidth = datarange.right - datarange.left\nnoiserange = (noiseposition-0.5noisewidth)..(noiseposition+0.5noisewidth)\n\n# integrate over the noise regions and take the standard deviation\n# (calculate the sum over the frequency dimension F1Dim, and use\n# `data` to convert from NMRData to a regular array)\nnoise = sum(spec[noiserange,:], dims=F1Dim) |> data |> std\n\n# calculate the integral of the data region similarly, using vec to convert to a list\nintegrals = sum(spec[datarange,:], dims=F1Dim) |> data |> vec\n\n# normalise noise and integrals by the maximum value\nnoise /= maximum(integrals)\nintegrals /= maximum(integrals)\nnothing # hide","title":"Calculate noise and peak integrals"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/relaxation/#Fitting","page":"1D relaxation analysis","text":"Now, we can fit the data to an exponential decay using the LsqFit package:\n\n# model parameters are (I0, R2)\nfunction model(t, p)\n    I0 = p[1]\n    R2 = p[2]\n    @. I0 * exp(-R2 * t)\nend\n\np0 = [1.0, 1.0] # rough guess of initial parameters\n\nfit = curve_fit(model, τ, integrals, p0) # run the fit\n\n# extract the fit parameters and standard errors\npfit = coef(fit)\nerr = stderror(fit)\nR2 = (pfit[2] ± err[2])\n\nSo we see that the fitted R₂ relaxation rate is 0.844 ± 0.016 s⁻¹","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/relaxation/#Plot-the-results","page":"1D relaxation analysis","text":"Finally, plot the results:\n\n# calculate the best-fit curve across 100 points so it looks nice and smooth\nx = LinRange(0, maximum(τ)*1.1, 100)\nyfit = model(x, pfit)\n\nfig = Figure()\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1],\n    xlabel=\"Relaxation time (s)\",\n    ylabel=\"Integrated signal\",\n    title=\"\",\n    xgridvisible=false,\n    ygridvisible=false)\nscatter!(ax1, τ, integrals; label=\"observed\")\nerrorbars!(ax1, τ, integrals, fill(noise, length(τ)))\nlines!(ax1, x, yfit; label=\"fit (R₂ = $R2 s⁻¹)\")\naxislegend(ax1; position=:rt)\nylims!(ax1, 0, nothing)\n\nap2 = nmrplot(fig[1, 2], spec[plotrange, 1]; title=\"\", color=:lightgray)\nnmrplot!(ap2.axis, spec[datarange, 1]; color=:red)\nhlines!(ap2.axis, [0.0]; color=:grey)\n\nsave(\"tutorial-relax-fit.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plot the results"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Installing-NMRTools","page":"Getting started","text":"Install NMRTools.jl through the Julia package manager:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"NMRTools\")\n\nThe examples in this tutorial also use the CairoMakie package for plotting, which can be obtained similarly.","title":"Installing NMRTools"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Plot-a-1D-spectrum","page":"Getting started","text":"Let's load some example data. This can be a Bruker experiment directory, a specific pdata folder, or an NMRPipe-format file.\n\nusing NMRTools, CairoMakie\nspec = exampledata(\"1D_19F\")\n\nNMRTools provides nmrplot for interactive and publication-quality figures. To plot a 1D spectrum:\n\nfig = nmrplot(spec)\nsave(\"plot-y.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nWe can zoom in using xlims, or select a chemical shift range from the data directly using the .. selector — the y-axis then rescales to fit only the displayed region:\n\nfig = nmrplot(spec[-124.5 .. -123])\nsave(\"plot-y2.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nAll plots can be saved as high quality vector graphics or PNG files using save:\n\nsave(\"myspectrum.pdf\", fig)\nsave(\"myspectrum.svg\", fig)\nsave(\"myspectrum.png\", fig)","title":"Plot a 1D spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Plot-a-2D-spectrum","page":"Getting started","text":"Two-dimensional spectra are plotted in exactly the same way.\n\nusing NMRTools, CairoMakie\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\nfig = nmrplot(spec)\nsave(\"plot-2d.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nContour levels start at five times the noise level with a geometric spacing of 1.7. Adjust the number of levels or spacing with the levels and spacing keywords. Use title=\"\" to suppress the title:\n\nfig = nmrplot(spec; levels=4, spacing=2.0, title=\"\")\nsave(\"plot-2d-scaled.svg\", fig); nothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plot a 2D spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Accessing-your-data","page":"Getting started","text":"Spectrum data and associated axis information, metadata, etc, is encapsulated in an NMRData structure.\n\nusing NMRTools # hide\nspec = exampledata(\"1D_19F\")\n\nData can be accessed with conventional array indexing, but also using the value-based selectors, Near and ..:\n\nspec[100:105]\nspec[Near(-124)]\nspec[-124 .. -123.5]\n\nThis also works for multidimensional data. For example:\n\nspec2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\nspec2d[8.1 .. 8.3, Near(124)]\n\nA plain array of data for the spectrum can be obtained from this using the data command:\n\ndata(spec)\n\nSimilarly, a plain vector containing axis values can be obtained from this using the data command, passing an additional argument to specify the dimension. This can either be a number or the axis type, e.g. F1Dim:\n\ndata(spec, 1)","title":"Accessing your data"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Next-steps","page":"Getting started","text":"Working with NMR data covers loading, indexing, referencing, metadata and saving in more depth.\nProcessing raw data shows how to turn raw fid and ser files into spectra, in one step with autoprocess or stage by stage:\n\nspec = loadfid(\"myexperiment/1\") |> autoprocess\n\nPeaks and peak lists covers peak picking and peak list files.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Window-functions","page":"Window functions","text":"Window functions (also called apodization functions) are applied to time-domain NMR data before Fourier transformation to modify the lineshape and signal-to-noise characteristics of the resulting spectrum. NMRTools automatically detects and stores the window function used during processing, whether by TopSpin or NMRPipe for loaded spectra, or by apodize for raw data processed in NMRTools (see Processing raw data). When a window is passed to apodize without tmax, for example Cos2Window(), its length is set from the acquisition time of each dimension.","title":"Window functions"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Overview","page":"Window functions","text":"Window functions are represented by subtypes of the abstract type WindowFunction. Each window function type stores:\n\nParameters specific to that window function\nThe acquisition time tmax (calculated after linear prediction but before zero filling)\n\nWindow functions are stored as axis metadata and can be accessed through the :window key:\n\nusing NMRTools\n\n# Load example spectrum\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\n\n# Get the window function for a specific dimension\nwindow = spec[F2Dim, :window]","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Available-window-functions","page":"Window functions","text":"","title":"Available window functions"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#No-apodization","page":"Window functions","text":"NullWindow(tmax)\n\nRepresents no apodization applied to the data. The multiplication factor is 1.0 for all time points.\n\nw = NullWindow(0.1)  # 0.1 second acquisition time","title":"No apodization"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Unknown-apodization","page":"Window functions","text":"UnknownWindow(tmax)\n\nUsed when the window function cannot be determined from the processing parameters. This typically occurs with older data formats or non-standard processing.","title":"Unknown apodization"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Exponential-(line-broadening)","page":"Window functions","text":"ExponentialWindow(lb, tmax)\n\nExponential apodization with line broadening lb in Hz. This is the most common apodization function in NMR processing.\n\nw(t) = exp(-pi cdot mathrmlb cdot t)\n\n# 5 Hz line broadening, 0.1 s acquisition\nw = ExponentialWindow(5.0, 0.1)\n\nEffect:\n\nPositive lb: Increases line broadening, improves S/N, reduces resolution\nNegative lb: Decreases line broadening (resolution enhancement), degrades S/N\nTypical values: 1-10 Hz for routine 1D proton spectra\n\nExample:\n\n# Get line broadening from a loaded spectrum\nlb = spec[F2Dim, :window]\nprintln(\"Line broadening: \", lb)","title":"Exponential (line broadening)"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Sine/Cosine-windows","page":"Window functions","text":"CosWindow(tmax)\n\nPure cosine (half-period) window function:\n\nw(t) = cosleft(fracpi t2t_mathrmmaxright)\n\nw = CosWindow(0.1)\n\nEffect: Commonly used for indirect dimensions in multidimensional NMR. Improves resolution with moderate S/N penalty.\n\n\n\nCos2Window(tmax)\n\nSquared cosine window function:\n\nw(t) = cos^2left(fracpi t2t_mathrmmaxright)\n\nw = Cos2Window(0.1)\n\nEffect: Gentler apodization than cosine, better S/N but slightly lower resolution.\n\n\n\nGeneralSineWindow(offset, endpoint, power, tmax)\n\nGeneral sine window function with adjustable parameters:\n\nw(t) = leftsinleft(\n    pi cdot mathrmoffset +\n    frac(mathrmendpoint - mathrmoffset)pi tt_mathrmmax\n    right)right^mathrmpower\n\nParameters:\n\noffset: Initial phase (0 to 1), determines starting value\nendpoint: Final phase (0 to 1), determines ending value\npower: Exponent applied to sine function\ntmax: Acquisition time\n\n# Typical shifted sine-bell\nw = GeneralSineWindow(0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 0.1)\n\nSpecial cases:\n\noffset=0.5, power=1.0 → CosWindow\noffset=0.5, power=2.0 → Cos2Window","title":"Sine/Cosine windows"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Gaussian-windows","page":"Window functions","text":"LorentzToGaussWindow(expHz, gaussHz, tmax)\n\nLorentz-to-Gauss transformation, combining inverse exponential with Gaussian broadening:\n\nw(t) = exp(+pi cdot mathrmexpHz cdot t) cdot expleft(-frac(pi cdot mathrmgaussHz cdot t)^22right)\n\n# 5 Hz inverse exponential, 10 Hz Gaussian\nw = LorentzToGaussWindow(5.0, 10.0, 0.1)\n\nParameters:\n\nexpHz: Inverse exponential (line narrowing) in Hz\ngaussHz: Gaussian broadening in Hz\ntmax: Acquisition time\n\nEffect: Converts Lorentzian lineshapes (natural in NMR) to Gaussian lineshapes. Improves resolution for crowded spectra but requires careful parameter optimization.\n\n\n\nGeneralGaussWindow(expHz, gaussHz, center, tmax)\n\nGeneral Gaussian window with adjustable center position:\n\nw(t) = exp(+pi cdot mathrmexpHz cdot t) cdot\n       expleft(-frac(pi cdot mathrmgaussHz cdot (t - t_mathrmcenter))^22right)\n\nParameters:\n\nexpHz: Inverse exponential in Hz\ngaussHz: Gaussian broadening in Hz\ncenter: Position of Gaussian maximum (0 to 1)\ntmax: Acquisition time\n\n# Shifted Gaussian window\nw = GeneralGaussWindow(5.0, 10.0, 0.3, 0.1)\n\nWhen center = 0: Simplifies to LorentzToGaussWindow","title":"Gaussian windows"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Working-with-window-functions","page":"Window functions","text":"","title":"Working with window functions"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Extracting-window-parameters","page":"Window functions","text":"Window function objects store their parameters as fields:\n\n# Load spectrum\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\n\n# Get window for F2 dimension\nwin = spec[F2Dim, :window]\n\n# For ExponentialWindow\nif win isa ExponentialWindow\n    println(\"Line broadening: \", win.lb, \" Hz\")\n    println(\"Acquisition time: \", win.tmax, \" s\")\nend\n\n# For sine windows\nif win isa CosWindow\n    println(\"Acquisition time: \", win.tmax, \" s\")\nend","title":"Extracting window parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Computing-the-apodization-function","page":"Window functions","text":"The apod function returns the time-domain apodization vector:\n\n# Load a spectrum\nspec = exampledata(\"2D_HN\")\n\n# Get apodization function for F2 dimension\napod_function = apod(spec, F2Dim)\n\nprintln(\"Apodization vector length: \", length(apod_function))\nprintln(\"First few values: \", apod_function[1:5])\n\nYou can also compute it directly from a dimension:\n\ndim = dims(spec, F2Dim)\napod_function = apod(dim)\n\nBy default, the apodization vector is zero-filled to match tdzf. To get only the acquired points:\n\napod_function = apod(spec, F2Dim, false)  # zerofill=false","title":"Computing the apodization function"},{"category":"section","location":"metadata/windows/#Simulating-lineshapes","page":"Window functions","text":"The lineshape function generates a simulated spectrum including the effect of the window function:\n\n# Get the F2 axis\naxis = dims(spec, F2Dim)\n\n# Simulate a peak at 8.5 ppm with R2 = 10 s-1\nchemical_shift = 8.5  # ppm\nR2 = 10.0            # s-1\nsimulated = lineshape(axis, chemical_shift, R2)\n\nprintln(\"Simulated lineshape length: \", length(simulated))\n\nThis is useful for:\n\nFitting experimental peaks\nUnderstanding window function effects\nQuality control and validation\n\nYou can also request complex-valued lineshapes:\n\n# Get complex lineshape\nsimulated_complex = lineshape(axis, chemical_shift, R2, ComplexLineshape())","title":"Simulating lineshapes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/referencing/#Chemical-Shift-Referencing","page":"Chemical shift referencing","text":"NMRTools supports chemical shift referencing, including:\n\nDirect referencing of individual dimensions\nIndirect referencing of heteronuclear dimensions using IUPAC Xi ratios\nTemperature-dependent water referencing for aqueous samples","title":"Chemical Shift Referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/referencing/#Basic-Referencing","page":"Chemical shift referencing","text":"To reference a spectrum, specify the dimension and the old and new chemical shift values:\n\nusing NMRTools\n\n# Load a spectrum\nspec = loadnmr(\"hsqc.ft2\")\n\n# Reference the 1H dimension\nspec = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0)\n\n# Or use dimension types\nspec = reference(spec, F1Dim, -0.12 => 0)\n\n# Or use dimension indices\nspec = reference(spec, 1, -0.12 => 0)","title":"Basic Referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/referencing/#Indirect-Referencing","page":"Chemical shift referencing","text":"By default, when you reference one dimension, NMRTools will automatically apply indirect referencing to all other frequency dimensions using IUPAC Xi ratios. This ensures consistent referencing across all dimensions based on a single reference point.\n\n# Reference 1H - 15N dimension will be automatically referenced too\nspec = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0)\n\n# Disable indirect referencing if needed\nspec = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0; indirect=false)\n\nThe Xi ratios used depend on whether the solvent is aqueous (DSS-based) or organic (TMS-based):\n\nNucleus DSS (aqueous) TMS (organic)\n¹H 1.0 1.0\n²H 0.153506088 0.153506088\n¹³C 0.251449530 0.25145020\n¹⁵N 0.101329118 0.10136767\n¹⁹F 0.940866982 0.94094011\n³¹P 0.404807356 0.40480742\n\nNMRTools automatically detects aqueous solvents from the spectrum metadata, or you can specify explicitly:\n\n# Force DSS (aqueous) Xi ratios\nspec = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0; aqueous=true)\n\n# Force TMS (organic) Xi ratios\nspec = reference(spec, H1, -0.12 => 0; aqueous=false)","title":"Indirect Referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/referencing/#Multi-Dimension-Referencing","page":"Chemical shift referencing","text":"Reference multiple dimensions at once:\n\n# Reference both 1H and 15N dimensions\nspec = reference(spec, [H1, N15], [-0.12 => 0, 120.0 => 118.5])\n\n# Or using dimension types\nspec = reference(spec, [F1Dim, F2Dim], [-0.12 => 0, 120.0 => 118.5])","title":"Multi-Dimension Referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/referencing/#Water-Referencing","page":"Chemical shift referencing","text":"For aqueous samples, NMRTools can automatically reference to water based on the sample temperature:\n\n# Reference to water (temperature from metadata)\nspec = reference(spec)\n\n# Specify temperature manually (in Kelvin)\nspec = reference(spec; temperature=298.15)\n\nThe water chemical shift is calculated using the empirical formula:\n\nδ(H₂O) = 7.83 − T / 96.9\n\nwhere T is the temperature in Kelvin. Bruker spectrometers typically set the water reference to 4.7 ppm when locking, so the correction is applied relative to this default.","title":"Water Referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/animation/#Animating-spectra","page":"Animating spectra","text":"Makie's record function can create animations of spectral data, saving directly to GIF or video formats. A couple of examples are provided below.","title":"Animating spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/animation/#Phosphorylation-kinetics","page":"Animating spectra","text":"First, load in some data. This pseudo-3D kinetic data — a series of 1H,15N SOFAST-HMQC spectra — shows the progressive phosphorylation of cJun by JNK1 kinase. Read more about the paper here: Waudby et al. Nat Commun (2022).\n\nusing NMRTools, CairoMakie\n\nspec = exampledata(\"pseudo3D_kinetics\")\nnothing # hide\n\nThe data have been processed in Topspin using ftnd 3 and ftnd 2, so that the final dimension of the 3D represents the phosphorylation time. Each 2D plane took 2 minutes to acquire, and there was an initial delay of 4 min between adding the kinase and recording the first free induction decay. We need to calculate a list of times from this information, that we can use to label the animation:\n\n# get number of time points\nnt = size(spec, 3)\n\n# calculate a list of measurement times\n# experiment was recorded with 2 min per spectrum, plus initial dead-time of 4 min\ntmin = LinRange(0, 2*(nt-1), nt) .+ 4\nthr = tmin / 60\n\nNow we can generate the animation, by looping over each point in the time series with Makie's record function, then saving as an animated gif.\n\nref = spec[:, :, 1]\nfig, ax, _ = nmrplot(ref; normalize=ref)\nrecord(fig, \"kinetics.gif\", 1:nt; framerate=30) do i\n    empty!(ax)\n    ax.title[] = \"Time elapsed: $(round(thr[i], digits=1)) hr\"\n    nmrplot!(ax, spec[:, :, i]; normalize=ref)\nend\nnothing # hide\n\n(Image: )","title":"Phosphorylation kinetics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/animation/#2D-titration","page":"Animating spectra","text":"First, load in some data — a series of 1H,15N HSQC spectra from a titration experiment:\n\nspectra2d = exampledata(\"2D_HN_titration\")\nnothing # hide\n\nNow loop over the spectra to produce the animation:\n\nref = spectra2d[1]\nfig, ax, _ = nmrplot(ref; normalize=ref, xlims=(6, 10.5))\nrecord(fig, \"titration.gif\", spectra2d; framerate=8) do s\n    empty!(ax)\n    ax.title[] = label(s)\n    nmrplot!(ax, s; normalize=ref)\nend\nnothing # hide\n\n(Image: )\n\nnote: Note\nThe normalize=ref parameter scales all spectra relative to the reference spectrum, defined as the first spectrum in the titration series. This ensures that contour levels are directly comparable across the different spectra. This normalization automatically compensates for differences in acquisition parameters such as number of scans and receiver gain that would otherwise affect absolute intensities.","title":"2D titration"},{"category":"section","location":"#NMRTools.jl","page":"Home","text":"NMRTools.jl is a Julia library for NMR spectroscopy data. It reads and writes 1D, 2D and higher-dimensional datasets, processes raw time-domain data, and exposes spectra as named-dimension arrays with full metadata support. Quadrature is carried by multicomplex numbers, so each dimension of a multidimensional experiment has its own imaginary unit.\n\nMulti-format support: read Bruker (processed and raw fid/ser), nmrPipe and UCSF/Sparky data; write Bruker processed data and raw FIDs\nProcessing: composable apodization, zero-filling, Fourier transform, phasing, linear prediction and solvent suppression, with automatic phasing and a one-step autoprocess\nPeaks: N-dimensional peak picking, peak lists that are Tables.jl tables, registration of a peak list onto a spectrum, and NMRAnalysis.jl and Sparky peak list files\nAssignments: import assigned chemical shifts from the BMRB and build peak lists from them\nChemical shift indexing: array-like indexing with chemical shift values using spec[8.0 .. 9.0] syntax\nPlotting: publication-ready plots via Plots.jl recipes or Makie, with contour level and colour control\nMetadata: acquisition parameters from Bruker acqus files, pulse programme annotations and sample information; window functions, power levels and frequency lists represented as typed objects\nDimensionalData.jl integration: named dimension arrays with selectors for frequency, time and gradient dimensions","title":"NMRTools.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Install NMRTools.jl through the Julia package manager:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"NMRTools\")\n\nLoad and plot a spectrum:\n\nusing NMRTools, Plots\nspec = loadnmr(\"path/to/experiment/1\")\nplot(spec)\n\nSee the Getting started guide for more examples, the tutorials for loading, processing, plotting and peak picking, and the worked examples for complete relaxation and diffusion analyses.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-packages","page":"Home","text":"NMRTools.jl is part of a suite of Julia packages for NMR data handling developed by the Waudby lab:\n\nMulticomplexNumbers.jl — Multicomplex number types with FFTW extension, providing the natural algebraic representation of multi-dimensional NMR data.\nNMRAnalysis.jl — Quantitative NMR data analysis, with a particular focus on biomolecular relaxation, diffusion, exchange, and protein dynamics measurements.\nNMRScreen.jl — tools for fragment screening by NMR, including automated analysis of large compound libraries.","title":"Related packages"}]
}
